window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"EN-1":{"unit":"EN-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01","title":"Endocrine Embryology","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter traces where each endocrine gland comes from: the thyroid from the floor of the pharynx, the parathyroids and thymus from the pharyngeal pouches, the pituitary from oral ectoderm and brain, the adrenal from mesoderm and neural crest, and the islets from foregut endoderm. Origin explains adult position, control, and disease. It tells you where ectopic tissue hides, why a midline neck cyst rises with the tongue, why one syndrome links low calcium with T cell deficiency, and why the adrenal medulla and sympathetic chain share tumors. Board questions give the defect, mass, or ectopic site and ask for the embryologic structure behind it.","prerequisites":["Germ layers and neural crest derivatives","Pharyngeal arches, pouches, and clefts in the head and neck","Foregut development and rotation of the duodenum (GI-1)","Basic actions of PTH, thyroid hormone, cortisol, catecholamines, and insulin","Hypothalamic control of the pituitary and the hypophyseal portal system"],"connections":[{"to":"Thyroid and parathyroid physiology (EN-2, EN-3)","how":"The endodermal follicular cell, the C cell, and the pouch-derived chief cell each make a different hormone because each has a different origin."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"The thymus shares the third pouch with the inferior parathyroids, so one pouch defect causes both T cell deficiency and hypocalcemia."},{"to":"Cardiovascular embryology","how":"Neural crest cells that build the conotruncal septum also populate the pharyngeal arches, which is why 22q11.2 deletion pairs outflow tract defects with pouch defects."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy and visual pathways","how":"The pituitary sits beneath the optic chiasm and hypothalamus, so a suprasellar mass from Rathke pouch remnants causes field loss and hypothalamic failure."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal embryology (GI-1)","how":"The islets develop inside the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds, so annular pancreas and pancreas divisum arise from the same process that builds the endocrine pancreas."}],"terms":[{"term":"Foramen cecum","def":"A blind pit on the dorsum of the tongue at the apex of the sulcus terminalis that marks the site where the thyroid diverticulum began."},{"term":"Thyroglossal duct","def":"The epithelial tract that connects the descending thyroid to the tongue and normally disappears after descent."},{"term":"Pharyngeal pouch","def":"An endodermal outpocketing of the lateral wall of the embryonic pharynx; four pairs give rise to the middle ear, tonsil, thymus, and parathyroids."},{"term":"Ultimobranchial body","def":"A derivative of the ventral part of the fourth pouch that fuses with the thyroid and delivers the cells that become parafollicular C cells."},{"term":"Parafollicular (C) cell","def":"A calcitonin-secreting neuroendocrine cell lying between thyroid follicles."},{"term":"Rathke pouch","def":"An upgrowth of ectoderm from the roof of the stomodeum (primitive mouth) that forms the anterior pituitary."},{"term":"Infundibulum","def":"A downgrowth from the floor of the diencephalon that forms the pituitary stalk and posterior lobe."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells that leave the dorsal neural folds and migrate widely to form sensory and autonomic neurons, melanocytes, chromaffin cells, and much of the facial and pharyngeal mesenchyme."},{"term":"Chromaffin cell","def":"A catecholamine-secreting cell of neural crest origin in the adrenal medulla and paraganglia, functionally a postganglionic sympathetic neuron without an axon."},{"term":"Fetal (provisional) cortex","def":"The large inner zone of the fetal adrenal cortex that makes DHEA sulfate for placental estrogen synthesis and regresses after birth."},{"term":"22q11.2 deletion syndrome","def":"A microdeletion on chromosome 22 that disrupts the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches and pharyngeal neural crest; includes DiGeorge and velocardiofacial syndromes."},{"term":"Islets of Langerhans","def":"Clusters of endocrine cells (alpha, beta, delta, PP, epsilon) scattered in the pancreas and derived from foregut endoderm."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C01","obj":"EN-1.1","title":"Thyroid development, descent, and ectopic thyroid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid is the first endocrine gland to appear, at about day 24, as a thickening of endoderm in the midline floor of the pharynx. The site lies between the two parts of the developing tongue (the tuberculum impar in front and the copula behind) and persists in the adult as the foramen cecum, at the apex of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis that separates the anterior two thirds from the posterior third of the tongue."},{"type":"p","text":"The thickening becomes a bilobed diverticulum that descends in the midline in front of the pharyngeal gut, passing anterior to the developing hyoid bone and laryngeal cartilages. Throughout descent it stays attached to the tongue by a narrow channel, the thyroglossal duct. The duct becomes a solid cord and then disappears, and by about week 7 the gland reaches its adult position anterior to the upper trachea as two lateral lobes joined by an isthmus. Follicles containing colloid appear by the end of the third month, when the gland begins to function. Before then the fetus relies on maternal thyroxine (T4) crossing the placenta."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thyroid descent","steps":["Endodermal thickening at the foramen cecum, about day 24","Bilobed diverticulum descends in the midline","Passes anterior to the hyoid and laryngeal cartilages","Thyroglossal duct tethers gland to tongue","Gland lies anterior to trachea by about week 7","Duct involutes; remnants form cyst or pyramidal lobe"],"caption":"Any thyroid tissue or duct epithelium left behind lies on this single midline track from tongue base to lower neck."},{"type":"p","text":"Follicular cell differentiation depends on transcription factors of the primordium, including NKX2-1 (TTF-1), FOXE1, and PAX8. Thyroid dysgenesis (agenesis, hypoplasia, or ectopy) is the main cause of permanent congenital hypothyroidism, and ectopy is its most common form."},{"type":"h","text":"Errors of descent and duct closure"},{"type":"p","text":"Because the whole path is midline, every remnant presents in or near the midline. A lingual thyroid results when the gland fails to descend and stays at the base of the tongue at or near the foramen cecum. It is the most common ectopic site, and in most patients it is the only thyroid tissue present. It often cannot meet demand, so TSH rises and drives growth of the ectopic tissue, which is why a lingual thyroid often enlarges at puberty or in pregnancy and may cause dysphagia, a muffled voice, or airway obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"A thyroglossal duct cyst forms when a segment of duct epithelium persists and fills with secretions. It is the most common congenital midline neck mass and usually sits at or just below the hyoid. Because the tract remains attached to the tongue base, the cyst rises when the tongue is protruded and moves with swallowing. A lateral neck mass along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid instead points to a branchial cleft cyst."},{"type":"table","title":"Abnormalities of thyroid descent","cols":["Abnormality","Embryologic error","Key features"],"rows":[["Lingual thyroid","Gland fails to descend","Mass at tongue base; often the only thyroid; hypothyroid tendency; TSH-driven growth"],["Ectopic thyroid along the track","Descent stops partway","Midline tissue above or below the hyoid"],["Thyroglossal duct cyst","Duct segment fails to involute","Midline mass near hyoid; rises with tongue protrusion; may become infected"],["Pyramidal lobe","Lower duct persists as thyroid tissue","Normal variant above isthmus; enlarges in diffuse goiter"]],"caption":"Every lesion lies on the midline path from the foramen cecum to the lower neck."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A midline mass at the base of the tongue is found in a 14-year-old girl with a slightly high TSH. Why is the mass enlarging, and why might it be her only thyroid tissue?","explain":"The gland failed to descend from the foramen cecum, so the lingual mass is often the only thyroid present. It cannot meet demand, TSH rises, and TSH drives growth of the ectopic tissue, especially around puberty. Loss of this tissue would leave her with no thyroid at all.","check":"The paragraph on lingual thyroid and the descent table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C02","obj":"EN-1.2","title":"Pharyngeal pouch origins of the parathyroids and C cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pharyngeal pouches are paired endodermal pockets that push outward from the lateral pharyngeal wall between the arches. Their lining epithelium forms the epithelial part of each derivative, while the surrounding arch mesenchyme, much of it neural crest, forms capsule and connective tissue. The thymus is a good example: its epithelial framework is endoderm, its capsule and septa are neural crest, and its lymphocytes arrive later from bone marrow progenitors."},{"type":"table","title":"Pharyngeal pouch derivatives","cols":["Pouch","Derivative","Notes"],"rows":[["First","Middle ear cavity and auditory (pharyngotympanic) tube","Tubotympanic recess; meets the first cleft at the tympanic membrane"],["Second","Epithelium and crypts of the palatine tonsil","Mostly obliterated by tonsil tissue"],["Third, dorsal wing","Inferior parathyroid glands","Migrate caudally with the thymus"],["Third, ventral wing","Thymus","Two halves fuse in the anterior mediastinum"],["Fourth, dorsal wing","Superior parathyroid glands","Short migration; stable position"],["Fourth, ventral part","Ultimobranchial body","Fuses with thyroid; source of parafollicular C cells"]],"caption":"The numbering runs opposite to the adult position of the parathyroids: third pouch glands end up inferior."},{"type":"p","text":"In the fifth week the third pouch splits into a dorsal wing that becomes a parathyroid and a ventral wing that becomes the thymus. Both lose their connection to the pharynx, and the thymus migrates caudally and medially into the anterior mediastinum, pulling the parathyroid with it. The parathyroid separates from the thymus and usually comes to rest on the dorsal surface of the lower thyroid. The fourth pouch parathyroid, which starts lower, makes only a short trip and settles on the dorsal surface of the upper thyroid. The third pouch glands therefore pass the fourth pouch glands and become the inferior parathyroids."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why third pouch glands end up lowest","steps":["Third pouch: dorsal wing parathyroid, ventral wing thymus","Thymus migrates caudally into the mediastinum","Attached parathyroid is pulled down with it","Parathyroid separates near the lower thyroid pole","Fourth pouch parathyroid moves only slightly","Third pouch glands become inferior; fourth become superior"],"caption":"A long, thymus-dependent migration gives the inferior glands their variable position."},{"type":"h","text":"Predicting ectopic glands"},{"type":"p","text":"The longer and more variable the migration, the more variable the final position. An inferior parathyroid that separates late from the thymus can be found within the thymus or elsewhere in the anterior superior mediastinum; one that separates early or fails to descend may lie high in the neck near the carotid bifurcation, above the superior glands. Superior glands vary much less and, when ectopic, tend to lie posteriorly, in the tracheoesophageal groove or behind the esophagus. Accessory parathyroid tissue and small thymic remnants are also found along the track. This is why a parathyroid adenoma missing from its expected place in the neck is often found in the thymus or anterior mediastinum."},{"type":"h","text":"C cells"},{"type":"p","text":"The ultimobranchial body, from the ventral part of the fourth pouch (sometimes called a rudimentary fifth pouch), fuses with the posterior aspect of the thyroid lobes and disperses its cells among the follicles, where they become parafollicular C cells that secrete calcitonin. Classic teaching, used in most board review sources, holds that these cells originate from neural crest that migrated into the ultimobranchial body. C cells and follicular cells therefore share one gland but not one lineage: medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from C cells, secretes calcitonin, and behaves as a neuroendocrine tumor, while papillary and follicular carcinomas arise from endodermal follicular cells."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C03","obj":"EN-1.3","title":"Dual origin of the pituitary and craniopharyngioma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pituitary (hypophysis) forms from two separate ectodermal sources that meet. In about the third week, Rathke pouch appears as an upgrowth of oral ectoderm from the roof of the stomodeum, just in front of the oropharyngeal membrane, and grows dorsally toward the brain. At the same time the infundibulum grows ventrally as a downgrowth of neuroectoderm from the floor of the diencephalon. By the end of the second month Rathke pouch loses its connection to the oral cavity as the sphenoid develops between them, and the pouch comes to lie against the infundibulum."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pituitary development","steps":["Rathke pouch rises from the stomodeal roof","Infundibulum descends from the diencephalic floor","The two outgrowths meet","Pouch loses its oral connection by the end of month 2","Anterior wall forms pars distalis; pars tuberalis wraps the stalk","Infundibulum forms stalk and pars nervosa"],"caption":"Glandular epithelium from the mouth wraps around an extension of the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"Cells of the anterior wall of the pouch proliferate to form the pars distalis, the bulk of the anterior lobe (adenohypophysis), and a small extension, the pars tuberalis, grows around the stalk. The posterior wall forms the pars intermedia, which is rudimentary in humans. The remaining lumen narrows to a residual cleft. The infundibulum forms the stalk and the pars nervosa (posterior lobe, neurohypophysis), which contains pituicytes (glial cells) and the axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons."},{"type":"table","title":"Anterior and posterior pituitary compared","cols":["Feature","Anterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary"],"rows":[["Origin","Rathke pouch, oral (surface) ectoderm","Infundibulum, diencephalic neuroectoderm"],["Tissue type","Glandular epithelium that synthesizes hormones","Neural tissue: axon terminals and pituicytes"],["Hormones","GH, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH","ADH and oxytocin, made in supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei"],["Link to hypothalamus","Blood: hypophyseal portal veins","Nerve: hypothalamohypophyseal tract"],["Disorder of loss","Hypopituitarism","Central diabetes insipidus"]],"caption":"Origin explains control: the anterior lobe answers to blood-borne releasing hormones, the posterior lobe releases neuronal hormones."},{"type":"h","text":"Remnants of Rathke pouch"},{"type":"p","text":"Epithelial rests can persist along the path of the pouch from the pharyngeal roof to the sella. A Rathke cleft cyst arises from the residual lumen. Craniopharyngioma is a benign but locally aggressive epithelial tumor arising from these rests, usually suprasellar. It has two age peaks, in childhood and in later adulthood. The adamantinomatous type, typical in children, is cystic and calcified, contains thick cholesterol-rich fluid, and is associated with mutations in the beta-catenin gene (CTNNB1). The papillary type, mostly in adults, is rarely calcified and often carries BRAF V600E."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow the location. Pressure on the optic chiasm from above causes bitemporal visual field loss, classically starting in the inferior temporal quadrants. Pressure on the stalk and hypothalamus causes hypopituitarism (growth failure from GH deficiency in a child), central diabetes insipidus from interrupted ADH delivery, mild hyperprolactinemia from loss of dopamine inhibition (the stalk effect), and sometimes hypothalamic obesity. Raised intracranial pressure causes headache. A calcified suprasellar mass in a child is craniopharyngioma until proved otherwise, whereas a pituitary adenoma arises within the sella and is rarely calcified."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 9-year-old with headaches and slowing growth develops polyuria with dilute urine. Imaging shows a cystic, calcified mass above the sella. Why does a tumor of anterior pituitary origin cause a posterior pituitary deficit?","explain":"Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch rests but grows above the sella, where it compresses the stalk and hypothalamus. ADH is made in hypothalamic nuclei and travels down the stalk, so compression interrupts its delivery and causes central diabetes insipidus.","check":"The pituitary comparison table and the craniopharyngioma paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C04","obj":"EN-1.4","title":"Two origins of the adrenal gland: mesodermal cortex and neural crest medulla","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal gland is two glands in one capsule. The cortex arises during the fifth week from mesoderm: cells of the coelomic epithelium (mesothelium) on the posterior abdominal wall, between the root of the dorsal mesentery and the developing gonad, proliferate into the underlying mesenchyme. Adrenal and gonadal steroid cells share this adrenogonadal primordium and the transcription factor SF-1, which is why both make steroids from cholesterol with overlapping enzymes, and why small adrenal rests can lie near the gonads or along their path of descent."},{"type":"p","text":"The first wave of cells forms a large fetal (provisional) cortex. A second wave surrounds it and forms the thin definitive cortex. The fetal zone lacks much 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity, so it produces large amounts of DHEA sulfate, which the placenta converts to estrogens, chiefly estriol. This fetoplacental partnership makes the fetal adrenal relatively huge, about 10 to 20 times the adult size relative to body weight. After birth the fetal zone regresses rapidly. The zona glomerulosa and fasciculata are present at birth, but the zona reticularis is not recognizable until about the end of the third year."},{"type":"p","text":"The medulla forms when neural crest cells from the thoracic region invade the medial side of the fetal cortex and differentiate into chromaffin cells. These cells belong to the same sympathoadrenal lineage as sympathetic ganglion neurons. Each is in effect a postganglionic sympathetic neuron without an axon: it receives preganglionic cholinergic fibers from the thoracic splanchnic nerves, depolarizes through nicotinic receptors, and releases catecholamines into blood instead of onto a target."},{"type":"flow","title":"How cortical blood shapes medullary output","steps":["Arterial blood enters the cortical sinusoids","Blood picks up cortisol at high concentration","Sinusoids drain inward into the medulla","Cortisol induces PNMT in chromaffin cells","PNMT methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine","Medulla secretes mostly epinephrine, about 80%"],"caption":"Extra-adrenal chromaffin tissue lacks this cortisol-rich blood, so its tumors secrete mainly norepinephrine."},{"type":"p","text":"The two origins explain separate disease patterns. Cortical disorders, such as adenomas, carcinomas, and the enzyme defects of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, involve steroid synthesis. Medullary tumors are neural crest tumors of the sympathoadrenal lineage and make catecholamines. Because neural crest cells also form the sympathetic chain and para-aortic chromaffin bodies, the same tumors can arise outside the adrenal anywhere from the skull base to the pelvis, where they are called paragangliomas or extra-adrenal neuroblastomas."},{"type":"table","title":"Neural crest tumors of the adrenal medulla","cols":["Feature","Pheochromocytoma","Neuroblastoma"],"rows":[["Cell of origin","Mature chromaffin cell","Immature neuroblast of the sympathoadrenal lineage"],["Usual age","Adults","Young children, most before age 5"],["Behavior","Usually benign; secretes active catecholamines","Malignant; the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood"],["Manifestations","Episodic or sustained hypertension, headache, sweating, palpitations","Abdominal mass, bone and marrow metastases; hypertension less common"],["Biochemical clue","Raised plasma or urine metanephrines","Raised urine homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA)"],["Pathology and genetics","Zellballen nests; RET (MEN2), VHL, NF1, succinate dehydrogenase genes","Small round blue cells, Homer-Wright pseudorosettes; MYCN amplification predicts poor outcome"],["Extra-adrenal form","Paraganglioma, mostly norepinephrine","Anywhere along the sympathetic chain"]],"caption":"Both tumors make catecholamines or their metabolites because both arise from the sympathoadrenal neural crest lineage."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroblastoma rarely causes the dramatic hypertension of pheochromocytoma because its immature cells metabolize much of what they make to HVA and VMA before release. It can also cause opsoclonus-myoclonus, an immune-mediated paraneoplastic syndrome, and occasionally matures spontaneously into a benign ganglioneuroma, behavior that reflects its neural crest identity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C05","obj":"EN-1.5","title":"22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome: pouch failure, hypocalcemia, and T cell deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"22q11.2 deletion syndrome is the most common microdeletion syndrome in humans, occurring in about 1 in 4,000 births and usually arising de novo. The deleted region contains TBX1, a transcription factor gene expressed in pharyngeal endoderm and mesoderm. Its loss disrupts development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches and of the neural crest-dependent pharyngeal arches and cardiac outflow tract. DiGeorge syndrome and velocardiofacial syndrome are names for the same deletion with different emphasis."},{"type":"table","title":"Features traced to their embryologic source","cols":["Structure affected","Origin","Result"],"rows":[["Parathyroid glands","Third and fourth pouches","Hypoparathyroidism: neonatal hypocalcemia, tetany, seizures"],["Thymus","Third pouch","Thymic hypoplasia or aplasia: T cell deficiency; absent thymic shadow"],["Conotruncal septum and aortic arches","Cardiac neural crest","Interrupted aortic arch type B, truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot"],["Palate and face","Pharyngeal arch neural crest mesenchyme","Cleft palate, velopharyngeal insufficiency, characteristic facies"]],"caption":"The familiar mnemonic CATCH-22 lists cardiac defects, abnormal facies, thymic hypoplasia, cleft palate, and hypocalcemia from chromosome 22."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the newborn develops tetany"},{"type":"p","text":"Before birth the placenta actively pumps calcium to the fetus, so fetal plasma calcium is higher than the mother's and fetal PTH is low. At birth that supply stops abruptly, and a healthy neonate must raise PTH within the first days to mobilize bone calcium, reduce urinary calcium loss, and stimulate renal calcitriol synthesis. An infant without parathyroid tissue cannot do this, so calcium falls in the first days of life. Phosphate rises because PTH normally promotes phosphate excretion."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pouch failure to neonatal tetany","steps":["22q11.2 deletion disrupts third and fourth pouches","Parathyroids absent or hypoplastic","Placental calcium supply ends at birth","No PTH response: bone release and calcitriol fall","Ionized calcium falls; phosphate rises","Na+ channels open more easily: tetany, seizures"],"caption":"Hypocalcemia appears in the first days of life, when the newborn first depends on its own PTH."},{"type":"p","text":"Extracellular Ca2+ normally stabilizes nerve membranes by binding to negative charges on the outer surface of voltage-gated Na+ channels. When ionized calcium falls, channels open at a more negative voltage, so peripheral nerves fire spontaneously. The result is jitteriness, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, and seizures. In the heart, low calcium lengthens the plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential, which prolongs the ST segment and therefore the QT interval on the ECG."},{"type":"h","text":"Why T cells fail but B cells do not"},{"type":"p","text":"T cell precursors come from bone marrow, but they mature only in the thymus, whose epithelium comes from the third pouch. With little thymic epithelium, few T cells mature. B cell numbers are typically normal because B cells mature in the marrow, but antibody responses that need T helper cells may be weak. Most patients have partial deficiency that improves with age; fewer than 1% have complete athymia, a severe combined immunodeficiency-like state with viral, fungal (including Candida), and Pneumocystis infections. Autoimmunity is also more common, reflecting poor thymic selection."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the neonatal labs","text":"A 3-day-old with a loud murmur has jitteriness and a seizure. Total calcium is 6.4 mg/dL (about 1.6 mmol/L), phosphate is high, magnesium is normal, PTH is low, and the chest film shows no thymic shadow. Low calcium with high phosphate means PTH action is absent, because PTH would lower phosphate. A low PTH during hypocalcemia means the glands cannot respond, so this is hypoparathyroidism, not PTH resistance (which would show high PTH) or vitamin D deficiency (which would show high PTH and low phosphate). Normal magnesium excludes hypomagnesemia, which suppresses PTH release. Combined with a murmur suggesting a conotruncal defect and the absent thymic shadow, the picture points to 22q11.2 deletion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-01-01-C06","obj":"EN-1.6","title":"Development of the pancreatic islets","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas forms from two buds of foregut endoderm at the level of the duodenum. The dorsal bud grows into the dorsal mesentery; the ventral bud arises close to the bile duct. As the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C-shaped, the ventral bud and bile duct swing behind the duodenum and come to lie below and behind the dorsal bud, and the two buds fuse."},{"type":"table","title":"Contributions of the two pancreatic buds","cols":["Bud","Adult parts","Duct contribution"],"rows":[["Ventral","Uncinate process and inferior part of the head","Proximal main pancreatic duct, joining the bile duct at the major papilla"],["Dorsal","Superior head, neck, body, and tail","Distal main duct; proximal part may persist as accessory duct to the minor papilla"]],"caption":"Failure of the two duct systems to fuse is pancreas divisum; a bifid ventral bud encircling the duodenum is annular pancreas."},{"type":"p","text":"Both endocrine and exocrine cells come from the same endodermal progenitors. PDX1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) marks all pancreatic progenitors. Cells that switch on neurogenin 3 (NGN3) become endocrine, and further factors such as NKX6.1 and MAFA direct beta cells while ARX directs alpha cells. Islets appear in the third month and scatter throughout the gland, and insulin secretion begins at about the fifth month, with glucagon and somatostatin cells developing from the same parenchyma. Islet cells were once thought to come from neural crest because they share neuroendocrine features, but lineage studies show they are endodermal. Loss of PDX1 on both alleles causes pancreatic agenesis, while one defective copy causes a form of maturity-onset diabetes of the young."},{"type":"p","text":"The two buds leave a lasting trace in islet composition. Islets in the posterior head and uncinate process, from the ventral bud, are rich in pancreatic polypeptide (PP) cells and poor in alpha cells, while islets from the dorsal bud are rich in alpha cells. Islets are somewhat more numerous in the tail. Islet tumors, such as insulinoma, arise from these endodermal endocrine cells."},{"type":"h","text":"Fetal insulin and maternal diabetes"},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal insulin acts mainly as a growth factor. Glucose crosses the placenta by facilitated diffusion, but maternal insulin does not, so fetal blood glucose follows the mother's while fetal insulin is set by the fetal beta cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"Maternal hyperglycemia and the fetal islets","steps":["Maternal hyperglycemia","Glucose crosses the placenta; insulin does not","Fetal beta cell hyperplasia and hyperinsulinemia","Insulin drives growth and fat deposition: macrosomia","Cord clamped: glucose supply stops abruptly","High insulin persists: neonatal hypoglycemia"],"caption":"The same fetal hyperinsulinemia also delays lung surfactant maturation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a macrosomic infant of a diabetic mother become hypoglycemic within hours of birth even though the mother's glucose was high?","explain":"The infant's beta cells are hyperplastic from months of high glucose exposure and keep secreting insulin after the cord is cut. The maternal glucose supply ends at once, but the insulin output does not, so glucose falls.","check":"The flow on maternal hyperglycemia and the fetal islets."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology predicts physiology and pathology in this unit. Germ layer identity sets what a cell makes: endodermal follicular and islet cells make iodinated and peptide hormones, mesodermal cortical cells make steroids, and neural crest chromaffin and C cells behave as neuroendocrine cells that secrete amines and calcitonin. Migration predicts location, so ectopic thyroid lies on a midline track, ectopic inferior parathyroids follow the thymus into the mediastinum, and paragangliomas follow the sympathetic chain. Shared origins predict linked defects, as when one pouch defect produces both hypocalcemia and T cell deficiency, or when cardiac neural crest failure adds conotruncal disease."},{"type":"p","text":"These rules have limits. Lineage assignments come largely from animal studies, and some classic teachings, such as the neural crest origin of C cells, are under revision. Ectopic positions are tendencies rather than certainties, and 22q11.2 deletion varies widely in severity even within one family, so a missing feature does not exclude the diagnosis."}],"summary":["The thyroid begins at the foramen cecum about day 24, descends in the midline anterior to the hyoid on the thyroglossal duct, and reaches the trachea by about week 7.","Failed descent gives a lingual thyroid, often the only thyroid tissue; a persistent duct gives a midline cyst that rises with tongue protrusion.","Third pouch forms the inferior parathyroids and thymus; fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroids and the ultimobranchial body.","Inferior parathyroids migrate with the thymus, so ectopic glands lie in the thymus or anterior mediastinum; superior glands vary less.","C cells reach the thyroid by way of the ultimobranchial body and are classically neural crest; they give rise to medullary carcinoma.","The anterior pituitary comes from Rathke pouch (oral ectoderm) and is controlled through portal blood; the posterior pituitary comes from the infundibulum (neuroectoderm) and releases hypothalamic hormones.","Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch rests as a calcified suprasellar mass that causes bitemporal field loss, hypopituitarism, and diabetes insipidus.","The adrenal cortex is coelomic mesoderm that makes steroids; the medulla is neural crest that makes catecholamines and gives pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.","22q11.2 deletion disrupts the third and fourth pouches and pharyngeal neural crest, causing neonatal hypocalcemic tetany, T cell deficiency, and conotruncal defects.","Islets arise from foregut endoderm of both pancreatic buds; maternal hyperglycemia causes fetal beta cell hyperplasia, macrosomia, and neonatal hypoglycemia."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, sections on pharyngeal pouches and the thyroid gland"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System, section on the hypophysis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, section on the suprarenal glands; Digestive System, section on the pancreas"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck, thyroid and parathyroid glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System; Diseases of Infancy and Childhood (neuroblastoma)"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"}]},"EN-10":{"unit":"EN-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01","title":"Immune Responses in Endocrine Disease","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the immune system attacks endocrine glands and how hormones and immunity regulate each other. It covers Graves disease and Hashimoto thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes and its slow adult form, the autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes, the cytokine and cortisol feedback loop, and why conjugate vaccines protect infants against meningococcal disease and its adrenal complication. Board questions usually give a set of thyroid, glucose, or electrolyte findings and ask which effector (antibody or T cell), which antigen, or which tolerance mechanism explains them. If you can say whether an antibody causes the injury or only marks it, most questions in this unit become straightforward.","prerequisites":["Gell and Coombs hypersensitivity types I to IV","Central and peripheral T cell tolerance, MHC class I and II presentation","Thyroid hormone synthesis and the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid feedback loop (EN-5, EN-8)","Insulin and glucagon secretion and C-peptide (EN-7, EN-8)","B cell activation, germinal centers, and class switching (IM-5, IM-13)"],"connections":[{"to":"Inflammatory endocrine diseases (EN-12-C)","how":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is compared there with subacute and painless thyroiditis, using the uptake and antibody findings explained here."},{"to":"Metabolic endocrine disease (EN-12-D)","how":"The beta cell loss described here produces the absolute insulin deficiency and ketoacidosis of type 1 diabetes."},{"to":"Endocrine pathogens (EN-11)","how":"Neisseria meningitidis causes the adrenal hemorrhage of Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome that conjugate vaccination prevents."},{"to":"Diseases of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","how":"Graves disease is the model of a receptor-stimulating type II reaction and Hashimoto thyroiditis of a T cell mediated type IV pattern."},{"to":"Immunogenetics (IM-12)","how":"HLA-DR3 and DR4, CTLA4, PTPN22, AIRE, and FOXP3 explain why endocrine autoimmunity clusters in individuals and families."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI)","def":"An IgG autoantibody that binds and activates the TSH receptor, causing Graves disease."},{"term":"Type II hypersensitivity","def":"Injury or dysfunction caused by antibody bound to an antigen fixed on a cell surface or in tissue matrix."},{"term":"Neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn)","def":"The receptor that transports maternal IgG across the placenta and protects IgG from degradation, prolonging its half-life."},{"term":"Thyroid peroxidase (TPO)","def":"The apical follicular cell enzyme that oxidizes iodide, iodinates thyroglobulin, and couples iodotyrosines; the main antigen in Hashimoto thyroiditis."},{"term":"Hurthle cell","def":"An oncocytic follicular cell with abundant granular eosinophilic cytoplasm full of mitochondria, typical of Hashimoto thyroiditis."},{"term":"Insulitis","def":"Lymphocytic infiltration of pancreatic islets, mainly CD8 T cells, that destroys beta cells in type 1 diabetes."},{"term":"C-peptide","def":"The connecting peptide cleaved from proinsulin in equal amounts with insulin, used to measure endogenous insulin secretion."},{"term":"LADA","def":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults: islet antibody positive diabetes that begins in adults and progresses slowly to insulin dependence."},{"term":"AIRE","def":"Autoimmune regulator, a thymic transcriptional regulator that makes medullary epithelial cells express tissue-restricted self proteins for negative selection."},{"term":"Annexin A1 (lipocortin 1)","def":"A glucocorticoid-induced protein that inhibits phospholipase A2 and so reduces prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis."},{"term":"T cell independent antigen","def":"An antigen, typically a repeating polysaccharide, that activates B cells without helper T cells, giving mainly IgM and weak memory."},{"term":"Linked recognition","def":"Help given to a B cell by a helper T cell that recognizes a different part (a carrier peptide) of the same antigen the B cell internalized."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C01","obj":"EN-10.1","title":"Graves disease: stimulating antibodies to the TSH receptor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Graves disease is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism. It is caused by IgG autoantibodies called thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) that bind the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells and activate it. The TSH receptor is a G protein-coupled receptor that signals mainly through Gs, adenylyl cyclase, and cAMP, so the antibody reproduces the actions of TSH: iodide uptake through the sodium-iodide symporter, synthesis of thyroglobulin and thyroid peroxidase, hormone release, and follicular cell hypertrophy and hyperplasia. The gland becomes diffusely enlarged and highly vascular, and histology shows tall columnar follicular cells with pale, scalloped colloid."},{"type":"p","text":"In the Gell and Coombs scheme this is a type II hypersensitivity, because the disease is caused by antibody bound to an antigen fixed on the cell surface. Most type II reactions destroy the target, but in receptor-directed variants the antibody changes cell function instead: it stimulates the TSH receptor in Graves disease and blocks the acetylcholine receptor in myasthenia gravis. Susceptibility is linked to HLA-DR3 and to variants of CTLA4 and PTPN22, genes that set T cell activation thresholds, and women are affected several times more often than men."},{"type":"flow","title":"Graves disease pathogenesis","steps":["Loss of tolerance to the TSH receptor","IgG TSI bind and activate the TSH receptor","Gs and cAMP drive hormone synthesis and follicular growth","Free T4 and T3 rise; pituitary TSH is suppressed","Antibody stimulation ignores feedback, so thyrotoxicosis persists"],"caption":"The stimulus comes from outside the feedback loop, which is why TSH is low while the gland keeps working."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the antibody is not regulated by feedback, hormone output stays high while pituitary TSH is suppressed. The typical pattern is low TSH, high free T4 and T3 (T3 is often disproportionately high because the stimulated gland converts more T4 to T3), and diffusely increased radioactive iodine uptake. Uptake separates Graves disease from thyroiditis, in which preformed hormone leaks from damaged follicles while new synthesis stops, so uptake is low."},{"type":"p","text":"Orbital fibroblasts and preadipocytes also express the TSH receptor. T cells infiltrating the orbit release cytokines that drive these fibroblasts to secrete hydrophilic glycosaminoglycans, mainly hyaluronan, and to differentiate into fat cells. The extraocular muscles and orbital fat swell and push the globe forward (exophthalmos). The same process in the dermis of the shin causes infiltrative dermopathy (pretibial myxedema). Because these lesions come from the autoimmune process, not from excess hormone, they are specific to Graves disease and need not track hormone levels."},{"type":"p","text":"TSI are IgG, the only immunoglobulin class transported across the placenta, carried by FcRn on the syncytiotrophoblast mainly in the third trimester. The fetal thyroid responds to TSH receptor stimulation from about 20 weeks of gestation, so high maternal TSI titers can cause fetal tachycardia and goiter and, after birth, neonatal thyrotoxicosis with irritability, tachycardia, poor weight gain, and goiter. The infant makes no TSI of its own, and maternal IgG has a half-life of about 3 weeks, so the disorder is transient and fades over about 1 to 3 months. Mothers with TSH receptor blocking antibodies can, by the same route, cause transient neonatal hypothyroidism."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: thyrotoxic newborn of a euthyroid mother","text":"A woman had Graves disease treated by thyroidectomy 4 years ago and takes thyroid hormone replacement; her TSH is normal throughout pregnancy. Her son at 5 days is irritable and tachycardic with a small goiter, low TSH, and high free T4. Step 1: the infant's thyroid is being stimulated, because both free T4 and gland size are increased while TSH is suppressed. Step 2: thyroidectomy removed the mother's target organ but not her memory B cells and plasma cells, so she can still make TSI, and her normal thyroid tests cannot reveal them. Step 3: her IgG crossed by FcRn and now acts on the infant's TSH receptors. Prediction: the disorder resolves over weeks to a few months as maternal IgG is catabolized."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C02","obj":"EN-10.2","title":"Hashimoto thyroiditis: T cell destruction, antibody markers, and lymphoma risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hashimoto thyroiditis (chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis) is the most common cause of hypothyroidism where dietary iodine is sufficient. It results from loss of tolerance to thyroid antigens, chiefly thyroid peroxidase (TPO) and thyroglobulin (Tg), the protein on which thyroid hormone is built. Susceptibility is linked to HLA-DR3 and DR5 and to CTLA4 and PTPN22 variants, and the disease is far more common in women, usually in middle age."},{"type":"p","text":"The injury is mainly cell mediated, a type IV hypersensitivity pattern. Three mechanisms combine. CD8 cytotoxic T cells kill follicular cells through perforin and granzymes and through Fas and Fas ligand. CD4 Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages that damage follicles. Anti-TPO and anti-Tg antibodies bound to follicular cells recruit NK cells for antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. The antibodies therefore add to the damage, but they are mainly a sign that the immune attack is under way."},{"type":"p","text":"The gland is diffusely enlarged, firm, and painless. Histology shows a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with well-formed germinal centers, small atrophic follicles, Hurthle cells, and variable fibrosis. As follicles are lost, T4 output falls and TSH rises, so the patient moves from subclinical hypothyroidism (high TSH, normal free T4) to overt hypothyroidism (high TSH, low free T4). Early rupture of follicles can release stored hormone and cause a brief thyrotoxic phase with low radioiodine uptake."},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-TPO antibodies are found in about 90% or more of patients and are the most sensitive marker; anti-Tg antibodies are found in fewer and are less specific. Neither proves the diagnosis alone, because anti-TPO is also present in about 10% of healthy adults (more in older women) and in most patients with Graves disease. In a patient with high TSH, anti-TPO identifies an autoimmune cause, and in subclinical hypothyroidism it predicts progression to overt disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Graves disease versus Hashimoto thyroiditis","cols":["Feature","Graves disease","Hashimoto thyroiditis"],"rows":[["Main effector","Stimulating IgG (type II)","CD8 and Th1 T cells (type IV), with antibody-mediated cytotoxicity"],["Defining antibody","TSI against the TSH receptor","Anti-TPO (most sensitive), anti-Tg"],["TSH and free T4","Low TSH, high free T4 and T3","High TSH, normal then low free T4"],["Radioiodine uptake","Diffusely increased","Normal or low; low in the early thyrotoxic phase"],["Histology","Tall hyperplastic follicles, scalloped colloid","Lymphoid follicles with germinal centers, Hurthle cells, atrophy"],["Extrathyroidal disease","Ophthalmopathy, dermopathy","Associated with other autoimmune disease"],["Long-term risk","Hyperthyroidism persists while TSI persist","Permanent hypothyroidism; primary thyroid B cell lymphoma"]],"caption":"Anti-TPO can be positive in both diseases; only Graves disease has stimulating TSH receptor antibodies."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic antigen stimulation builds organized lymphoid follicles inside the gland. Continuous B cell proliferation in their germinal centers, where activation-induced cytidine deaminase (AID) drives somatic hypermutation and class switching, raises the chance of a mutation or translocation that gives one B cell clone a survival advantage. Primary thyroid lymphoma is rare, but nearly all cases arise in a gland with Hashimoto thyroiditis, at a risk many times that of the general population. Most are extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphomas or diffuse large B cell lymphomas, and the classic presentation is an older woman with known Hashimoto disease whose gland enlarges rapidly. The sequence parallels gastric MALT lymphoma arising in chronic Helicobacter pylori gastritis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman has a high anti-TPO titer and a normal TSH. Is the antibody causing her disease, and what will happen to her TSH over time?","explain":"The antibody mainly marks an ongoing T cell attack, although it contributes some antibody-dependent killing. A normal TSH means enough follicles remain to meet demand. As T cells destroy more follicles, T4 output falls and TSH rises, first with a normal free T4 and later with a low one.","check":"The mechanism paragraph and the antibody paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C03","obj":"EN-10.3","title":"Type 1 diabetes: insulitis, islet autoantibodies, and LADA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells that ends in absolute insulin deficiency. The strongest genetic risk lies in HLA class II: about 90% or more of patients carry the HLA-DR3-DQ2 or DR4-DQ8 haplotypes, whose molecules present beta cell peptides efficiently to CD4 T cells. Other loci include a repeat sequence upstream of the insulin gene that controls how much insulin is expressed in the thymus (and so how well insulin-reactive T cells are deleted), CTLA4, and PTPN22. Concordance in identical twins is only about 30 to 50%, so environmental factors, with viral infections the leading candidates, are thought to contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"The islet lesion is insulitis, an infiltrate made mostly of CD8 T cells with CD4 T cells, B cells, and macrophages. Autoreactive CD4 Th1 cells are activated by dendritic cells presenting beta cell peptides in the pancreatic lymph nodes and help activate CD8 T cells. In the islet, CD8 cells kill beta cells that display the same peptides on MHC class I, using perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand, while interferon-gamma, TNF-alpha, and IL-1 add cytokine-induced beta cell apoptosis. The attack is antigen specific, so alpha, delta, and PP cells in the same islet survive. Glucagon secretion persists, and without local insulin to restrain it, glucagon excess adds to hyperglycemia and ketogenesis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Progression of type 1 diabetes","steps":["HLA-DR3 or DR4 risk plus loss of tolerance","Autoreactive T cells infiltrate islets (insulitis)","Islet autoantibodies appear; glucose still normal","CD8 T cells destroy beta cells, sparing alpha cells","Insulin and C-peptide fall as beta cell mass shrinks","Hyperglycemia when remaining insulin cannot meet demand"],"caption":"Autoantibodies appear years before hyperglycemia, which appears only after most beta cell function is lost."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoantibodies arise as beta cell proteins are released and presented during the attack. They do not destroy beta cells: infants of mothers with type 1 diabetes receive these IgG antibodies across the placenta without developing diabetes, in contrast to the transplacental Graves disease of C01. They are, however, the best markers of the process. Risk rises with the number of antibodies, and children with two or more have a very high lifetime risk. This is the basis of current staging: stage 1 is two or more antibodies with normal glucose, stage 2 adds dysglycemia, and stage 3 is clinical diabetes meeting diagnostic glucose criteria. C-peptide, cleaved from proinsulin in equal amounts with insulin and absent from injected insulin, measures remaining endogenous secretion and is low."},{"type":"table","title":"Islet autoantibodies","cols":["Antibody","Target antigen","Notes"],"rows":[["GAD65","Glutamic acid decarboxylase, 65 kDa isoform (makes GABA in beta cells)","Most common in adults and in LADA; tends to persist"],["Insulin autoantibody (IAA)","Insulin","Often the first to appear in young children; interpretable only before insulin therapy, because injected insulin induces antibodies"],["IA-2","Tyrosine phosphatase-like protein of insulin secretory granules","Associated with faster progression"],["ZnT8","Zinc transporter 8, which loads zinc into insulin granules","Detects some patients negative for the other three"]],"caption":"All four are markers of autoimmunity, not effectors; the number of positive antibodies predicts risk better than any single one."},{"type":"p","text":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA, also called type 1.5 diabetes) is the same autoimmune process moving more slowly. Patients are adults, often over 30 and frequently not obese, positive for at least one islet antibody (most often GAD65), and do not need insulin at first, so they are easily mistaken for type 2 diabetes. C-peptide is preserved early and falls over months to years until insulin is required; a high GAD65 titer and multiple antibodies predict faster loss. Type 2 diabetes, by contrast, has no insulitis and no islet antibodies, and its insulin deficiency is relative, from beta cell dysfunction on a background of insulin resistance."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C04","obj":"EN-10.4","title":"Autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes type 1 and type 2","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Central T cell tolerance depends on showing developing T cells the body's own proteins. In the thymic medulla, the transcriptional regulator AIRE (autoimmune regulator) makes medullary thymic epithelial cells express thousands of tissue-restricted proteins, such as insulin, thyroglobulin, and adrenal and parathyroid antigens. Thymocytes that bind these self peptides strongly are deleted. Without AIRE, T cells reactive to many organ-specific antigens leave the thymus, and several glands are attacked at once."},{"type":"p","text":"APS-1, also called APECED (autoimmune polyendocrinopathy, candidiasis, ectodermal dystrophy), is caused by loss-of-function mutations in both copies of AIRE on chromosome 21, so it is autosomal recessive, begins in childhood, and is most frequent in genetically isolated populations such as Finns. The classic triad usually appears in this order: chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis in early childhood, then hypoparathyroidism, then primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease). Two of the three establish the diagnosis. Ectodermal dystrophy (enamel hypoplasia, nail dystrophy), vitiligo, and ovarian failure are common additions."},{"type":"p","text":"The candidiasis is itself autoimmune. Patients make neutralizing autoantibodies against IL-17A, IL-17F, and IL-22, the Th17 cytokines that recruit neutrophils to mucosal surfaces and induce epithelial antimicrobial peptides. Mucosal defense against Candida depends heavily on this pathway, so infection affects skin, nails, and mucous membranes, while neutrophil killing in deeper tissue is intact and invasive candidiasis is not a feature. Neutralizing antibodies against type I interferons are present in nearly all patients and are a useful diagnostic marker."},{"type":"p","text":"APS-2 is far more common. It is defined by Addison disease together with autoimmune thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes, or both. There is no single-gene defect: it is polygenic, linked to HLA-DR3 and DR4 and to CTLA4, often affects several relatives across generations, begins in adulthood, and is about three times more common in women. Candidiasis and hypoparathyroidism are not features. A third disorder completes the comparison: IPEX, caused by FOXP3 mutation, is an X-linked failure of regulatory T cells (peripheral tolerance) with enteropathy, eczema, and diabetes in infancy."},{"type":"table","title":"APS-1, APS-2, and IPEX","cols":["Feature","APS-1","APS-2","IPEX"],"rows":[["Genetics","AIRE, autosomal recessive","Polygenic: HLA-DR3, DR4, CTLA4","FOXP3, X-linked recessive"],["Tolerance defect","Central (thymic negative selection)","No single defect; lowered threshold","Peripheral (regulatory T cells)"],["Onset","Childhood","Adulthood, women more than men","Infancy, boys"],["Core features","Mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, Addison disease","Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes","Enteropathy, eczematous dermatitis, early type 1 diabetes"],["Characteristic antibodies","Anti-IL-17 and anti-IL-22, anti-type I interferon, anti-21-hydroxylase","Anti-21-hydroxylase, anti-TPO, islet antibodies","Multiple organ-specific antibodies"]],"caption":"Candidiasis and hypoparathyroidism point to APS-1; their absence with Addison disease and thyroid disease or diabetes points to APS-2."},{"type":"p","text":"Each gland's failure follows from the hormone lost. Without PTH, renal calcium reabsorption, renal phosphate excretion, bone resorption, and calcitriol synthesis fall, giving low calcium, high phosphate, and neuromuscular irritability. Adrenal destruction, usually with antibodies to 21-hydroxylase, removes cortisol and aldosterone. Low cortisol releases ACTH from feedback, and the POMC-derived melanocyte-stimulating hormone darkens skin and mucosa; aldosterone loss causes sodium wasting, hyperkalemia, and hypotension."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a child with thrush, cramps, and darkening skin","text":"A 9-year-old has had oral thrush and nail infections since age 3. She now has muscle cramps and carpopedal spasm: calcium 6.8 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5), phosphate high, PTH low. Later she is tired and hyperpigmented, with sodium 128 mmol/L (about 135 to 145) and potassium 5.9 mmol/L (about 3.5 to 5.0). Step 1: low calcium with high phosphate and low PTH places the defect in the parathyroid gland, not in vitamin D or the kidney. Step 2: low sodium, high potassium, and pigmentation indicate loss of both cortisol and aldosterone with high ACTH, so the adrenal cortex itself has failed. Step 3: childhood candidiasis plus two endocrine failures is the APS-1 triad. Step 4: absent AIRE let self-reactive T cells escape thymic deletion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C05","obj":"EN-10.5","title":"Cytokines, the HPA axis, and the anti-inflammatory actions of cortisol","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Inflammation and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis form a negative feedback loop. IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha released by activated macrophages reach the brain by several routes: through circumventricular organs, where the blood-brain barrier is weak; by inducing cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) in brain endothelial cells to make prostaglandin E2; and through vagal afferents from inflamed tissue. Neurons of the paraventricular nucleus then release more corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) and vasopressin, which raise pituitary ACTH, and IL-6 also acts directly on the pituitary and adrenal. ACTH increases cortisol synthesis in the zona fasciculata. The same cytokines and prostaglandin E2 raise the hypothalamic temperature set point, so fever and the cortisol rise start together."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Cytokine and cortisol feedback loop","steps":["Infection or injury activates macrophages","IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha reach the hypothalamus","CRH and vasopressin raise pituitary ACTH","ACTH raises cortisol from the zona fasciculata","Cortisol blocks NF-kB and phospholipase A2","Cytokine and eicosanoid production falls"],"caption":"Cortisol shuts down the same cytokines that called for it, limiting damage from the host's own inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol diffuses into cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which sheds its heat shock proteins and moves to the nucleus. There it acts in two ways. In transactivation, receptor dimers bind glucocorticoid response elements and increase anti-inflammatory proteins, including annexin A1 and IkB-alpha, the inhibitor that holds NF-kB in the cytoplasm. In transrepression, the receptor binds the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1 directly and blocks them, shutting off genes for IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, TNF-alpha, chemokines, endothelial adhesion molecules, inducible nitric oxide synthase, and COX-2."},{"type":"p","text":"Annexin A1 inhibits phospholipase A2, the enzyme that releases arachidonic acid from membrane phospholipids. Arachidonic acid is the common substrate of cyclooxygenase and 5-lipoxygenase, so cortisol lowers both prostaglandins and leukotrienes, and reduced COX-2 transcription lowers prostaglandin synthesis further. This is broader than the effect of NSAIDs, which block only cyclooxygenase and leave leukotriene synthesis intact."},{"type":"table","title":"Effects of cortisol on circulating leukocytes","cols":["Cell","Change","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Neutrophils","Increase","Demargination from reduced endothelial adhesion, release from marrow, slower exit into tissue, delayed apoptosis"],["Lymphocytes (T more than B)","Decrease","Redistribution to marrow and lymphoid tissue, T cell apoptosis, less IL-2"],["Eosinophils","Decrease","Apoptosis and redistribution, less IL-5"],["Monocytes","Decrease","Redistribution; reduced macrophage activation and cytokine output"]],"caption":"A high white count from cortisol is neutrophilic with low lymphocytes and eosinophils; cortisol deficiency shows the reverse."},{"type":"p","text":"These effects protect the host from its own response. In cortisol deficiency (Addison disease), cytokine production is unopposed, the blood often shows eosinophilia and relative lymphocytosis, and infection or surgery can precipitate shock. In cortisol excess (Cushing syndrome), the count shows neutrophilia with lymphopenia and eosinopenia, wound healing is slow, and bacterial, fungal, and opportunistic infections are more frequent because T cell function and tissue recruitment of leukocytes are reduced."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with Cushing syndrome and no fever has a white count of 14,000/microL (reference about 4,500 to 11,000) with 88% neutrophils, few lymphocytes, and no eosinophils. Does this prove infection?","explain":"No. Cortisol demarginates neutrophils, releases them from marrow, and slows their exit into tissue, while it redistributes and kills lymphocytes and eosinophils. The pattern is expected from cortisol itself, so infection must be judged on other evidence; absent eosinophils actually fit cortisol excess.","check":"The leukocyte table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-10-01-C06","obj":"EN-10.6","title":"Conjugate meningococcal vaccines and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Haemophilus influenzae type b resist phagocytosis because their polysaccharide capsules limit complement deposition and phagocyte recognition. Protection depends on IgG against the capsule, which activates complement on the bacterial surface. For N. meningitidis the main effector is lysis by the membrane attack complex (serum bactericidal activity), which is why people lacking C5 to C9 have recurrent neisserial infection; opsonization and splenic clearance also contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"Plain polysaccharides are T cell independent antigens. Their repeating epitopes crosslink many B cell receptors at once and activate B cells, mainly splenic marginal zone and B-1 cells, without T cell help. Polysaccharides cannot be processed and loaded onto MHC class II, so helper T cells never see them. Without CD40 ligand and T cell cytokines there is little germinal center reaction, and the response is mostly low-affinity IgM from short-lived plasma cells, with little class switching, no affinity maturation, weak memory, and no booster effect. Children under about 2 years respond poorly because their marginal zone B cells are immature and express little CD21 (complement receptor 2), which normally lowers the activation threshold when the complement fragment C3d coats the polysaccharide."},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugation applies the hapten-carrier principle. The capsular polysaccharide is covalently linked to a protein carrier such as tetanus toxoid, diphtheria toxoid, or CRM197 (a nontoxic mutant of diphtheria toxin). A B cell whose receptor binds the polysaccharide internalizes the whole conjugate, digests the protein, and presents carrier peptides on MHC class II. Carrier-specific helper T cells recognize these peptides and give help to that B cell (linked recognition), so the antibody it makes is still against the polysaccharide but now has the features of a T cell dependent response. The response works in infants, and the IgG also reduces nasopharyngeal carriage, which protects unvaccinated contacts (herd protection)."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a conjugate vaccine creates memory","steps":["B cell receptor binds the capsular polysaccharide","B cell internalizes and processes the protein carrier","Carrier peptides presented on MHC class II","Helper T cell CD40 ligand and cytokines activate the B cell","Germinal center: class switching to IgG, affinity maturation","Long-lived plasma cells and memory B cells against the capsule"],"caption":"The T cell recognizes the carrier, but the antibody targets the polysaccharide because the B cell chose the antigen."},{"type":"table","title":"Plain polysaccharide versus conjugate vaccine","cols":["Feature","Plain polysaccharide","Conjugate"],"rows":[["T cell help","No (T cell independent)","Yes (T cell dependent, linked recognition)"],["Main antibody","IgM, low affinity","Class-switched IgG, high affinity"],["Germinal centers and memory","Minimal","Strong; boosts with repeat doses"],["Response under age 2","Poor","Good"],["Effect on carriage","Little","Reduced, giving herd protection"]],"caption":"The antibody target is the same capsule in both; only the help the B cell receives differs."},{"type":"p","text":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is bilateral adrenal hemorrhage during fulminant sepsis, classically meningococcemia. N. meningitidis sheds lipooligosaccharide (endotoxin) in outer membrane blebs, which drives massive TNF-alpha and IL-1 release, endothelial injury, and disseminated intravascular coagulation. The richly vascular adrenals hemorrhage, adding acute adrenal insufficiency to septic shock and removing the cortisol brake described in C05 when it is most needed. Vaccination prevents the syndrome by preventing invasive meningococcal disease. Conjugate vaccines cover serogroups A, C, W, and Y; the serogroup B capsule resembles polysialic acid on human neural cell adhesion molecule, is tolerated as self, and is poorly immunogenic, so serogroup B vaccines use surface proteins instead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why would a plain meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine given at 6 months fail to protect, while the same polysaccharide conjugated to tetanus toxoid succeeds?","explain":"At 6 months, marginal zone B cells are immature, so a T cell independent antigen gives little antibody and no memory. The conjugate lets the polysaccharide-specific B cell present tetanus peptides to helper T cells, which drive germinal center class switching to IgG and memory against the capsule.","check":"The T cell independent paragraph, the conjugation paragraph, and the vaccine table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The same tolerance machinery explains every disease in this chapter. HLA class II alleles decide which self peptides are shown to CD4 T cells, AIRE decides which tissue proteins are shown in the thymus, and FOXP3 regulatory T cells and CTLA4 restrain cells that escape, so defects at any level cluster autoimmune disease across the thyroid, islets, adrenal, and parathyroid. Pathology then depends on the effector. A stimulating IgG (Graves disease) causes hyperfunction and can cross the placenta, while T cell destruction (Hashimoto thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, autoimmune Addison disease) causes hypofunction, with antibodies serving as markers. The anatomy of the hormone loss, from PTH to cortisol, predicts the laboratory findings."},{"type":"p","text":"Endocrine and immune systems also regulate each other in health. Cytokines activate the HPA axis and cortisol limits the cytokine response, so adrenal failure from autoimmunity or meningococcal hemorrhage leaves inflammation unchecked. Microbiology and immunology meet in vaccine design: the conjugate vaccine works because polysaccharides cannot enter the MHC class II pathway on their own. Limits apply: antibody prevalence figures vary between populations and assays, a positive marker shows autoimmunity but not necessarily disease, and some patients with clinical type 1 diabetes or Hashimoto disease are antibody negative."}],"summary":["Graves disease is a receptor-stimulating type II hypersensitivity: IgG TSI activate the TSH receptor, giving low TSH, high free T4 and T3, and diffusely high radioiodine uptake.","TSI cross the placenta through FcRn and cause neonatal thyrotoxicosis that resolves over weeks to months as maternal IgG is cleared, even if the mother's thyroid was removed.","In Hashimoto thyroiditis the gland is destroyed mainly by CD8 and Th1 T cells; anti-TPO (most sensitive) and anti-Tg mark the attack and add antibody-dependent killing.","Chronic germinal center activity in Hashimoto thyroiditis underlies primary thyroid B cell lymphoma, usually MALT or diffuse large B cell type.","Type 1 diabetes is CD8 T cell insulitis that spares alpha cells; GAD65, insulin, IA-2, and ZnT8 antibodies are markers, and two or more predict disease.","LADA is antibody-positive (usually GAD65) adult diabetes that initially needs no insulin and progresses slowly as C-peptide falls.","APS-1 is autosomal recessive AIRE deficiency (failed thymic deletion): candidiasis from anti-IL-17 and anti-IL-22 antibodies, hypoparathyroidism, and Addison disease.","APS-2 is polygenic, adult-onset Addison disease with autoimmune thyroid disease or type 1 diabetes, without candidiasis or hypoparathyroidism.","IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha raise CRH, ACTH, and cortisol; cortisol blocks NF-kB, induces annexin A1 to inhibit phospholipase A2, raises neutrophils, and lowers lymphocytes and eosinophils.","Conjugating capsular polysaccharide to a protein carrier recruits T cell help, giving class-switched IgG and memory even in infants and preventing meningococcal sepsis and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Abbas, Basic Immunology","where":"Humoral immune responses to T cell dependent and T cell independent antigens"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System: thyroid, endocrine pancreas, adrenal cortex, and autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones; Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Thyroid Disease; Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex"}]},"EN-11":{"unit":"EN-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11-01","title":"Endocrine Pathogens","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microorganisms that injure endocrine glands and how they do it. Neisseria meningitidis causes bilateral adrenal hemorrhage through endotoxin-driven sepsis and disseminated intravascular coagulation (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome). Pharyngeal bacteria cause acute suppurative thyroiditis, in children usually through a pyriform sinus fistula, while a preceding viral infection sets off subacute granulomatous thyroiditis. Tuberculosis, histoplasmosis, and cytomegalovirus destroy the adrenal glands and can cause Addison disease. Board questions describe an organism, a tissue lesion, or a hormonal picture and ask you to connect them, so the emphasis is on how each organism reaches the gland and how the damage explains the signs and laboratory findings.","prerequisites":["Bacterial cell envelope: capsule, outer membrane, lipid A, and the Gram and acid-fast stains","Complement pathways and the membrane attack complex (IM-7)","Coagulation cascade, natural anticoagulants, and fibrinolysis (HE-4)","Adrenal cortex zones, their hormones, and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis (EN-5)","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives and thyroid development (EN-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine blood supply (EN-4)","how":"The adrenal arterial plexus that drains through a single central vein explains why sepsis and coagulopathy cause adrenal hemorrhage."},{"to":"Endocrine embryology (EN-1)","how":"A persistent tract from the caudal pharyngeal pouches gives pharyngeal bacteria a direct route to the left thyroid lobe."},{"to":"Inflammatory endocrine diseases (EN-12-C)","how":"Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis must be separated from painless, postpartum, and Hashimoto thyroiditis by pain, ESR, and radioiodine uptake."},{"to":"Endocrine hypofunction (EN-12-B)","how":"Infectious adrenalitis is one cause of primary adrenal insufficiency, alongside the more common autoimmune form."},{"to":"Systemic pathogens and acquired immunodeficiency (IM-14, IM-15-B)","how":"Complement deficiency and advanced HIV infection explain who is susceptible to meningococcemia and to cytomegalovirus adrenalitis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Lipooligosaccharide (LOS)","def":"The Neisseria outer membrane glycolipid: lipid A plus a short core oligosaccharide, without the long repeating O antigen of enteric LPS."},{"term":"Endotoxin","def":"Lipid A of the gram-negative outer membrane, which triggers host cytokine release through TLR4."},{"term":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","def":"Widespread activation of coagulation that forms microthrombi and consumes platelets and clotting factors, causing both thrombosis and bleeding."},{"term":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","def":"Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage with acute adrenal insufficiency in overwhelming sepsis, classically meningococcemia."},{"term":"Purpura fulminans","def":"Rapidly spreading skin hemorrhage and necrosis from dermal microvascular thrombosis in severe sepsis with DIC."},{"term":"Pyriform sinus fistula","def":"A tract persisting from the caudal pharyngeal pouches that runs from the pyriform sinus toward the thyroid, almost always on the left."},{"term":"Acute suppurative thyroiditis","def":"Bacterial infection of the thyroid with neutrophilic inflammation and abscess formation."},{"term":"Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","def":"Painful, self-limited thyroid inflammation after a viral illness, with granulomas and giant cells around damaged follicles; also called de Quervain thyroiditis."},{"term":"Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease)","def":"Deficiency of cortisol and usually aldosterone caused by destruction of the adrenal cortex, with a compensatory rise in ACTH."},{"term":"Caseous necrosis","def":"Cheese-like granular necrosis at the center of a granuloma, characteristic of tuberculosis."},{"term":"Dimorphic fungus","def":"A fungus that grows as a mold in the environment and as a yeast at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F)."},{"term":"Owl's eye inclusion","def":"A large intranuclear viral inclusion surrounded by a clear halo in an enlarged cytomegalovirus-infected cell."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11-01-C01","obj":"EN-11.1","title":"Neisseria meningitidis and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria meningitidis is a gram-negative diplococcus whose paired cells have flattened, kidney-bean shaped facing sides. It is oxidase positive, grows on chocolate agar, and ferments both glucose and maltose, which separates it from Neisseria gonorrhoeae (glucose only). It spreads by respiratory droplets and close contact, colonizes the nasopharynx of a minority of healthy people, and causes outbreaks where young adults live closely together, such as dormitories and military barracks."},{"type":"table","title":"Meningococcal virulence factors","cols":["Factor","What it does"],"rows":[["Type IV pili","Attach to nonciliated nasopharyngeal epithelial cells"],["IgA1 protease","Cleaves secretory IgA1 at the mucosal surface"],["Polysaccharide capsule","Blocks phagocytosis and complement deposition in blood; defines serogroups (A, B, C, W, X, Y)"],["Lipooligosaccharide","Lipid A endotoxin; shed in large amounts in outer membrane blebs"]],"caption":"Each factor matches one step of invasion or injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Once the organism crosses the mucosa, survival in blood depends on the capsule, and clearance depends on antibody plus complement assembling the membrane attack complex (C5b to C9) on the outer membrane. Deficiency of terminal complement components or properdin therefore predisposes to recurrent neisserial bacteremia, and asplenia removes a major site of clearance of encapsulated bacteria."},{"type":"h","text":"From endotoxin to adrenal hemorrhage"},{"type":"p","text":"Continuous shedding of blebs makes blood endotoxin levels in meningococcemia among the highest of any infection. Lipid A binds TLR4 with its coreceptors MD-2 and CD14 on monocytes and macrophages and activates NF-kB, driving release of TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6. These cytokines cause vasodilation and capillary leak, and they induce tissue factor on monocytes and endothelium while suppressing thrombomodulin and the protein C pathway. Coagulation is switched on throughout the microcirculation: microthrombi form, platelets and clotting factors are consumed, and bleeding follows. This is disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Thrombosis in dermal vessels produces the petechial rash that can progress to purpura fulminans."},{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal gland is especially vulnerable because of its vascular design. Superior, middle, and inferior suprarenal arteries form a rich subcapsular plexus that feeds cortical sinusoids, and all of this blood leaves through a single central vein. Thrombosis of the venous outflow in DIC, together with damaged capillaries, causes hemorrhagic infarction of both glands. The sudden loss of cortisol removes its permissive support of vascular tone and catecholamine responsiveness, so hypotension stops responding to fluids. Because the loss is abrupt, ACTH has no time to cause hyperpigmentation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Meningococcemia to Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","steps":["Droplet spread; pili attach in the nasopharynx","Organism invades blood; capsule resists phagocytosis","Bacteria shed LOS-rich outer membrane blebs","Lipid A binds TLR4; TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6 released","Tissue factor up, protein C pathway down: DIC","Adrenal venous thrombosis and hemorrhagic infarction, both glands","Acute cortisol loss deepens septic shock"],"caption":"The causal chain from colonization to adrenal failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is classically meningococcal but also occurs in sepsis from Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Staphylococcus aureus. Acute adrenal insufficiency worsens the shock, although how much it drives the shock rather than marks its severity is debated."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the laboratory picture","text":"A 19-year-old in a dormitory develops fever of 39.5 °C (103.1 °F), spreading purpura, and hypotension despite fluids. Platelets are low, PT and aPTT are prolonged, fibrinogen is low, and D-dimer is high: consumption of platelets and factors with active fibrinolysis means DIC. Low sodium with high potassium suggests adrenal loss, although sepsis-related acidosis and kidney injury can also raise potassium. Blood culture grows gram-negative diplococci. Both problems trace back to lipid A: tissue factor drives DIC, and DIC infarcts glands that drain through a single vein."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the infection risk in a patient who lacks C7.","explain":"Blood-borne Neisseria are killed mainly by the membrane attack complex, so lacking C7 causes recurrent neisserial bacteremia, while C3b opsonization still protects against most other bacteria.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on clearance in blood."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11-01-C02","obj":"EN-11.2","title":"Acute suppurative thyroiditis and the pyriform sinus fistula","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial infection of the thyroid is rare because the gland resists it well. It is enclosed in a fibrous capsule, has a very rich blood supply and lymphatic drainage, and its follicles contain high concentrations of iodide along with the hydrogen peroxide used for hormone synthesis. Infection therefore usually needs a special route or a weakened gland: a pyriform sinus fistula in children, or preexisting nodular goiter, thyroid cancer, needle aspiration, or immunosuppression in adults."},{"type":"p","text":"The usual organisms are those of the skin and upper airway. They are told apart by Gram stain and simple tests, summarized below. When a fistula connects the gland to the pharynx, mixed oropharyngeal flora including anaerobes is also common."},{"type":"table","title":"Bacteria of acute suppurative thyroiditis","cols":["Organism","Gram stain and growth","Identifying tests"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-positive cocci in clusters","Catalase positive, coagulase positive"],["Streptococcus pyogenes (group A)","Gram-positive cocci in chains; beta-hemolytic","Catalase negative, bacitracin sensitive"],["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci; alpha-hemolytic","Catalase negative, optochin sensitive, bile soluble"]],"caption":"The three classic causes. All produce neutrophilic inflammation and abscess."},{"type":"h","text":"The pyriform sinus fistula"},{"type":"p","text":"The pyriform sinus is the recess of the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet. A pyriform sinus fistula is a remnant of the caudal pharyngeal pouches (the third or, more often, the fourth) that persists as a tract from the apex of the sinus down to the region of the upper thyroid lobe. It occurs on the left in the large majority of cases. Pharyngeal bacteria travel down the tract, often after an upper respiratory infection inflames the pharynx, and seed the left lobe."},{"type":"flow","title":"Recurrent left-sided thyroid abscess in a child","steps":["Caudal pharyngeal pouch remnant persists as a tract","Tract runs from left pyriform sinus to thyroid","Upper respiratory infection inflames the pharynx","Oropharyngeal bacteria track down the tract","Neutrophilic abscess forms in the left lobe","Infection recurs while the tract remains"],"caption":"Why the same side becomes infected again and again."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism explains the presentation: fever, a tender, warm, sometimes fluctuant anterior neck swelling and pain on swallowing. Because bacteria destroy a limited region rather than the whole gland, thyroid function is usually normal, although destruction of follicles occasionally releases enough stored hormone to cause transient thyrotoxicosis. The abscess takes up little radioiodine, so it appears as a cold area on a scan. A second left-sided thyroid infection in a child is the classic clue to a fistula, which is usually demonstrated by contrast swallow once the inflammation settles."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why would a right-sided thyroid abscess in a child without prior neck surgery make a fistula less likely?","explain":"Pyriform sinus fistulas arise almost always on the left, so a right-sided abscess suggests another route such as bloodstream seeding, local trauma, or an abnormal gland.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the pyriform sinus fistula."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11-01-C03","obj":"EN-11.3","title":"Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis after viral infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis (de Quervain thyroiditis) is a painful, self-limited inflammation of the thyroid that typically begins about 2 to 8 weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. It is most common in women aged about 30 to 50 years. Coxsackievirus and mumps virus are the classic associations; adenovirus, influenza virus, echovirus, measles virus, and others have also been implicated. Virus is rarely recovered from the gland itself, which suggests that the injury is driven by the host response rather than by ongoing viral replication."},{"type":"table","title":"Viruses classically linked to subacute thyroiditis","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Envelope","Other diseases it causes"],"rows":[["Coxsackievirus","Picornavirus (enterovirus); positive-sense single-stranded RNA","Nonenveloped","Group A: herpangina, hand-foot-and-mouth disease; group B: myocarditis, pericarditis, pleurodynia"],["Mumps virus","Paramyxovirus; negative-sense single-stranded RNA, nonsegmented","Enveloped","Parotitis, orchitis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis"]],"caption":"Coxsackievirus spreads by fecal-oral and respiratory routes; mumps spreads by respiratory droplets."},{"type":"p","text":"The leading model is that viral antigen, or thyroid antigen released from virus-damaged cells, stimulates cytotoxic T cells in genetically susceptible people. The strongest genetic link is HLA-B35, an MHC class I allele, which fits a CD8 T cell response against follicular cells. The T cells destroy follicular epithelium, and colloid leaks into the interstitium."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology follows the time course. Early lesions show disrupted follicles and neutrophils, sometimes forming microabscesses. Later, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages gather, and multinucleated giant cells surround and engulf fragments of extravasated colloid, forming granulomas. These granulomas are noncaseating and centered on follicles, and the gland eventually heals with patchy fibrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thyroid function in subacute thyroiditis","steps":["Follicles destroyed; stored T4 and T3 leak into blood","Thyrotoxic phase: TSH suppressed, radioiodine uptake very low","Stores exhausted: brief euthyroid phase","Hypothyroid phase while follicles regenerate","Recovery to normal function in most patients"],"caption":"Hormone release is passive leakage, not new synthesis, which explains low uptake during thyrotoxicosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from this mechanism. Inflammation stretches the thyroid capsule, producing neck pain that radiates to the jaw or ear and a firm, very tender gland, with fever, malaise, and a markedly raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein. Leakage of preformed hormone causes thyrotoxicosis with a relatively high T4 to T3 ratio, reflecting the composition of stored hormone, and a high serum thyroglobulin. Because TSH is suppressed and damaged follicles cannot trap iodide, 24-hour radioiodine uptake is very low, the key contrast with Graves disease, in which TSH receptor antibodies drive high uptake. A minority of patients remain permanently hypothyroid."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are tested often. Painless (silent) and postpartum thyroiditis also cause low-uptake thyrotoxicosis, but they are autoimmune lymphocytic processes with no pain, a normal ESR, and often anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies. Acute suppurative thyroiditis is painful too, but it is a localized bacterial abscess with usually normal thyroid function."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two painful thyroids","text":"Patient A has fever and a fluctuant, tender left thyroid swelling; TSH and free T4 are normal and the swelling is cold on scan. Patient B had a cold 4 weeks ago and now has a diffusely tender gland, low TSH, high free T4, ESR of 80 mm/h, and 24-hour radioiodine uptake under 5 percent. In A, a localized bacterial abscess spares most follicles, so function is normal. In B, T cell destruction across the gland releases stored hormone, suppressing TSH, and the injured follicles cannot trap iodide, so uptake is low. A is acute suppurative thyroiditis; B is subacute granulomatous thyroiditis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-11-01-C04","obj":"EN-11.4","title":"Infectious adrenalitis and Addison disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal cortex has a large functional reserve, so cortisol and aldosterone fall only after about 90 percent of the cortex in both glands is destroyed. A disease must therefore reach both glands, and infections do this by hematogenous spread. Autoimmune adrenalitis is the most common cause of Addison disease in developed countries, but tuberculosis is the leading cause worldwide and was the cause in Addison's original cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a slow-growing, obligately aerobic rod whose cell wall is rich in mycolic acids, so it resists decolorization with acid alcohol and stains as acid-fast (Ziehl-Neelsen stain). It seeds the adrenals during hematogenous spread from the lung. Inside macrophages it blocks phagosome-lysosome fusion. Th1 cells release interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages and organizes granulomas maintained by TNF-alpha; the centers undergo caseous necrosis. Caseating granulomas replace cortex and medulla alike, so early disease enlarges the glands, and healed disease leaves small, often calcified glands."},{"type":"p","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum is a dimorphic fungus that grows as a mold in soil contaminated by bird and bat droppings, especially in the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys, and as a small yeast (about 2 to 4 µm) at body temperature. Inhaled spores convert to yeasts that multiply inside macrophages, which carry them through the blood. In disseminated histoplasmosis the adrenals are a frequent target, and granulomatous destruction often enlarges both glands. Other endemic fungi, notably Paracoccidioides brasiliensis in South America, can act the same way."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is an enveloped, double-stranded DNA herpesvirus that establishes latency and reactivates when cellular immunity fails, usually at CD4 counts below 50 cells per microliter in AIDS. It is the most common adrenal infection found at autopsy in AIDS. Infected cells enlarge and carry owl's eye intranuclear inclusions, and the necrotizing adrenalitis often begins in the medulla and spreads into the adjacent cortex. Because it seldom destroys 90 percent of the cortex, overt Addison disease from CMV alone is uncommon."},{"type":"table","title":"Infectious causes of adrenalitis","cols":["Organism","Type and identification","Adrenal lesion","Typical host"],"rows":[["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Acid-fast rod; mycolic acid wall","Caseating granulomas; enlarged then calcified glands","Endemic areas; reactivation or miliary disease"],["Histoplasma capsulatum","Dimorphic fungus; small yeasts in macrophages","Granulomas; bilateral enlargement","River valley exposure; disseminated disease"],["Cytomegalovirus","Enveloped dsDNA herpesvirus; owl's eye inclusions","Necrotizing adrenalitis, medulla and inner cortex","AIDS with CD4 below about 50 per microliter"]],"caption":"All three spread through the blood and involve both glands and the medulla, unlike autoimmune adrenalitis, which leaves small glands and spares the medulla."},{"type":"flow","title":"Tuberculous Addison disease","steps":["Blood-borne M. tuberculosis seeds both adrenals","Th1 and macrophages form caseating granulomas","Granulomas replace cortex and medulla","About 90 percent cortex lost: cortisol and aldosterone fall","Low cortisol raises CRH and POMC-derived ACTH","Excess ACTH stimulates melanocortin 1 receptors: hyperpigmentation"],"caption":"Granulomatous destruction produces the full hormonal picture of primary adrenal insufficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from the lost hormones. Cortisol deficiency causes fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, and fasting hypoglycemia. Aldosterone deficiency causes sodium wasting, volume depletion, salt craving, hyperkalemia, and a mild non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, with high renin. Chronic ACTH excess darkens skin creases, scars, and buccal mucosa. Laboratory confirmation rests on low morning cortisol with high ACTH and a poor cortisol response to synthetic ACTH."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a patient with adrenal insufficiency from pituitary failure show hyperpigmentation or hyperkalemia?","explain":"No. ACTH is low, so there is no melanocortin drive to pigment, and aldosterone is preserved because the renin-angiotensin system, not ACTH, is its main regulator, so potassium stays normal.","check":"Compare with the tuberculous Addison disease flow and the final paragraph of this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each infection in this chapter is explained by joining anatomy, microbiology, immunology, and pathology. Adrenal vascular anatomy, with a rich arterial plexus draining through one vein, turns lipid A-driven DIC into bilateral hemorrhagic infarction. Pharyngeal pouch embryology turns ordinary throat flora into a recurrent left-sided thyroid abscess. HLA class I genetics and cytotoxic T cells turn a common viral illness into granulomatous destruction of follicles, and Th1 granuloma formation, or loss of T cell immunity in AIDS, determines which organisms destroy the adrenal cortex."},{"type":"p","text":"The endocrine consequence then depends on how much of the gland is lost and how fast. Sudden bilateral adrenal hemorrhage causes crisis without pigment change; slow granulomatous destruction causes Addison disease with hyperpigmentation; focal thyroid abscess spares function, while diffuse follicle damage releases stored hormone. Limits matter: most Addison disease in developed countries is autoimmune, CMV adrenalitis is frequent but rarely sufficient alone, and a viral cause of subacute thyroiditis is usually inferred from history rather than proven in the gland."}],"summary":["Neisseria meningitidis is a gram-negative, oxidase-positive diplococcus that ferments glucose and maltose and spreads by respiratory droplets.","Pili attach, IgA1 protease protects at the mucosa, the capsule protects in blood, and LOS lipid A causes the injury.","Terminal complement (C5 to C9) or properdin deficiency and asplenia predispose to meningococcal bacteremia.","Lipid A binds TLR4, cytokines induce tissue factor and suppress protein C, and DIC follows.","Adrenal arteries feed a plexus drained by one central vein, so DIC causes bilateral hemorrhagic infarction and shock unresponsive to fluids.","Acute suppurative thyroiditis is usually Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes, or Streptococcus pneumoniae, with normal thyroid function.","Recurrent left-sided thyroid abscess in a child points to a pyriform sinus fistula from the caudal pharyngeal pouches.","Subacute thyroiditis follows a viral infection such as coxsackievirus or mumps, is linked to HLA-B35, and shows a painful gland, high ESR, and very low radioiodine uptake.","Infectious Addison disease needs about 90 percent loss of both cortices: tuberculosis (caseating granulomas, calcification), histoplasmosis (bilateral enlargement), and CMV in AIDS (owl's eye inclusions, rarely sufficient alone)."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on the Neisseriae, staphylococci and streptococci, mycobacteria, herpesviruses, picornaviruses, paramyxoviruses, and medical mycology"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System: thyroiditis and adrenocortical insufficiency"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolism, and Shock"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity and the complement system"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck: pharyngeal pouches"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"}]},"EN-12-A":{"unit":"EN-12-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01","title":"Diseases of Endocrine Hyperfunction","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers diseases in which a gland makes too much hormone: Cushing syndrome, primary and secondary hyperaldosteronism, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, primary, secondary, and tertiary hyperparathyroidism, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, gigantism and acromegaly, Graves disease and toxic multinodular goiter, SIADH, and precocious puberty. Every one of them is solved the same way. First ask whether the hormone is escaping its normal feedback, then read the paired trophic or regulatory signal (ACTH, renin, PTH with calcium, TSH, LH and FSH) to find the source, and finally explain each sign from what the hormone normally does. Board questions give a lab panel and ask for the lesion, or give a lesion and ask for the panel.","prerequisites":["Hypothalamic-pituitary axes and negative feedback (EN-5, EN-6)","Adrenal steroid synthesis pathway and its enzymes (EN-8)","PTH, calcitriol, and FGF23 regulation of calcium and phosphate","Collecting duct handling of Na+, K+, H+, and water (aldosterone and ADH)","Thyroid hormone synthesis and nuclear receptor action"],"connections":[{"to":"Hormone synthesis and degradation (EN-8)","how":"The position of each blocked enzyme in the steroid pathway predicts every finding in congenital adrenal hyperplasia."},{"to":"Immune responses in endocrine disease (EN-10)","how":"Graves disease is a stimulating antibody disease (type II hypersensitivity) aimed at the TSH receptor."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hypofunction (EN-12-B)","how":"Each hyperfunction state has a mirror image (Addison disease, hypoparathyroidism, diabetes insipidus) read with the same paired tests."},{"to":"Endocrine neoplasms (EN-12-G)","how":"Adenomas of the pituitary, adrenal, and parathyroid, and paraneoplastic ACTH or ADH from small cell carcinoma, cause most of these syndromes."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"Aldosterone, ADH, PTH, and the calcium-sensing receptor all act on the nephron, so urine sodium, potassium, calcium, and osmolality are diagnostic."}],"terms":[{"term":"ACTH-dependent Cushing syndrome","def":"Cortisol excess driven by ACTH from a pituitary corticotroph adenoma or an ectopic tumor, causing bilateral adrenal hyperplasia."},{"term":"Dexamethasone suppression","def":"A test that gives a potent synthetic glucocorticoid and measures whether cortisol falls, showing whether ACTH secretion still obeys feedback."},{"term":"Aldosterone to renin ratio","def":"Screening ratio that is high when aldosterone is made independently of renin, as in primary hyperaldosteronism."},{"term":"Aldosterone escape","def":"Return of sodium excretion toward normal despite continued mineralocorticoid excess, through pressure natriuresis and natriuretic peptides, which prevents edema."},{"term":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","def":"Autosomal recessive deficiency of a cortisol-synthesis enzyme that raises ACTH, enlarges both adrenals, and diverts precursors into other pathways."},{"term":"Osteitis fibrosa cystica","def":"PTH-driven bone disease with osteoclastic resorption, marrow fibrosis, cysts, and brown tumors."},{"term":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","def":"Autonomous PTH secretion with hypercalcemia that develops after prolonged secondary stimulation, typically in chronic kidney disease."},{"term":"IGF-1","def":"Insulin-like growth factor 1, made mainly in the liver under GH control, which mediates the growth effects of GH and serves as a stable marker of GH secretion."},{"term":"Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin","def":"IgG autoantibody that binds and activates the TSH receptor in Graves disease."},{"term":"Thyroid storm","def":"Life-threatening decompensated thyrotoxicosis with fever, tachyarrhythmia, heart failure, and altered mental status."},{"term":"SIADH","def":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion: ADH release not suppressed by low plasma osmolality, causing water retention and euvolemic hyponatremia."},{"term":"Calcium-sensing receptor","def":"G protein-coupled receptor on parathyroid chief cells and renal tubule cells that suppresses PTH secretion and renal calcium reabsorption when calcium rises."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C01","obj":"EN-12-A.1","title":"Finding the source of Cushing syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cushing syndrome is the clinical state of chronic glucocorticoid excess from any cause. The most common cause overall is exogenous glucocorticoid therapy. Among endogenous causes, an ACTH-secreting pituitary corticotroph adenoma, usually a microadenoma under 10 mm, accounts for about 70% and is called Cushing disease. Ectopic ACTH, most often from small cell lung carcinoma or a bronchial carcinoid, and primary adrenal adenoma or carcinoma make up most of the rest."},{"type":"p","text":"Once cortisol excess is proven (C02), the question is whether ACTH is driving it. Plasma ACTH answers this. When the adrenal is the source, or the patient takes glucocorticoids, cortisol suppresses the hypothalamus and corticotrophs, so ACTH is low. When a pituitary adenoma or ectopic tumor is the source, ACTH is normal or high, and its trophic effect produces bilateral adrenal cortical hyperplasia. The adrenal morphology follows directly: a cortisol-secreting adenoma leaves the opposite gland and the adjacent cortex atrophic, and exogenous steroids leave both glands atrophic."},{"type":"p","text":"High-dose dexamethasone then separates pituitary from ectopic ACTH. A corticotroph adenoma is not fully autonomous; its feedback set point is raised, so a large dose of glucocorticoid (8 mg) still lowers ACTH and cortisol, classically by more than 50%. Ectopic tumors and adrenal tumors do not respond. Some well-differentiated carcinoids do suppress, so imaging and, where needed, inferior petrosal sinus sampling (a central-to-peripheral ACTH gradient means a pituitary source) settle doubtful cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Very high ACTH levels also explain two clues to an ectopic source. ACTH and melanocyte-stimulating hormone both come from proopiomelanocortin and both activate the melanocortin 1 receptor on melanocytes, causing hyperpigmentation. Very high cortisol saturates renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, so cortisol activates mineralocorticoid receptors and causes marked hypokalemia. Ectopic disease often develops too fast for the classic body habitus to form. Adrenal carcinoma, unlike adenoma, often secretes androgens as well and causes virilization."},{"type":"flow","title":"Localizing cortisol excess","steps":["Confirm cortisol excess with screening tests","Measure plasma ACTH","Low ACTH: adrenal tumor or exogenous steroid","Normal or high ACTH: pituitary or ectopic source","High-dose dexamethasone suppresses cortisol: pituitary adenoma","No suppression with high ACTH: ectopic ACTH"],"caption":"Exogenous steroid use is excluded by history; imaging follows the biochemistry, never the reverse."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns in Cushing syndrome","cols":["Cause","Plasma ACTH","High-dose dexamethasone","Adrenal glands","Clues"],"rows":[["Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Normal or high","Cortisol suppresses","Bilateral hyperplasia","Most common endogenous cause; women 20 to 50"],["Ectopic ACTH (small cell carcinoma, carcinoid)","Very high","No suppression (some carcinoids suppress)","Bilateral hyperplasia","Hyperpigmentation, severe hypokalemia, rapid course"],["Adrenal adenoma","Low","No suppression","Tumor; contralateral atrophy","Pure cortisol excess"],["Adrenal carcinoma","Low","No suppression","Large tumor; contralateral atrophy","Often virilizing from androgen co-secretion"],["Exogenous glucocorticoids","Low","Not informative; endogenous cortisol already suppressed","Bilateral atrophy","Drug history; most common cause overall"]],"caption":"ACTH divides dependent from independent causes; high-dose dexamethasone divides pituitary from ectopic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A smoker loses weight over three months, develops severe hypokalemia and hyperpigmentation, but has no striae or central obesity. Plasma cortisol is very high. Is this Cushing syndrome, and where is the ACTH coming from?","explain":"Yes. The very high ACTH from an ectopic source, likely small cell lung carcinoma, causes pigmentation through the melanocortin 1 receptor, and the huge cortisol load overwhelms 11-beta-HSD2 and acts as a mineralocorticoid. The course is too rapid and catabolic for fat redistribution and striae to develop. High-dose dexamethasone would not suppress cortisol.","check":"The ectopic ACTH paragraph and the patterns table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C02","obj":"EN-12-A.2","title":"Manifestations of cortisol excess and screening tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol is a catabolic, gluconeogenic hormone that acts through the intracellular glucocorticoid receptor, a ligand-activated transcription factor. Its normal job in stress is to supply glucose: it breaks down muscle protein to provide amino acids, induces hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes such as phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase, and reduces insulin-stimulated glucose uptake in muscle and fat. Sustained excess turns these adaptive actions into disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat redistribution is the most visible change. Cortisol promotes lipolysis in the limbs, while the combination of cortisol and the hyperinsulinemia caused by insulin resistance favors fat storage in visceral, facial, dorsocervical, and supraclavicular depots, which have more glucocorticoid receptors and 11-beta-HSD1 activity. The result is central obesity with thin limbs, moon facies, and a dorsocervical fat pad."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of protein and inhibition of fibroblasts explain the skin, muscle, and bone. The dermis thins because collagen synthesis falls, so expanding fat stretches it into wide (often over 1 cm) purple striae, colored by the venous blood visible through thin skin, and minor trauma causes bruising. Proximal muscles waste and weaken, with selective atrophy of type II (fast) fibers. Bone loss follows from suppressed osteoblast function, reduced intestinal calcium absorption, and increased urinary calcium loss. Wound healing is slow because fibroblast activity and inflammation are suppressed, and infections are more frequent."},{"type":"table","title":"Linking each feature to a cortisol action","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Central obesity, moon facies, dorsocervical fat pad","Hyperinsulinemia plus cortisol favor central fat deposition; limbs lose fat and muscle"],["Wide purple striae, thin skin, bruising","Reduced collagen synthesis thins the dermis; vessels show through"],["Proximal muscle weakness","Protein catabolism with selective type II fiber atrophy"],["Hyperglycemia","Gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance, permissive effect on glucagon and epinephrine"],["Hypertension","Cortisol at mineralocorticoid receptors when 11-beta-HSD2 is overwhelmed; more angiotensinogen; greater vascular response to catecholamines"],["Osteoporosis, fractures","Less osteoblast activity, less gut calcium absorption, more urinary calcium loss"],["Poor wound healing, infections","Suppressed fibroblasts, cytokines, and lymphocytes"],["Hirsutism, menstrual change (ACTH-dependent forms, adrenal carcinoma)","ACTH drives adrenal androgen output; carcinomas secrete androgens"]],"caption":"Most features trace to protein catabolism, insulin antagonism, or spillover to the mineralocorticoid receptor."},{"type":"h","text":"Screening: proving loss of normal regulation"},{"type":"p","text":"A single random cortisol is useless because secretion is pulsatile and has a strong circadian rhythm, peaking in the early morning and falling to a nadir near midnight. Screening tests therefore look for the features of autonomy. The 24-hour urine free cortisol measures unbound cortisol filtered over a whole day, an integrated measure of excess. Late-night salivary cortisol detects loss of the midnight trough, an early abnormality. The overnight low-dose (1 mg) dexamethasone suppression test checks feedback: in a normal person morning cortisol falls below about 1.8 micrograms/dL (50 nmol/L), whereas any form of Cushing syndrome fails to suppress. Two concordant abnormal tests are usually required, because depression, alcohol use, severe obesity, and acute illness can produce mild false positives (pseudo-Cushing states)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C03","obj":"EN-12-A.3","title":"Primary and secondary hyperaldosteronism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone is made in the zona glomerulosa in response to angiotensin II and plasma K+. It binds the mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct and increases apical ENaC channels, basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, and apical ROMK K+ channels. Na+ entering through ENaC leaves the lumen electrically negative, which drives K+ secretion through ROMK. Aldosterone also stimulates the H+-ATPase of alpha-intercalated cells."},{"type":"p","text":"In primary hyperaldosteronism the adrenal makes aldosterone without regard to renin. Bilateral idiopathic adrenal hyperplasia is now the more common cause, and an aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome) most of the rest. Sodium retention expands volume and raises blood pressure, and the expanded volume suppresses renin. The classic triad is hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with high aldosterone and low renin, so a high aldosterone to renin ratio is the screening clue. Many patients are normokalemic at diagnosis, so a normal K+ does not exclude it."},{"type":"flow","title":"From autonomous aldosterone to the lab picture","steps":["Adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia secretes aldosterone independently","More ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells","Lumen-negative voltage drives K+ secretion","Alpha-intercalated cells secrete more H+","Hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","Volume expansion raises pressure and suppresses renin"],"caption":"Hypokalemia itself sustains the alkalosis by shifting H+ into cells and increasing renal ammoniagenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Edema is absent because of aldosterone escape. After a few days of sodium retention, the higher arterial pressure causes pressure natriuresis and stretched atria release natriuretic peptides, so sodium excretion returns to match intake at a slightly larger volume. Potassium and hydrogen ion wasting continue because distal Na+ delivery stays high. Hypokalemia causes muscle weakness, polyuria from reduced collecting duct response to ADH, and U waves on the ECG."},{"type":"p","text":"In secondary hyperaldosteronism the stimulus is renin. Renal hypoperfusion or low effective arterial volume activates the renin-angiotensin system, so renin and aldosterone are both high. Renal artery stenosis and renin-secreting tumors cause it with hypertension; heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome cause it with edema; and diuretics or vomiting cause it with low or normal pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading renin and aldosterone together","cols":["Pattern","Renin","Aldosterone","Examples"],"rows":[["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Low","High","Bilateral hyperplasia, Conn adenoma"],["Secondary hyperaldosteronism","High","High","Renal artery stenosis, heart failure, cirrhosis, diuretics"],["Mineralocorticoid effect without aldosterone","Low","Low","Cushing syndrome, 11-beta- and 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency, licorice, Liddle syndrome"]],"caption":"Hypertension with hypokalemia always prompts this three-way split."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two hypertensive patients have hypokalemia and high aldosterone. One has an abdominal bruit and high renin; the other has low renin. Which has an adrenal lesion?","explain":"The low-renin patient. Autonomous aldosterone expands volume and suppresses renin. In the patient with a bruit, renal artery stenosis lowers pressure at the juxtaglomerular apparatus, so renin rises first and drives aldosterone, a secondary form.","check":"The renin and aldosterone table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C04","obj":"EN-12-A.4","title":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a group of autosomal recessive defects in enzymes needed to make cortisol. Low cortisol removes negative feedback, ACTH rises, and chronic ACTH stimulation enlarges both adrenal cortices. Steroid precursors pile up behind the block and are pushed into any pathway that remains open. Every finding can be predicted from two questions: which products are missing, and which products are being overmade."},{"type":"flow","title":"General logic of congenital adrenal hyperplasia","steps":["Enzyme defect blocks cortisol synthesis","Low cortisol removes feedback; ACTH rises","ACTH causes bilateral adrenal hyperplasia","Precursors accumulate behind the block","Precursors shunted into open pathways","Deficient and excess products set the phenotype"],"caption":"ACTH can also drive skin pigmentation, most obvious in the 21-hydroxylase form."},{"type":"p","text":"21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2) deficiency causes over 90% of cases. The enzyme converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol, so both the mineralocorticoid and glucocorticoid arms are blocked. 17-hydroxyprogesterone, the diagnostic marker, accumulates and is diverted through 17,20-lyase to adrenal androgens. In the classic salt-wasting form, aldosterone deficiency causes Na+ loss, hypovolemia, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and high renin, usually presenting as a crisis in the first weeks of life. Girls (46,XX) are born with ambiguous, virilized external genitalia but normal internal female organs; boys look normal at birth and may present with salt wasting or later with early virilization. Milder forms present as simple virilizing or nonclassic disease with hirsutism and irregular menses."},{"type":"p","text":"11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1) deficiency blocks the final step to cortisol. 11-deoxycortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone (DOC) accumulate, and androgens rise. DOC is a potent mineralocorticoid, so instead of salt wasting the patient has hypertension and hypokalemia with low renin and low aldosterone, together with virilization."},{"type":"p","text":"17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1) deficiency blocks entry into both the cortisol and the sex steroid pathways. Pregnenolone is diverted to DOC and corticosterone, producing hypertension and hypokalemia with low renin and aldosterone. Corticosterone has enough glucocorticoid activity to prevent adrenal crisis. Without sex steroids, 46,XY individuals are undervirilized with female-appearing external genitalia, and 46,XX individuals have no puberty and primary amenorrhea."},{"type":"table","title":"The three CAH forms","cols":["Feature","21-hydroxylase","11-beta-hydroxylase","17-alpha-hydroxylase"],"rows":[["Mineralocorticoid effect","Deficient: salt wasting","Excess DOC","Excess DOC and corticosterone"],["Blood pressure","Low","High","High"],["Serum K+","High","Low","Low"],["Renin","High","Low","Low"],["Adrenal androgens","High","High","Low"],["Marker","17-hydroxyprogesterone","11-deoxycortisol","DOC, corticosterone"],["46,XX","Virilized at birth","Virilized at birth","No puberty"],["46,XY","Normal at birth; early virilization","Normal at birth; early virilization","Undervirilized"]],"caption":"Androgens decide genital findings; DOC versus aldosterone deficiency decides blood pressure and K+."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting a CAH variant","text":"A 15-year-old with female external genitalia, no breast development, and blood pressure 160/100 mm Hg has K+ 2.9 mEq/L and low renin. Hypertension with low K+ and low renin means a mineralocorticoid other than aldosterone is in excess, which points to 11-beta- or 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency. Virilization would mean 11-beta. Absent puberty means no sex steroids, so the block is at 17-alpha-hydroxylase. Karyotype may be 46,XX or 46,XY; in either case no estrogen is made, and a 46,XY patient was undervirilized in utero because no testosterone was made. DOC is high, cortisol is low, and corticosterone prevents crisis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C05","obj":"EN-12-A.5","title":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"PTH raises plasma ionized calcium in three ways. It stimulates osteoblasts to express RANKL, which activates osteoclasts and releases calcium and phosphate from bone. It increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule and reduces phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule by removing NaPi-IIa cotransporters. It also stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, and the resulting calcitriol increases intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. Normally a rise in calcium acts on the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) of chief cells to suppress PTH."},{"type":"p","text":"In primary hyperparathyroidism the gland escapes this feedback. A single parathyroid adenoma causes about 85% of cases, multigland hyperplasia about 10% (including MEN1 and MEN2A), and carcinoma about 1%. The laboratory signature is hypercalcemia with a PTH that is high or inappropriately normal, since any detectable PTH is abnormal when calcium is high. Phosphate is low or low-normal because of PTH phosphaturia. PTH also reduces proximal bicarbonate reabsorption, so a mild hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis may occur, and alkaline phosphatase rises when bone turnover is high. In hypercalcemia of malignancy, by contrast, PTH is suppressed."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Kidney stones (calcium oxalate or phosphate), nephrocalcinosis","Filtered calcium load exceeds distal reabsorption, so urine calcium is high"],["Polyuria, thirst","Calcium on CaSR in the thick ascending limb reduces NaCl reabsorption and the collecting duct responds less to ADH"],["Bone pain, fractures, raised alkaline phosphatase","Sustained osteoclastic resorption, mainly of cortical bone"],["Constipation, nausea","High extracellular calcium reduces smooth muscle and nerve excitability"],["Peptic ulcer","Calcium stimulates gastrin release"],["Pancreatitis","Calcium deposition and premature trypsinogen activation in the pancreas"],["Fatigue, depression, confusion","Reduced neuronal excitability from raised extracellular calcium"],["Short QT interval","Faster ventricular repolarization"]],"caption":"The older summary is stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric overtones; most cases today are found on routine chemistry while asymptomatic."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypercalcemia raises the threshold for opening voltage-gated Na+ channels by screening negative surface charges on the membrane, so nerves and muscle become less excitable. This single mechanism explains the weakness, constipation, and mental slowing."},{"type":"h","text":"Osteitis fibrosa cystica"},{"type":"p","text":"Severe or long-standing disease produces osteitis fibrosa cystica. Osteoclasts tunnel into trabeculae and resorb cortical bone, the marrow is replaced by fibrovascular tissue, and cysts form. The classic radiographic sign is subperiosteal resorption on the radial side of the middle phalanges of the index and middle fingers, with a salt-and-pepper skull and tapering of the distal clavicles. Brown tumors are masses of osteoclast-type giant cells, fibrous stroma, and hemorrhage; hemosiderin from the bleeding gives the brown color. They are reactive, not neoplastic, and regress when PTH falls. Brown tumors can also occur in severe secondary hyperparathyroidism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C06","obj":"EN-12-A.6","title":"Secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is an appropriate rise in PTH in response to a stimulus outside the parathyroid gland, usually low calcium, high phosphate, or low calcitriol. The glands are behaving normally, so calcium is low or low-normal, never high. The two classic causes are chronic kidney disease (CKD) and vitamin D deficiency, and phosphate separates them."},{"type":"p","text":"In CKD, falling glomerular filtration reduces phosphate excretion. Phosphate retention raises FGF23 from osteocytes, and FGF23 together with loss of renal mass reduces 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity, so calcitriol falls early. Low calcitriol reduces intestinal calcium absorption and also removes calcitriol's direct suppression of the PTH gene. Phosphate stimulates PTH directly and binds calcium. The parathyroid cells respond with hyperplasia and, over time, express fewer CaSR and vitamin D receptors, so they become harder to suppress."},{"type":"flow","title":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD","steps":["Falling GFR retains phosphate","FGF23 rises; renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase falls","Low calcitriol reduces gut calcium absorption","Low calcium, high phosphate, low calcitriol stimulate PTH","Parathyroid hyperplasia; fewer CaSR and vitamin D receptors","High-turnover bone disease and renal osteodystrophy"],"caption":"The rise in PTH is appropriate at first; gland hyperplasia is what later allows autonomy."},{"type":"p","text":"In vitamin D deficiency (poor sunlight, malabsorption, liver disease), low calcitriol reduces calcium absorption and PTH rises to defend calcium. Because the kidneys work, PTH causes phosphaturia and phosphate absorption from the gut is also low, so phosphate is low. The bone lesion is defective mineralization, osteomalacia in adults and rickets in children."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal osteodystrophy is the bone disease of CKD. It includes high-turnover osteitis fibrosa from PTH excess, defective mineralization (osteomalacia), low-turnover adynamic bone disease when PTH is oversuppressed, and mixed forms. Bone also buffers the metabolic acidosis of CKD, adding to mineral loss, and a high calcium-phosphate product promotes vascular and soft tissue calcification."},{"type":"p","text":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism develops when years of stimulation leave hyperplastic, often nodular glands whose set point no longer responds to calcium. PTH becomes very high and calcium rises into the hypercalcemic range. It is seen most often in long-standing dialysis patients and after kidney transplantation, when the new kidney makes calcitriol but the autonomous glands keep secreting PTH."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading calcium, phosphate, and PTH","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Urine calcium"],"rows":[["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low or low-normal","High or inappropriately normal","Normal or high"],["Secondary, CKD","Low or normal","High","High","Low"],["Secondary, vitamin D deficiency","Low or normal","Low","High","Low"],["Tertiary","High","Often high (CKD) or variable","Very high","Variable"],["Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (C12)","Mildly high","Normal or low-normal","Normal or mildly high","Very low"],["Hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTHrP)","High","Low","Suppressed","High"]],"caption":"Calcium tells you whether the gland is autonomous; phosphate tells you whether the kidney is failing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A dialysis patient has calcium 8.2 mg/dL (2.05 mmol/L), phosphate 7.0 mg/dL (2.3 mmol/L), and PTH ten times normal. Two years after a successful kidney transplant, calcium is 11.4 mg/dL (2.85 mmol/L) and PTH is still very high. What changed?","explain":"Before transplant this was secondary disease: PTH was high because calcium and calcitriol were low and phosphate high. After transplant those stimuli were corrected, but the hyperplastic glands had become autonomous, so PTH stayed high and now raises calcium. That is tertiary hyperparathyroidism.","check":"The CKD flow and the tertiary paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C07","obj":"EN-12-A.7","title":"Gigantism and acromegaly","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Growth hormone (GH) acts in two ways. Its indirect, growth-promoting effects are mediated by IGF-1, made mainly in the liver and also locally in cartilage, which stimulates chondrocyte proliferation, bone growth, and growth of soft tissues and viscera. Its direct effects oppose insulin: GH increases lipolysis and hepatic glucose output and reduces insulin-stimulated glucose uptake. GH excess almost always comes from a somatotroph pituitary adenoma (C08)."},{"type":"p","text":"Whether GH excess causes gigantism or acromegaly depends only on the epiphyseal growth plates. Before the plates fuse, IGF-1 drives chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate, so long bones lengthen and the child becomes very tall with proportionately large hands and feet; this is gigantism. After fusion, which estrogen completes at the end of puberty, linear growth is impossible. Excess GH and IGF-1 then thicken bone by periosteal (appositional) growth and enlarge cartilage and soft tissue; this is acromegaly. A patient whose disease begins in adolescence and continues into adulthood can show both."},{"type":"table","title":"Acromegaly: findings and mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Frontal bossing, prognathism, widened tooth spacing, enlarged nose and lips","Periosteal bone growth and soft tissue enlargement of the face and jaw"],["Enlarged hands and feet (rising ring and shoe size)","Bone, cartilage, and soft tissue growth"],["Macroglossia, deep voice, sleep apnea","Soft tissue growth of tongue, pharynx, and larynx"],["Carpal tunnel syndrome, arthropathy","Soft tissue and synovial swelling; cartilage overgrowth"],["Thick, oily skin, excess sweating","Dermal and sweat gland hypertrophy"],["Insulin resistance, impaired glucose tolerance, diabetes","Direct anti-insulin effects of GH on liver, muscle, and fat"],["Hypertension","GH and IGF-1 promote renal sodium retention"],["Cardiomyopathy, heart failure","Concentric biventricular hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, diastolic dysfunction"],["Headache, bitemporal hemianopia, hypopituitarism","Mass effect of a macroadenoma on the optic chiasm and normal pituitary"]],"caption":"Visceromegaly and colonic polyps also occur, reflecting IGF-1's growth effects on many tissues."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiovascular disease, chiefly cardiomyopathy, hypertension, and arrhythmia, is the leading cause of death in untreated acromegaly, with respiratory disease and diabetes also contributing. Because the changes are slow, patients often go years before diagnosis, and comparison with old photographs is a classic clue. About a quarter to a third of these tumors also secrete prolactin, and a large tumor can compress the stalk and remove dopamine inhibition, so galactorrhea and hypogonadism may coexist."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C08","obj":"EN-12-A.8","title":"Confirming GH excess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A random GH level is unreliable. GH is released in pulses, mainly during slow-wave sleep and after exercise, stress, or fasting, and its plasma half-life is only about 20 minutes, so a single value in a healthy person can be high or nearly undetectable. IGF-1 solves this problem. It circulates bound to IGF-binding protein 3 and an acid-labile subunit, has a half-life of many hours, and therefore integrates GH secretion over the previous day. A raised IGF-1 is the screening test for acromegaly."},{"type":"p","text":"IGF-1 must be read against age- and sex-matched ranges. It is physiologically high in puberty and pregnancy, and it is reduced by malnutrition, liver disease, hypothyroidism, poorly controlled diabetes, and oral estrogen, which can mask GH excess."},{"type":"p","text":"The oral glucose tolerance test confirms autonomy. In a normal person a 75 g glucose load raises blood glucose, which increases hypothalamic somatostatin and suppresses GH to below about 1 ng/mL (lower cutoffs, about 0.4 ng/mL, apply with sensitive assays). A somatotroph adenoma does not respond to this signal, so GH stays high and sometimes rises paradoxically. After biochemical confirmation, pituitary MRI locates the tumor."},{"type":"flow","title":"Diagnostic sequence for GH excess","steps":["Clinical suspicion: acral and facial growth, sleep apnea","Measure IGF-1 against age and sex range","IGF-1 high: give 75 g oral glucose","GH fails to suppress: autonomous GH secretion","Pituitary MRI, usually a macroadenoma","Normal pituitary on MRI: look for ectopic GHRH"],"caption":"Biochemistry first, then imaging, as with Cushing syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Most somatotroph adenomas are macroadenomas (over 10 mm) at diagnosis. On routine histology they are usually acidophilic (eosinophilic) cells, as normal somatotrophs are. About 40% carry an activating somatic mutation of GNAS, the gene for the stimulatory G protein alpha subunit, which keeps adenylyl cyclase active and mimics constant GHRH stimulation. Rarely, GH excess comes from ectopic GHRH secreted by a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor or bronchial carcinoid, which causes pituitary somatotroph hyperplasia rather than an adenoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C09","obj":"EN-12-A.9","title":"Graves disease versus toxic multinodular goiter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both Graves disease and toxic multinodular goiter are forms of hyperthyroidism, meaning the gland itself is overproducing hormone. Both show low TSH, from negative feedback of thyroid hormone on thyrotrophs and the hypothalamus, with high free T4 and T3. They differ in what drives the gland and in which tissue responds, and the radioiodine uptake scan shows that difference."},{"type":"p","text":"Graves disease is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism, typically in women aged 20 to 50, and is linked to HLA-DR3 and other susceptibility genes. IgG thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins bind the TSH receptor and activate it as TSH would, through Gs and cAMP. This is stimulatory type II hypersensitivity. Every follicle is stimulated, so the gland enlarges diffusely and becomes highly vascular, often with a bruit, and radioiodine uptake is diffusely increased. Histology shows tall, crowded columnar follicular cells, pale scalloped colloid, and lymphoid infiltrates. T3 production is often disproportionately high."},{"type":"p","text":"The extrathyroidal signs are specific to Graves disease because orbital fibroblasts and preadipocytes, and dermal fibroblasts, express the TSH receptor. Autoantibodies and infiltrating T cells activate these fibroblasts to secrete hydrophilic glycosaminoglycans such as hyaluronan, and orbital preadipocytes differentiate into fat. Swollen extraocular muscles and fat push the globe forward, causing proptosis, diplopia, and in severe cases optic nerve compression. The same glycosaminoglycan deposition in the pretibial dermis produces nonpitting pretibial myxedema. Lid retraction and lid lag, by contrast, come from sympathetic overactivity of the smooth muscle of the upper lid and occur in any thyrotoxicosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Toxic multinodular goiter arises in a long-standing goiter, usually in an older adult from an area of past iodine deficiency. Some nodules acquire activating somatic mutations of the TSH receptor or Gs alpha and secrete hormone independently of TSH. The resulting suppressed TSH silences the normal tissue, so the scan shows patchy uptake in hot nodules with a suppressed background. An iodine load, as from contrast media, can unmask it. There is no autoimmunity, so there is no ophthalmopathy or dermopathy. A single autonomous nodule is a toxic adenoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Graves disease versus toxic multinodular goiter","cols":["Feature","Graves disease","Toxic multinodular goiter"],"rows":[["Driver","TSH receptor stimulating antibody","Autonomous nodules (activating TSH receptor or Gs mutations)"],["Typical patient","Woman aged 20 to 50","Older adult with long-standing goiter"],["Gland","Diffuse, vascular goiter, often with bruit","Multiple nodules"],["Radioiodine uptake","Diffusely increased","Patchy: hot nodules, suppressed background"],["Ophthalmopathy, pretibial myxedema","Present in many","Absent"],["Lid lag, lid retraction","Present","Present"],["TSH, free T4","Low, high","Low, high"]],"caption":"Low-uptake thyrotoxicosis (subacute or painless thyroiditis, exogenous hormone) is not hyperthyroidism of the gland and is covered elsewhere (EN-12-C)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C10","obj":"EN-12-A.10","title":"Thyrotoxic manifestations and thyroid storm","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thyrotoxicosis is the clinical state of excess thyroid hormone action from any source. T4 is converted to T3 in tissues, and T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors that pair with the retinoid X receptor on thyroid response elements and change gene transcription. Two groups of target genes explain nearly every finding: those that raise metabolic rate and those that increase sensitivity to catecholamines."},{"type":"p","text":"T3 increases synthesis of Na+/K+-ATPase, mitochondrial enzymes, and mitochondria themselves, so ATP turnover, oxygen consumption, and heat production rise. Patients are heat intolerant and sweat, because the body must dissipate the extra heat through cutaneous vasodilation. They lose weight despite a large appetite, since fat and protein are consumed faster than they are replaced, and proximal muscles waste. Gut transit speeds, causing frequent stools."},{"type":"p","text":"Circulating catecholamines are normal or low, but tissues respond more strongly. T3 increases beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart and alters post-receptor signaling, and it acts directly on cardiac genes, increasing alpha-myosin heavy chain and SERCA2 and reducing phospholamban, which speeds contraction and relaxation. Peripheral vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance, so cardiac output rises, pulse pressure widens, and systolic pressure climbs. T3 also shortens the atrial action potential and refractory period, which favors atrial fibrillation, especially in older patients, and can lead to high-output heart failure. Heightened adrenergic responsiveness in the nervous system causes fine tremor, hyperreflexia, anxiety, and lid retraction."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone increases bone remodeling, activating both osteoclasts and osteoblasts, but resorption exceeds formation, so bone mineral density falls and fracture risk rises, particularly in postmenopausal women. Released calcium can cause mild hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria, which suppress PTH."},{"type":"table","title":"Thyrotoxic findings and their basis","cols":["Finding","Main mechanism"],"rows":[["Heat intolerance, sweating, warm moist skin","More Na+/K+-ATPase and mitochondrial activity raise heat production"],["Weight loss with increased appetite","Higher basal metabolic rate; catabolism of fat and protein"],["Tachycardia, wide pulse pressure","More beta-1 receptors, direct cardiac gene effects, low vascular resistance"],["Atrial fibrillation","Shortened atrial refractory period plus adrenergic drive"],["Tremor, hyperreflexia, anxiety, lid lag","Increased adrenergic responsiveness of nerve and muscle"],["Osteoporosis","Accelerated remodeling with resorption exceeding formation"],["Frequent stools, oligomenorrhea","Faster gut motility; altered gonadal steroid binding and metabolism"]],"caption":"Normal catecholamine levels with exaggerated responses is why adrenergic findings dominate."},{"type":"h","text":"Thyroid storm"},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid storm is acute, life-threatening decompensation of thyrotoxicosis, usually in untreated or undertreated Graves disease. It is triggered by a stressor such as infection, surgery, trauma, childbirth, an iodine load, or abrupt withdrawal of antithyroid medication, which adds a surge of sympathetic activity and may raise the free hormone fraction. The features are those of thyrotoxicosis pushed past the limits of compensation: high fever, often above 40 °C (104 °F), as heat production exceeds heat loss; tachycardia often above 140 per minute, atrial fibrillation, and heart failure; agitation, delirium, or coma; and vomiting, diarrhea, and jaundice. Hormone levels are not reliably higher than in uncomplicated thyrotoxicosis, so the diagnosis rests on clinical severity. Mortality remains substantial even with intensive care."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C11","obj":"EN-12-A.11","title":"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ADH (vasopressin) is made in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei and released from the posterior pituitary when plasma osmolality rises above about 280 to 285 mOsm/kg, or when effective arterial volume falls substantially. It binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, activates Gs and cAMP, and inserts aquaporin-2 channels into the apical membrane, so water is reabsorbed into the hypertonic medulla. Normally, falling osmolality switches ADH off and a dilute urine excretes the excess water."},{"type":"p","text":"In SIADH, ADH is secreted even though plasma osmolality is low and volume is normal. The ADH may come from the tumor itself, as in small cell lung carcinoma, or from the posterior pituitary under abnormal stimulation, as in CNS disease (trauma, stroke, subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis), pulmonary disease (pneumonia, tuberculosis), drugs (SSRIs, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide, vincristine), and postoperative pain and nausea."},{"type":"p","text":"The kidney keeps reabsorbing water, so total body water rises and plasma sodium is diluted. Two-thirds of the retained water enters cells, so the extracellular expansion is modest and there is no edema. That small expansion still raises natriuretic peptides and suppresses renin and aldosterone, and the kidney excretes sodium. This natriuresis brings volume back near normal at the cost of further sodium loss, which is why the patient is clinically euvolemic and why urine sodium is high (usually above about 30 to 40 mEq/L) despite hyponatremia. Serum uric acid and BUN are often low because of the mild volume expansion."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory criteria for SIADH","cols":["Measure","Finding","Why"],"rows":[["Serum sodium","Below 135 mEq/L","Retained free water dilutes sodium"],["Serum osmolality","Below about 275 mOsm/kg","True hypotonic hyponatremia"],["Urine osmolality","Above 100 mOsm/kg, often above serum","ADH acting when it should be off"],["Urine sodium","High, above about 30 to 40 mEq/L","Natriuresis from mild volume expansion"],["Volume status","Clinically euvolemic","Water spread across all compartments; escape natriuresis"],["Thyroid and adrenal function","Normal","Hypothyroidism and glucocorticoid deficiency must be excluded"]],"caption":"SIADH is a diagnosis of exclusion; glucocorticoid deficiency raises ADH because cortisol normally restrains it."},{"type":"table","title":"SIADH among the hyponatremias","cols":["Type","Examples","Urine sodium","Urine osmolality"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic, extrarenal loss","Vomiting, diarrhea","Low (below 20 mEq/L)","High"],["Hypovolemic, renal loss","Diuretics, mineralocorticoid deficiency","High","High"],["Euvolemic","SIADH, hypothyroidism, glucocorticoid deficiency","High","High"],["Euvolemic, low solute or excess water","Primary polydipsia, low solute intake","Variable","Low (below 100)"],["Hypervolemic","Heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome","Low","High"]],"caption":"Volume status first, then urine sodium and osmolality."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms come from the brain. As plasma osmolality falls, water moves into neurons and glia, causing cerebral edema with headache, nausea, confusion, seizures, and coma, most severe when sodium falls quickly or below about 120 mEq/L. Within about 48 hours brain cells export K+ and organic osmolytes to restore volume. This adaptation makes chronic hyponatremia less symptomatic but leaves the brain vulnerable to osmotic demyelination if sodium is raised too fast."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C12","obj":"EN-12-A.12","title":"Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) is a G protein-coupled receptor on parathyroid chief cells and on cells of the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. In the parathyroid, rising calcium activates CaSR and suppresses PTH secretion. In the thick ascending limb, CaSR activation reduces the lumen-positive voltage that drives paracellular calcium and magnesium reabsorption, so more calcium is excreted when plasma calcium is high. CaSR therefore sets both the PTH response and the renal response to calcium."},{"type":"p","text":"Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) is usually caused by a heterozygous inactivating mutation of CASR, inherited as an autosomal dominant trait. With fewer working receptors, a higher calcium is needed to suppress PTH, so the set point shifts upward. Plasma calcium settles at a mildly raised level with a normal or mildly raised PTH, and magnesium is often high-normal. The kidney also reads calcium as low, so it keeps reabsorbing calcium and urine calcium is inappropriately low."},{"type":"p","text":"FHH is usually lifelong, asymptomatic, and benign, with no stones or bone disease, and parathyroidectomy does not correct the calcium because the abnormality is in every chief cell and in the kidney. That is why it must be distinguished from primary hyperparathyroidism before surgery. Infants who inherit two inactivating alleles develop neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism with very high calcium and PTH."},{"type":"table","title":"FHH versus primary hyperparathyroidism","cols":["Feature","FHH","Primary hyperparathyroidism"],"rows":[["Cause","Inactivating CASR mutation, autosomal dominant","Adenoma, hyperplasia, or carcinoma"],["Plasma calcium","Mildly high, stable for life","Mildly to markedly high"],["PTH","Normal or mildly high","High or inappropriately normal"],["Urine calcium","Low","Normal or high"],["Calcium to creatinine clearance ratio","Usually below 0.01","Usually above 0.02"],["Family history","Hypercalcemia in relatives, often from childhood","Usually sporadic"],["Stones, bone disease","Absent","May be present"],["Parathyroidectomy","Does not cure","Cures"]],"caption":"The urinary calcium measurement is the decisive test."},{"type":"example","title":"Calcium to creatinine clearance ratio","text":"A 35-year-old has calcium 10.9 mg/dL and PTH at the upper limit of normal. Her mother has the same finding. A 24-hour urine gives calcium 80 mg/day. The ratio is (urine calcium times serum creatinine) divided by (serum calcium times urine creatinine). With serum creatinine 0.8 mg/dL and urine creatinine 1,200 mg/day: (80 times 0.8) divided by (10.9 times 1,200) equals 64 divided by 13,080, about 0.005. A value below 0.01 means the kidney is retaining calcium despite hypercalcemia, which fits FHH. In primary hyperparathyroidism a high filtered load meets normal CaSR braking of loop reabsorption, so the ratio is usually above 0.02. Ratios of 0.01 to 0.02 are indeterminate."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-01-C13","obj":"EN-12-A.13","title":"Central versus peripheral precocious puberty","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Puberty begins when the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator, quiet through childhood, is reactivated. Pulsatile GnRH stimulates gonadotrophs to release LH and FSH, which drive gonadal sex steroid production and gametogenesis. Precocious puberty is traditionally defined as secondary sexual development before age 8 in girls or 9 in boys. Sex steroids accelerate growth and bone maturation, so affected children are tall for age with advanced bone age, but estrogen also closes the growth plates early, which reduces adult height."},{"type":"p","text":"Central (gonadotropin-dependent) precocious puberty is the normal axis switched on early. LH and FSH are in the pubertal range, and stimulation with GnRH or a GnRH agonist produces a pubertal, LH-predominant response. Development is consonant, following the normal sequence: breast development first in girls, testicular enlargement (above 4 mL) first in boys because FSH and LH act on the testis. Most cases in girls are idiopathic. Boys more often have a CNS cause, such as a hypothalamic hamartoma, tumor, hydrocephalus, or prior cranial irradiation, so imaging is essential. Loss-of-function mutations in MKRN3, a gene that restrains GnRH, cause some familial cases."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral (gonadotropin-independent) precocious puberty arises when sex steroids are made without GnRH. These steroids feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, so LH and FSH are low and do not rise with GnRH stimulation. In boys the testes stay small because they are not being stimulated, unless the source is in the testis itself. CAH, especially 21-hydroxylase deficiency, causes early virilization from adrenal androgens. McCune-Albright syndrome, from a postzygotic activating GNAS mutation in a mosaic distribution, causes autonomous ovarian estrogen production (most often in girls), cafe-au-lait macules with irregular borders, and polyostotic fibrous dysplasia. Sex steroid secreting tumors of the ovary, testis, or adrenal, hCG-secreting germ cell tumors in boys (hCG acts on the LH receptor), and exogenous hormone exposure complete the list."},{"type":"table","title":"Central versus peripheral precocious puberty","cols":["Feature","Central","Peripheral"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Early activation of GnRH pulses","Sex steroids made independently of GnRH"],["Basal LH and FSH","Pubertal (raised)","Low (suppressed)"],["Response to GnRH stimulation","Pubertal, LH-predominant","Flat"],["Sequence of development","Normal order","Often out of order"],["Testes in boys","Enlarged (above 4 mL)","Small, unless testicular tumor or hCG source"],["Examples","Idiopathic, hypothalamic hamartoma, CNS tumor, irradiation","CAH, McCune-Albright, gonadal or adrenal tumors, hCG tumors, exogenous steroids"],["Bone age","Advanced","Advanced"]],"caption":"LH and FSH reveal whether the drive comes from the brain or from the periphery."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged exposure to sex steroids from a peripheral source can mature the hypothalamus and trigger secondary central puberty, so the two forms can coexist in a child with long-untreated CAH."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every syndrome in this chapter is a failure of negative feedback, and the paired test shows which side failed. Plasma ACTH separates pituitary from adrenal cortisol excess, renin separates adrenal from renal aldosterone excess, PTH read beside calcium and phosphate separates autonomous from appropriate parathyroid activity, TSH with the uptake scan separates gland overactivity from leakage, and LH and FSH separate central from peripheral puberty. Histology supplies the lesions: pituitary, adrenal, and parathyroid adenomas, parathyroid hyperplasia, diffuse thyroid hyperplasia in Graves disease, and ectopic hormone from small cell carcinoma, a neuroendocrine lung tumor. Biochemistry supplies the CAH pathway, genetics supplies CASR, CYP21A2, and GNAS mutations (GNAS links somatotroph adenomas, toxic nodules, and McCune-Albright syndrome through constant cAMP signaling), and immunology supplies the stimulating antibody of Graves disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal physiology is where most of these diseases become measurable. Aldosterone excess appears as K+ and H+ loss, ADH excess as a concentrated urine with natriuresis, PTH excess as hypercalciuria and phosphaturia, and CaSR loss as hypocalciuria. The limits are equally important. Hormone tests are confounded by circadian rhythm, binding proteins, drugs, illness, and assay differences, so mild abnormalities need confirmation, and imaging follows biochemistry because incidental pituitary and adrenal nodules are common."}],"summary":["Cushing syndrome is confirmed by loss of regulation (high 24-hour urine free cortisol, high late-night salivary cortisol, no suppression with 1 mg dexamethasone) and localized by ACTH: low means adrenal or exogenous, normal or high means pituitary or ectopic.","High-dose dexamethasone suppresses a pituitary corticotroph adenoma but not ectopic ACTH; ectopic ACTH causes hyperpigmentation and severe hypokalemia.","Cortisol excess features follow from protein catabolism, insulin antagonism, fat redistribution, and spillover onto mineralocorticoid receptors.","Primary hyperaldosteronism is hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with high aldosterone and low renin, without edema because of aldosterone escape; secondary forms have high renin.","In CAH, 21-hydroxylase deficiency causes salt wasting and virilization with high 17-hydroxyprogesterone; 11-beta causes virilization with hypertension; 17-alpha causes hypertension with absent sex steroids.","Primary hyperparathyroidism gives high calcium, low phosphate, and high or inappropriately normal PTH, usually from a single adenoma, with stones, bone disease, and osteitis fibrosa cystica.","Secondary hyperparathyroidism has low or normal calcium with high PTH; phosphate is high in CKD and low in vitamin D deficiency; tertiary disease is autonomous with hypercalcemia.","FHH has mildly high calcium, normal or mildly high PTH, and low urine calcium (clearance ratio below 0.01) from an inactivating CASR mutation, and surgery does not help.","GH excess causes gigantism before epiphyseal closure and acromegaly after; it is confirmed by high IGF-1 and failure of GH to suppress after oral glucose.","Graves disease (diffuse uptake, eye and skin signs) and toxic multinodular goiter (patchy uptake) both give low TSH and high free T4; SIADH gives euvolemic hyponatremia with concentrated urine and high urine sodium; central precocious puberty has raised LH and FSH, peripheral has suppressed LH and FSH."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine system sections on the thyroid gland, the adrenal gland, and the endocrine regulation of growth"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex; Thyroid Disease; Disorders of the Parathyroids and Calcium and Phosphorus Metabolism; Disorders of the Hypothalamus and Pituitary Gland"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Steroid hormone synthesis and adrenal cortical enzyme deficiencies"}]},"EN-12-B":{"unit":"EN-12-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02","title":"Diseases of Endocrine Hypofunction","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers diseases in which a gland makes too little hormone or its target cannot respond: diabetes insipidus, primary, acute, and secondary adrenal insufficiency, hypoparathyroidism and pseudohypoparathyroidism, hypopituitarism and hypothalamic lesions, the hypothyroid states, nonthyroidal illness, and short stature. Each axis is a feedback loop, so pairing the trophic hormone with its target hormone locates the defect: a low target hormone with a high trophic hormone means the gland or end organ has failed, while a low target hormone with a low or inappropriately normal trophic hormone means the pituitary or hypothalamus has failed. The electrolyte, skin, and cardiovascular findings then follow from what each missing hormone normally does.","prerequisites":["Hypothalamic and pituitary anatomy, the hypophyseal portal circulation, and negative feedback (EN-3, EN-5, EN-6)","ADH action on the collecting duct and the medullary concentrating gradient","Adrenal cortical zones, their hormones, and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","PTH, calcitriol, and calcium and phosphate handling by bone, gut, and kidney (EN-5)","Thyroid hormone synthesis, deiodination, and nuclear receptor action (EN-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hyperfunction (EN-12-A)","how":"Each hypofunction state here has a mirror-image excess state, and the same trophic and target hormone pairing locates both."},{"to":"Renal physiology and acid-base","how":"Diabetes insipidus, aldosterone deficiency, and PTH deficiency are read through water, potassium, hydrogen ion, calcium, and phosphate handling by the nephron."},{"to":"Cardiac electrophysiology (CV-7)","how":"Hyperkalemia from aldosterone loss and the long QT of hypocalcemia are electrolyte effects on the cardiac action potential."},{"to":"Immunology and microbiology (EN-10, EN-11)","how":"Autoimmune adrenalitis, autoimmune hypoparathyroidism, tuberculosis, and meningococcemia are leading causes of gland destruction."},{"to":"Embryology and genetics (EN-1, EN-9)","how":"DiGeorge syndrome, thyroid dysgenesis, GNAS imprinting, and X-linked V2 receptor defects explain congenital forms."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primary hypofunction","def":"Failure of the target gland itself, so its hormone is low and the pituitary trophic hormone rises."},{"term":"Secondary hypofunction","def":"Failure of the pituitary to secrete a trophic hormone, so both trophic and target hormones are low; failure of the hypothalamus is sometimes called tertiary."},{"term":"End-organ resistance","def":"Failure of target tissue to respond to a hormone that is present, so hormone levels rise while the clinical picture resembles deficiency."},{"term":"Diabetes insipidus","def":"Excretion of large volumes of dilute urine because ADH is absent (central) or the kidney cannot respond to it (nephrogenic)."},{"term":"Desmopressin","def":"A synthetic ADH analog selective for the V2 receptor, used in testing to ask whether the collecting duct can respond to ADH."},{"term":"Proopiomelanocortin (POMC)","def":"The precursor protein cleaved in corticotrophs to ACTH and related peptides, including melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH)."},{"term":"Adrenal crisis","def":"Acute, life-threatening cortisol deficiency, with or without aldosterone deficiency, presenting as shock."},{"term":"Tetany","def":"Involuntary sustained muscle contraction caused by spontaneous firing of hyperexcitable motor nerves, as in hypocalcemia."},{"term":"Albright hereditary osteodystrophy","def":"A phenotype of short stature, round face, obesity, and short fourth and fifth metacarpals caused by inactivating GNAS mutations."},{"term":"Goiter","def":"Enlargement of the thyroid gland, from any cause, most often TSH-driven hyperplasia when hormone synthesis is impaired."},{"term":"Reverse T3 (rT3)","def":"An inactive isomer of T3 formed when type 3 deiodinase removes an iodine from the inner ring of T4."},{"term":"Bone age","def":"Skeletal maturity judged from a hand and wrist radiograph and compared with chronological age."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C01","obj":"EN-12-B.1","title":"Central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) is made in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, carried down axons in the pituitary stalk, and released from the posterior pituitary. Osmoreceptors trigger release when plasma osmolality rises by as little as about 1%; a large fall in blood volume or pressure is a second, less sensitive stimulus. ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells of the collecting duct. The Gs-cAMP-protein kinase A pathway inserts aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane, and water leaves through basolateral aquaporin-3 and aquaporin-4 into the hypertonic medulla. ADH also raises urea permeability in the inner medullary collecting duct, which helps maintain the medullary gradient."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ADH release to concentrated urine","steps":["Rising plasma osmolality stimulates hypothalamic osmoreceptors","ADH released from posterior pituitary","ADH binds V2 receptor on principal cell","Gs raises cAMP, protein kinase A activated","Aquaporin-2 inserted into apical membrane","Water moves into hypertonic medulla, urine concentrated"],"caption":"Central diabetes insipidus breaks the chain at ADH release; nephrogenic diabetes insipidus breaks it at the V2 receptor, cAMP signaling, or aquaporin-2."},{"type":"p","text":"Diabetes insipidus is the loss of this system. In central diabetes insipidus too little ADH is secreted. Symptoms usually appear only after most of the ADH-producing neurons are lost, and lesions low on the stalk spare more function than lesions near the hypothalamus because ADH can still be released from the cut proximal end. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus ADH is present, often raised, but the collecting duct cannot respond."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of diabetes insipidus","cols":["Type","Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Central","Head trauma, pituitary or hypothalamic surgery","Damage to ADH neurons or their axons in the stalk"],["Central","Craniopharyngioma, germinoma, metastases, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, sarcoidosis","Destruction or infiltration of the hypothalamus or stalk"],["Central","Idiopathic (often autoimmune)","Inflammation of hypothalamic nuclei or the stalk"],["Nephrogenic, inherited","AVPR2 mutation (X-linked, most inherited cases)","Nonfunctional V2 receptor"],["Nephrogenic, inherited","AQP2 mutation (autosomal recessive or dominant)","Defective or misrouted aquaporin-2"],["Nephrogenic, acquired","Lithium","Enters principal cells through ENaC and reduces aquaporin-2 expression"],["Nephrogenic, acquired","Hypercalcemia","Calcium-sensing receptor activation reduces aquaporin-2 and weakens the medullary gradient"],["Nephrogenic, acquired","Hypokalemia","Reduced aquaporin-2 expression and a weaker medullary gradient"]],"caption":"Central causes act on ADH supply; nephrogenic causes act on the collecting duct response."},{"type":"p","text":"Both forms produce the same picture: polyuria (more than about 3 L per day in an adult), nocturia, intense thirst, and inappropriately dilute urine, usually below about 300 mOsm/kg and often below 200 mOsm/kg in complete disease. Thirst normally replaces the lost water, so serum sodium stays high-normal. Hypernatremia (sodium above about 145 mEq/L) appears when intake cannot keep up: in infants, in elderly or obtunded patients, after surgery, or when a hypothalamic lesion also destroys the thirst center. This separates diabetes insipidus from primary polydipsia, in which excess drinking comes first and plasma sodium and osmolality run low-normal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C02","obj":"EN-12-B.2","title":"Interpreting the water deprivation test","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The water deprivation test asks two questions in sequence. First, when the patient is dehydrated, can the body concentrate urine with its own ADH? Second, if it cannot, does the kidney respond when ADH activity is supplied as desmopressin? Fluids are withheld while urine osmolality, plasma osmolality or sodium, and body weight are measured at intervals. Deprivation ends when urine osmolality plateaus, plasma osmolality or sodium rises above normal, or body weight falls by about 3 to 5%. Desmopressin is then given and urine osmolality is measured again."},{"type":"p","text":"A healthy person concentrates urine well above plasma during deprivation, often to more than about 600 to 800 mOsm/kg, because rising osmolality releases endogenous ADH; desmopressin then adds little, since ADH is already acting at full effect. In central diabetes insipidus the dehydrated patient cannot release ADH, so urine stays dilute, but the collecting duct is intact and urine osmolality rises sharply (commonly by more than about 50%) after desmopressin. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus urine stays dilute both before and after desmopressin, because the defect lies beyond ADH. Primary polydipsia behaves like the normal response, although the maximum concentration may be somewhat reduced because chronic water loading washes out solute from the medullary interstitium."},{"type":"table","title":"Expected results of water deprivation and desmopressin","cols":["Condition","Plasma osmolality at start","Urine after deprivation","Urine after desmopressin"],"rows":[["Normal","Normal","Concentrated","Little further rise"],["Primary polydipsia","Low-normal","Concentrated, sometimes submaximal","Little further rise"],["Complete central DI","High-normal","Remains dilute","Large rise"],["Partial central DI","High-normal","Partly concentrated","Further moderate rise"],["Nephrogenic DI","High-normal","Remains dilute","Little or no rise"]],"caption":"Deprivation tests endogenous ADH release; desmopressin tests the kidney's response to ADH."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the two steps","text":"A man with polyuria has a plasma sodium of 144 mEq/L. After 6 hours of water deprivation his plasma osmolality is 302 mOsm/kg and urine osmolality is 180 mOsm/kg. Step 1: plasma is concentrated, yet urine is less concentrated than plasma, so ADH is either missing or ineffective; primary polydipsia is excluded. After desmopressin, urine osmolality rises to 520 mOsm/kg, nearly a threefold increase. Step 2: the collecting duct responds to ADH, so the receptor and aquaporin-2 pathway work and the defect is ADH supply. This is central diabetes insipidus. If urine had stayed near 200 mOsm/kg, the lesion would be nephrogenic."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C03","obj":"EN-12-B.3","title":"Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary adrenal insufficiency is destruction of the adrenal cortex itself, so all three zones fail together: the zona glomerulosa (aldosterone), the zona fasciculata (cortisol), and the zona reticularis (adrenal androgens). Symptoms usually appear only after about 90% of both cortices are destroyed, which explains why the onset is gradual and why unilateral disease does not cause it. In developed countries the most common cause is autoimmune adrenalitis, with lymphocytic infiltration and autoantibodies most often directed against 21-hydroxylase; it may be isolated or part of autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (AIRE mutation, with hypoparathyroidism and mucocutaneous candidiasis) or type 2 (with autoimmune thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes). Worldwide, tuberculosis is a leading cause, through caseating destruction that may later calcify. Bilateral metastases, most often from lung or breast carcinoma, and disseminated fungal infection are other causes."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Feedback in primary adrenal failure","steps":["Adrenal cortex destroyed, cortisol falls","Less negative feedback on hypothalamus and pituitary","CRH and POMC synthesis increase","ACTH and MSH rise but cannot restore cortisol"],"caption":"The loop stays open: rising ACTH cannot act on a destroyed cortex, so ACTH remains high."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding follows from a missing hormone. Aldosterone normally drives sodium reabsorption through ENaC in principal cells, with potassium secretion through ROMK and hydrogen ion secretion by intercalated cells. Without it the kidney wastes sodium and water, plasma volume falls, and potassium and hydrogen ion are retained, giving hypotension, salt craving, hyperkalemia, and a mild non-anion gap metabolic acidosis. Renin rises because volume is low. Hyponatremia has two sources: renal sodium loss, and increased ADH release from both hypovolemia and cortisol deficiency, since cortisol normally restrains ADH and is needed to excrete free water."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol loss causes fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, fasting hypoglycemia (less gluconeogenesis), and eosinophilia and lymphocytosis. Low cortisol removes feedback, so the pituitary increases POMC synthesis. Cleavage of POMC yields ACTH and related melanocortin peptides including MSH, and ACTH itself also stimulates the melanocortin 1 receptor on melanocytes, increasing melanin. Hyperpigmentation therefore appears in sun-exposed skin, palmar creases, knuckles, scars, gums, and buccal mucosa. Loss of adrenal androgens reduces axillary and pubic hair in women, who depend on adrenal precursors for much of their androgen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C04","obj":"EN-12-B.4","title":"Acute adrenocortical insufficiency (adrenal crisis)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adrenal crisis is an acute shortfall of cortisol relative to need. A normal adrenal can increase cortisol output several-fold during infection, surgery, or trauma. A patient whose adrenal or pituitary reserve is lost cannot, so a stress that a healthy person tolerates becomes life-threatening. Three settings account for most cases."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of adrenal crisis","cols":["Setting","Mechanism","Aldosterone"],"rows":[["Stress (infection, surgery, trauma, vomiting) in known chronic insufficiency","Demand rises but cortisol cannot increase","Low in primary disease, preserved in secondary"],["Abrupt withdrawal of long-term glucocorticoids","Suppressed CRH and ACTH and an atrophied fasciculata cannot restart quickly","Preserved, because the glomerulosa does not depend on ACTH"],["Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage: anticoagulation, DIC, antiphospholipid syndrome","Sudden destruction of both glands","Low"],["Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","Hemorrhagic adrenal necrosis in fulminant sepsis, classically Neisseria meningitidis, with DIC","Low"],["Pituitary apoplexy","Sudden loss of ACTH","Preserved"]],"caption":"Infection is the most common trigger in known insufficiency; hemorrhage is the most abrupt cause."},{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal is vulnerable to hemorrhage because it has a rich arterial supply draining into a single central vein. Venous congestion or thrombosis, or a coagulopathy, therefore causes hemorrhage into the gland. In meningococcemia, endotoxin-driven DIC and endothelial injury produce bilateral hemorrhagic necrosis together with a purpuric rash."},{"type":"p","text":"Shock is the central feature, and cortisol explains why. Cortisol is permissive for vascular tone: it maintains expression of adrenergic receptors and the vascular response to catecholamines and angiotensin II, and it supports endothelial integrity. Cortisol delivered through the intra-adrenal portal blood also induces phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase in the medulla, so epinephrine synthesis falls with it. Without cortisol, vessels dilate and respond poorly to catecholamines, producing hypotension that responds poorly to fluids and vasopressors. In primary disease, aldosterone loss adds volume depletion and hyperkalemia. Patients also have abdominal pain, vomiting, fever, confusion, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia, which can mimic an acute abdomen or sepsis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cortisol loss to shock","steps":["Stress raises cortisol demand","Adrenal or pituitary cannot increase cortisol","Vessels lose adrenergic responsiveness and tone","Aldosterone loss adds sodium and water depletion (primary)","Vasodilation and hypovolemia lower blood pressure","Shock unresponsive to fluids and pressors alone"],"caption":"The aldosterone step applies only when the cortex itself has failed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C05","obj":"EN-12-B.5","title":"Secondary adrenocortical insufficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Secondary adrenal insufficiency is cortisol deficiency caused by too little ACTH. The most common cause overall is suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis by long-term exogenous glucocorticoids, which feed back on CRH and ACTH just as cortisol does. Pituitary causes include macroadenoma, surgery, radiation, Sheehan necrosis, and apoplexy, often with other pituitary deficits. Hypothalamic disease that removes CRH (tertiary insufficiency) behaves the same way."},{"type":"p","text":"Two classic signs of primary disease are absent, and both are predictable. ACTH is low, so there is no excess POMC-derived peptide and no hyperpigmentation; patients may look pale. Aldosterone is regulated mainly by angiotensin II and plasma potassium, with ACTH only a minor, short-term influence, so the zona glomerulosa keeps working. Potassium therefore stays normal, salt craving and severe volume depletion are uncommon, and renin is not markedly raised."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol deficiency still causes fatigue, weight loss, hypoglycemia, and hyponatremia. The hyponatremia here is dilutional rather than salt-losing: cortisol deficiency increases ADH release and impairs free water excretion, so water is retained while volume is near normal. Chronic ACTH deficiency also causes the fasciculata and reticularis to atrophy. This is why the adrenal response to an ACTH (cosyntropin) stimulation test is blunted in long-standing secondary disease even though the gland is not destroyed, but may still be normal soon after a sudden pituitary insult."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary versus secondary adrenal insufficiency","cols":["Finding","Primary","Secondary"],"rows":[["Cortisol","Low","Low"],["ACTH","High","Low or inappropriately normal"],["Aldosterone","Low","Normal"],["Renin","High","Normal"],["Potassium","High","Normal"],["Sodium","Low (salt loss plus ADH)","May be low (dilutional, ADH)"],["Hyperpigmentation","Present","Absent"],["Other pituitary hormones","Normal","Often deficient if the pituitary is diseased"]],"caption":"ACTH level and the presence of hyperkalemia and hyperpigmentation separate the two forms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient stops prednisone abruptly after a year of high-dose use. Predict the ACTH, aldosterone, potassium, and skin findings.","explain":"ACTH is suppressed, so cortisol falls without hyperpigmentation; angiotensin II still drives aldosterone, so potassium stays normal.","check":"Compare with the primary versus secondary table and the regulation of the zona glomerulosa."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C06","obj":"EN-12-B.6","title":"Hypoparathyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is secreted when the calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells detects a fall in ionized calcium. It raises calcium and lowers phosphate by three actions: it increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule, inhibits sodium-phosphate cotransport in the proximal tubule, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase so that calcitriol increases intestinal absorption. It also mobilizes calcium from bone. In hypoparathyroidism all of these fail together, giving low serum calcium, high phosphate, low calcitriol, and a PTH that is low or inappropriately normal for the calcium level."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hypoparathyroidism","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Surgical removal or devascularization during thyroid, parathyroid, or neck surgery","Most common cause; may be transient or permanent"],["Autoimmune, isolated or in autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1","Immune destruction of chief cells; APS-1 adds Addison disease and candidiasis"],["DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)","Failure of third and fourth pharyngeal pouches: no parathyroids and thymic hypoplasia with T cell deficiency"],["Severe hypomagnesemia","Magnesium is needed for PTH secretion and action, so PTH is low and less effective"],["Activating calcium-sensing receptor mutation","Chief cells sense normal calcium as high and suppress PTH"],["Infiltration (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease) or radiation","Destruction of glandular tissue"]],"caption":"Low PTH with low calcium and high phosphate defines true hypoparathyroidism whatever the cause."},{"type":"p","text":"The neuromuscular signs come from the effect of extracellular calcium on voltage-gated sodium channels. Calcium ions bound to the outer membrane surface raise the threshold for channel opening. When ionized calcium falls, sodium channels open at a less depolarized voltage, so peripheral nerves fire spontaneously and repetitively. Sensory nerves give perioral and fingertip numbness and tingling; motor nerves give cramps, carpopedal spasm, and in severe cases laryngospasm and seizures. Alkalosis worsens symptoms because albumin binds more calcium and ionized calcium falls, which is why hyperventilation can provoke tetany."},{"type":"bullets","items":["Chvostek sign: tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear causes twitching of the ipsilateral facial muscles; it is also seen in some healthy people.","Trousseau sign: inflating a blood pressure cuff above systolic pressure for about 3 minutes causes carpal spasm (wrist and metacarpophalangeal flexion, interphalangeal extension, thumb adduction); it is more specific."]},{"type":"p","text":"In the heart, hypocalcemia lengthens the plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential, which prolongs the ST segment and therefore the QT interval, while the T wave itself is usually normal. Chronic hypoparathyroidism also causes basal ganglia calcification, cataracts, and dental abnormalities if it begins in childhood. Total calcium must be read with albumin, since low albumin lowers total calcium without changing ionized calcium; a common correction adds 0.8 mg/dL for every 1 g/dL that albumin falls below 4 g/dL. Normal total calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L) and phosphate about 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C07","obj":"EN-12-B.7","title":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is end-organ resistance to PTH. The PTH receptor (PTH1R) signals through the stimulatory G protein alpha subunit (Gs alpha), which activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. Gs alpha is encoded by GNAS, an imprinted gene. In the renal proximal tubule and in some endocrine tissues (thyroid, gonads, pituitary somatotrophs), Gs alpha is expressed mainly from the maternal allele, while most other tissues express both alleles. This tissue-specific imprinting explains the inheritance pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"In pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a, an inactivating GNAS mutation inherited from the mother removes nearly all Gs alpha in the proximal tubule. The kidney cannot respond to PTH: phosphate is retained and calcitriol is not made. Serum calcium falls and phosphate rises, exactly as in true hypoparathyroidism, but the parathyroid glands are healthy and respond to hypocalcemia, so PTH is high. Because one functional allele is missing in all tissues, patients also have Albright hereditary osteodystrophy: short stature, round face, obesity, subcutaneous ossification, and brachydactyly with short fourth and fifth metacarpals. Resistance to other Gs-coupled hormones, especially TSH and gonadotropins, is common. In the classic test, giving PTH fails to raise urinary cAMP, which places the block before cAMP formation."},{"type":"p","text":"When the same mutation is inherited from the father, the kidney still expresses Gs alpha from the normal maternal allele, so PTH acts normally and calcium is normal. The patient has the Albright phenotype without hormone resistance, called pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism. Type 1b results from defective methylation of the GNAS locus and causes renal PTH resistance without the Albright phenotype."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing low calcium states by PTH","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Albright phenotype"],"rows":[["True hypoparathyroidism","Low","High","Low","No"],["Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a (maternal GNAS)","Low","High","High","Yes"],["Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1b (methylation defect)","Low","High","High","Usually no"],["Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism (paternal GNAS)","Normal","Normal","Normal","Yes"],["Vitamin D deficiency","Low or normal","Low","High","No"]],"caption":"High phosphate with low calcium points to PTH deficiency or resistance; PTH level separates the two. Low phosphate points to vitamin D deficiency with secondary hyperparathyroidism."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two unrelated children both have short fourth metacarpals from an inactivating GNAS mutation. One has tetany and the other has normal calcium. Predict which parent passed the mutation to each child.","explain":"The child with tetany inherited it from the mother, because the renal tubule expresses Gs alpha mainly from the maternal allele; the child with normal calcium inherited it from the father.","check":"Compare with the imprinting paragraph and the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C08","obj":"EN-12-B.8","title":"Hypopituitarism and empty sella syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypopituitarism is deficiency of one or more anterior pituitary hormones. Clinical deficiency usually appears only after about 75% of the anterior lobe is lost. When loss is gradual, as with compression, GH and the gonadotropins usually fail first, then TSH, and ACTH last, while prolactin is typically preserved or even mildly raised. ACTH loss is the most dangerous deficit because it can cause adrenal crisis. The posterior pituitary usually keeps working in pituitary adenoma, so diabetes insipidus suggests a hypothalamic or stalk lesion instead."},{"type":"table","title":"Major causes of hypopituitarism","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Clue"],"rows":[["Nonfunctioning macroadenoma (most common in adults)","Compression of normal pituitary and stalk","Headache, bitemporal hemianopia from chiasm compression"],["Sheehan necrosis","Pregnancy enlarges the gland by lactotroph hyperplasia without matching blood supply; postpartum hemorrhage and hypotension cause infarction","Failure to lactate, then amenorrhea, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency"],["Pituitary apoplexy","Sudden hemorrhage or infarction, usually into an adenoma","Sudden severe headache, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia from cavernous sinus nerve compression, adrenal crisis"],["Surgery","Removal or damage of normal tissue or stalk","Onset right after the operation"],["Radiation","Delayed damage to pituitary and hypothalamus","Deficits develop over years; GH is most sensitive"],["Other","Trauma, infiltrative disease, lymphocytic hypophysitis","Hypophysitis often occurs in late pregnancy or postpartum"]],"caption":"Sheehan necrosis and apoplexy are acute and vascular; adenoma and radiation are gradual."},{"type":"p","text":"Each deficit has a predictable picture. GH deficiency causes poor growth in children and reduced lean mass and increased fat in adults. Gonadotropin deficiency causes amenorrhea, infertility, low libido, and loss of secondary sexual characteristics. TSH deficiency causes central hypothyroidism, and ACTH deficiency causes secondary adrenal insufficiency without hyperpigmentation or hyperkalemia. In each case the target hormone is low and the trophic hormone is low or inappropriately normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragma sellae is a fold of dura that roofs the sella turcica and is pierced only by the pituitary stalk. In primary empty sella syndrome this diaphragm is congenitally incomplete, so the arachnoid and its CSF-filled subarachnoid space herniate into the sella. CSF pulsation flattens the pituitary against the floor, and imaging shows a sella that appears empty. It is common in obese, multiparous women and in idiopathic intracranial hypertension. Pituitary function is usually normal because the flattened tissue still works, although mild hyperprolactinemia from stalk traction or hypopituitarism can occur. Secondary empty sella follows loss of pituitary tissue from surgery, radiation, apoplexy, or Sheehan necrosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C09","obj":"EN-12-B.9","title":"Hypothalamic lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The hypothalamus controls the anterior pituitary through releasing hormones (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH) secreted into the hypophyseal portal veins at the median eminence. Prolactin is the exception: it is under tonic inhibition by dopamine delivered through the same portal veins. ADH is made by hypothalamic neurons and travels down the stalk to the posterior pituitary. A lesion of the hypothalamus or upper stalk therefore produces three linked findings, which together identify the site."},{"type":"flow","title":"Predicting the effects of a hypothalamic or stalk lesion","steps":["Lesion destroys hypothalamic nuclei or upper stalk","Releasing hormones fail to reach the anterior pituitary","GH, LH, FSH, TSH, and ACTH fall","Dopamine delivery fails, so prolactin rises","ADH neurons damaged, causing central diabetes insipidus"],"caption":"Low anterior pituitary output with high prolactin and diabetes insipidus points above the pituitary."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone that rises, and usually only mildly, a pattern called the stalk effect. A small or modest prolactin rise with a large sellar mass therefore suggests stalk compression rather than a prolactinoma. Diabetes insipidus is common because the lesion reaches ADH neurons or their axons above the median eminence. It may be masked when ACTH deficiency coexists, since cortisol is needed to excrete free water, and appear once cortisol is restored."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hypothalamic dysfunction","cols":["Lesion","Features"],"rows":[["Craniopharyngioma","Arises from Rathke pouch remnants; peaks in children and older adults; suprasellar, cystic, often calcified, with oily fluid; growth failure, bitemporal hemianopia, diabetes insipidus"],["Infiltrative disease (sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis)","Granulomas or histiocytes in the hypothalamus and stalk; diabetes insipidus is a classic presentation"],["Germinoma","Suprasellar germ cell tumor in children and young adults, often with diabetes insipidus"],["Cranial radiation","Delayed loss of releasing hormones; GH deficiency first"],["Trauma","Shearing of the stalk or hypothalamus"]],"caption":"Diabetes insipidus at presentation favors a hypothalamic or stalk lesion over a pituitary adenoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the hypothalamus also controls appetite, temperature, thirst, and sleep, large lesions add hyperphagia and obesity, temperature instability, somnolence, and loss of thirst. Loss of thirst with diabetes insipidus is especially dangerous because water loss is not replaced and severe hypernatremia develops."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child with a calcified suprasellar mass has polyuria, slowed growth, and a mildly raised prolactin. Explain why prolactin rose while GH fell.","explain":"The lesion stops both GHRH and dopamine reaching the pituitary; loss of a stimulator lowers GH, while loss of the inhibitor releases prolactin.","check":"Compare with the flow figure and the stalk effect paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C10","obj":"EN-12-B.10","title":"Iodine deficiency goiter and the manifestations of hypothyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Iodide is concentrated into follicular cells by the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS), oxidized and attached to tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin by thyroid peroxidase, and coupled to form T4 and T3. Adults need about 150 micrograms of iodine per day, more in pregnancy. When supply falls, less hormone is made. The gland adapts by trapping iodide more avidly and by making relatively more T3, which needs one fewer iodine atom and is more potent, but output still falls and the pituitary responds."},{"type":"flow","title":"From iodine deficiency to multinodular goiter","steps":["Dietary iodine deficient","Less T4 and T3 synthesized","Less negative feedback, TSH rises","Follicular hypertrophy and hyperplasia: diffuse goiter","Repeated cycles of hyperplasia and involution","Uneven follicle growth, hemorrhage, fibrosis: multinodular goiter"],"caption":"TSH drives growth; over years, heterogeneous follicle clones turn diffuse enlargement into nodules."},{"type":"p","text":"Early on the gland is diffusely enlarged and may stay euthyroid because TSH-driven growth compensates. Over years, follicular cells that respond unequally to TSH grow at different rates, and areas of hemorrhage, scarring, and calcification create a multinodular goiter. Some nodules become autonomous and no longer depend on TSH. A large goiter compresses the trachea or esophagus, causing dyspnea or dysphagia, or extends behind the sternum. Iodine deficiency is the most common cause of goiter and hypothyroidism worldwide, while autoimmune thyroiditis is the most common cause where iodine intake is sufficient."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone enters cells, T4 is converted to T3, and T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors that regulate gene transcription. It raises basal metabolic rate through Na+/K+-ATPase and mitochondrial activity, increases cardiac beta-1 receptors and contractile proteins, increases hepatic LDL receptors, and speeds muscle contraction and relaxation. Hypothyroidism reverses each of these."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations of hypothyroidism explained by mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Cold intolerance, fatigue","Lower basal metabolic rate and heat production"],["Weight gain (modest)","Lower energy expenditure plus fluid and glycosaminoglycan retention"],["Bradycardia, reduced cardiac output, diastolic hypertension","Fewer beta-1 receptors, weaker contractility, raised peripheral resistance"],["Myxedema (nonpitting edema, coarse face, large tongue, hoarse voice)","Glycosaminoglycans such as hyaluronic acid accumulate in dermis and soft tissue and bind water"],["Hyperlipidemia (raised LDL)","Fewer hepatic LDL receptors, so LDL clearance falls"],["Delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes","Slower muscle contraction and relaxation from reduced calcium reuptake and myosin ATPase activity"],["Constipation, dry skin, slowed thinking","Reduced gut motility, sweat and sebum secretion, and neuronal metabolism"]],"caption":"Each sign reverses a normal action of T3."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C11","obj":"EN-12-B.11","title":"Interpreting thyroid function tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"TSH is the most sensitive screening test because the pituitary responds to small changes in free T4 with large, roughly logarithmic changes in TSH. That sensitivity depends on a normal pituitary and hypothalamus. Free T4 is read alongside it, because the pairing locates the defect. Typical adult reference ranges are about 0.4 to 4.5 mIU/L for TSH and about 0.8 to 1.8 ng/dL (10 to 23 pmol/L) for free T4, varying by laboratory."},{"type":"table","title":"Thyroid function patterns","cols":["Pattern","TSH","Free T4","Explanation"],"rows":[["Primary hypothyroidism","High","Low","Gland fails (autoimmune thyroiditis, iodine deficiency, ablation); feedback raises TSH"],["Subclinical primary hypothyroidism","High","Normal","Early gland failure compensated by TSH"],["Central hypothyroidism","Low or inappropriately normal","Low","Pituitary or hypothalamus cannot respond; TSH made may also be less bioactive"],["TSH-secreting adenoma","High or inappropriately normal","High","Autonomous TSH drives the gland despite high T4; often raised glycoprotein alpha subunit"],["Primary hyperthyroidism (for contrast)","Low","High","Gland overproduces; feedback suppresses TSH"]],"caption":"Discordance, a TSH that fails to move opposite to free T4, signals a pituitary or hypothalamic problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Central hypothyroidism is the main trap. A normal TSH looks reassuring, but in the setting of a low free T4 it is inappropriately normal, meaning the pituitary is not responding. Screening with TSH alone therefore misses central disease. A TSH-secreting adenoma is the reverse discordance: both TSH and free T4 are high, and the patient is hyperthyroid with a goiter. The main alternative with the same pattern is thyroid hormone resistance, which is usually familial and lacks a pituitary mass and a raised alpha subunit."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary hypothyroidism can raise prolactin. Low T4 increases hypothalamic TRH, and TRH stimulates lactotrophs as well as thyrotrophs; clearance of prolactin may also be reduced. Prolactin suppresses pulsatile GnRH release, so LH and FSH fall, causing anovulation, oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and infertility in women and hypogonadism in men. Prolonged TRH stimulation can also enlarge the pituitary through thyrotroph hyperplasia, which can be mistaken for an adenoma. Restoring thyroid hormone removes the TRH drive, so checking TSH is part of evaluating any hyperprolactinemia."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a normal TSH that is not normal","text":"A woman who had a severe postpartum hemorrhage now has fatigue, cold intolerance, and no menses. TSH is 1.2 mIU/L and free T4 is 0.5 ng/dL. Step 1: free T4 is low, so she is hypothyroid. Step 2: with low free T4, the normal pituitary would drive TSH well above range; a TSH of 1.2 is inappropriately normal, so the pituitary is not responding. Step 3: this is central hypothyroidism, and the history points to Sheehan necrosis, so ACTH and gonadotropin deficiency should be expected as well. Had TSH been 25 mIU/L, the defect would be in the thyroid itself."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C12","obj":"EN-12-B.12","title":"Congenital hypothyroidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid forms from endoderm at the foramen cecum of the tongue and descends along the thyroglossal duct to the front of the neck. The fetal thyroid begins making hormone at about 10 to 12 weeks of gestation. Before then the fetus relies on maternal T4, and some maternal T4 continues to cross the placenta until birth. This explains why most affected newborns look normal at birth. Thyroid hormone is essential for neuronal migration, myelination, and synapse formation in the fetal and early postnatal brain, so untreated deficiency in the first months of life causes permanent intellectual disability."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of congenital hypothyroidism","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Goiter"],"rows":[["Thyroid dysgenesis (agenesis, hypoplasia, ectopic gland such as lingual thyroid)","Failure of thyroid formation or descent; most permanent cases where iodine is sufficient","No"],["Dyshormonogenesis (thyroid peroxidase, thyroglobulin, NIS, DUOX2, or pendrin defects)","Inherited, usually autosomal recessive, enzyme or transport defects in hormone synthesis","Yes, because TSH drives a gland that cannot make hormone"],["Maternal iodine deficiency (endemic cretinism)","Neither mother nor fetus can make enough hormone; most common cause worldwide","Often"],["Transplacental TSH receptor blocking antibodies or maternal antithyroid drugs","Temporary blockade of the fetal gland","Variable; transient"]],"caption":"Goiter at birth points to a synthesis defect or iodine deficiency rather than an absent gland."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical signs reflect slowed metabolism and glycosaminoglycan accumulation, the same mechanisms seen in adults. Infants are lethargic, feed poorly, sleep excessively, and are hypotonic and constipated, with a hoarse cry, dry skin, and a cool body. Jaundice is prolonged and unconjugated, because thyroid hormone is needed for maturation of hepatic bilirubin conjugation. Macroglossia comes from glycosaminoglycan deposition in the tongue, and umbilical hernia from weak abdominal wall tone. A large posterior fontanelle and delayed bone age reflect slowed skeletal maturation. Because symptoms appear late and brain damage starts early, newborn screening detects the disease before it is clinically obvious. Pendred syndrome, a pendrin defect, combines goiter with sensorineural deafness."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C13","obj":"EN-12-B.13","title":"Nonthyroidal illness (euthyroid sick) syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid secretes mostly T4, and most circulating T3 comes from peripheral removal of an outer-ring iodine by type 1 deiodinase (liver, kidney, thyroid) and type 2 deiodinase (brain, pituitary, muscle, brown fat). Type 3 deiodinase removes an inner-ring iodine, converting T4 to inactive reverse T3 (rT3) and T3 to inactive T2. Reverse T3 is itself cleared mainly by type 1 deiodinase."},{"type":"p","text":"In severe illness, such as sepsis, myocardial infarction, major surgery, burns, starvation, and critical illness in the intensive care unit, cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha), raised cortisol, and caloric deprivation lower type 1 deiodinase activity and raise type 3 activity. Less T4 becomes T3, more becomes rT3, and rT3 is cleared more slowly, so T3 falls and rT3 rises. This is thought to be an adaptive reduction in catabolism rather than true hypothyroidism. TSH is usually normal or low-normal. In prolonged, severe illness T4 also falls, through reduced binding proteins, circulating binding inhibitors, and central suppression of TRH and TSH by cytokines, glucocorticoids, and dopamine; a low T4 in this setting predicts a worse outcome."},{"type":"table","title":"Nonthyroidal illness compared with true hypothyroidism","cols":["Test","Nonthyroidal illness","Primary hypothyroidism","Central hypothyroidism"],"rows":[["TSH","Usually normal or low-normal; may rise transiently in recovery","High","Low or inappropriately normal"],["Free T4","Normal, low in severe prolonged illness","Low","Low"],["Total T3","Low","Low or normal","Low or normal"],["Reverse T3","High","Low or normal","Low or normal"]],"caption":"A high reverse T3 favors nonthyroidal illness, because true hypothyroidism supplies less T4 to convert into anything."},{"type":"p","text":"Two features cause errors on examinations. During recovery, TSH may rise briefly above normal as the axis restarts, which can be mistaken for primary hypothyroidism. A low T4 with a normal TSH in a critically ill patient can resemble central hypothyroidism; the raised rT3 and the clinical setting point to illness instead."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-02-C14","obj":"EN-12-B.14","title":"Causes of short stature","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Short stature is usually defined as height more than 2 standard deviations below the mean for age and sex. Growth velocity, bone age, and parental heights matter more than a single measurement. GH is secreted in pulses, mainly during sleep, under stimulation by GHRH and ghrelin and inhibition by somatostatin. It binds the GH receptor, which signals through JAK2 and STAT5b, mainly in the liver, to produce IGF-1. IGF-1 drives chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plates and feeds back to suppress GH. Because GH secretion is pulsatile, a single GH level is uninformative, while IGF-1 reflects average GH activity."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing causes of short stature","cols":["Cause","GH","IGF-1","Bone age","Growth pattern and outcome"],"rows":[["GH deficiency","Low on stimulation","Low","Delayed","Slow growth velocity; increased fat, chubby appearance; neonatal hypoglycemia possible"],["GH receptor defect (Laron syndrome)","High","Low","Delayed","Severe growth failure; GH high because IGF-1 feedback is lost"],["Constitutional growth delay","Normal","Normal or low for age, normal for bone age","Delayed","Normal velocity, late puberty, often a parent who matured late; reaches normal adult height"],["Familial short stature","Normal","Normal","Normal (equals chronological age)","Normal velocity and puberty timing; adult height short but in line with parental heights"],["Hypothyroidism","Normal or low","Low","Markedly delayed","Slow growth with weight relatively preserved"]],"caption":"Bone age separates familial short stature (normal) from the delayed forms; GH and IGF-1 separate GH deficiency from receptor resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"The key distinction is between a child who grows slowly and one who grows at a normal rate along a low percentile. Endocrine causes reduce growth velocity and delay bone age, and weight is usually relatively preserved, so the child looks heavy for height. Malnutrition and chronic disease such as celiac disease or inflammatory bowel disease usually reduce weight first. Bone age shows how much growth remains: when it lags behind chronological age, the growth plates stay open longer and catch-up growth is possible, as in constitutional delay. When bone age matches chronological age, the child is simply following a genetically short trajectory."},{"type":"p","text":"Laron syndrome is end-organ resistance, the growth equivalent of pseudohypoparathyroidism: the hormone is high because the receptor cannot respond and the feedback signal (IGF-1) is missing. Other causes include Turner syndrome in girls, glucocorticoid excess (growth failure with weight gain), and skeletal dysplasias, which produce disproportionate short stature."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across this chapter, three principles from physiology predict the pathology. First, feedback loops locate the lesion: high ACTH, TSH, or PTH with a low target effect means the gland or end organ has failed, and a low or inappropriately normal trophic hormone means the pituitary or hypothalamus has failed. End-organ resistance (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, pseudohypoparathyroidism, Laron syndrome) produces a deficiency picture with high hormone levels. Second, each missing hormone explains its own signs through the kidney, vessel, nerve, or cell it normally acts on: aldosterone through principal cells, cortisol through vascular tone and free water excretion, PTH through nephron calcium and phosphate handling, and T3 through nuclear receptors. Third, anatomy and embryology predict which glands fail together: the stalk links ADH, dopamine, and releasing hormones; the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches link the parathyroids and thymus; and autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes link the adrenal, parathyroid, and thyroid."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and microbiology supply the most common causes: autoimmunity in developed countries, tuberculosis and iodine deficiency worldwide, and meningococcal sepsis for adrenal hemorrhage. The limits are worth knowing. Laboratory cutoffs differ between assays and references, a single hormone value can mislead when pulsatile secretion, binding proteins, or acute illness alter it, and several deficiencies can mask one another, as when ACTH deficiency hides diabetes insipidus."}],"summary":["Pair each trophic hormone with its target hormone: high trophic with low target means primary failure; low or inappropriately normal trophic means pituitary or hypothalamic failure.","Central diabetes insipidus lacks ADH; nephrogenic diabetes insipidus cannot respond to it (V2 receptor, aquaporin-2, lithium, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia); both give dilute polyuria, with hypernatremia only when water intake is limited.","On water deprivation, primary polydipsia concentrates urine, central diabetes insipidus concentrates only after desmopressin, and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus does not concentrate at all.","Addison disease removes cortisol and aldosterone: hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, acidosis, and hyperpigmentation from high ACTH and POMC-derived peptides.","Adrenal crisis is cortisol deficiency under stress, steroid withdrawal, or bilateral hemorrhage; loss of cortisol's permissive effect on vascular tone produces shock resistant to fluids and pressors.","Secondary adrenal insufficiency has low ACTH, so there is no hyperpigmentation, and angiotensin II preserves aldosterone, so there is no hyperkalemia.","Hypoparathyroidism gives low calcium, high phosphate, and low PTH; low calcium lowers the nerve firing threshold (tetany, Chvostek and Trousseau signs) and prolongs phase 2 and the QT interval.","Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a is maternally inherited GNAS loss with high PTH and Albright hereditary osteodystrophy; paternal inheritance gives the phenotype without hypocalcemia.","Hypothalamic and stalk lesions lower anterior pituitary hormones, cause diabetes insipidus, and raise prolactin by cutting off dopamine; primary empty sella usually leaves pituitary function intact.","Iodine deficiency raises TSH and causes diffuse then multinodular goiter; euthyroid sick syndrome has low T3, high reverse T3, and usually normal TSH; bone age separates familial short stature from delayed growth."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus; Thyroid Metabolic Hormones; Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine physiology: hypothalamic-pituitary axes, thyroid, adrenal cortex, and calcium regulation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System: pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, and adrenal insufficiency"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the hypothalamus and pituitary, thyroid, parathyroids, and adrenal cortex"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and neck: pharyngeal pouches and development of the thyroid gland"}]},"EN-12-C":{"unit":"EN-12-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03","title":"Inflammatory Diseases of the Thyroid","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the inflammatory diseases of the thyroid: Hashimoto thyroiditis, subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis, painless and postpartum thyroiditis, and Riedel thyroiditis. Each disease is traced from its immune or inflammatory mechanism to the findings it produces in the gland, the blood, the radioiodine scan, and the biopsy. Board questions usually describe how the gland feels, a set of thyroid function tests, an uptake result, or a histology description, then ask for the disease or its next phase. Two questions solve most of them: is the gland making new hormone or leaking stored hormone, and is the follicular cell mass lost for good or able to regenerate?","prerequisites":["Thyroid follicle structure, follicular cells, colloid, and C cells (EN-2)","Thyroid hormone synthesis: iodide trapping by the sodium-iodide symporter, organification by thyroid peroxidase, and storage on thyroglobulin (EN-8)","The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis and negative feedback by T4 and T3 (EN-5, EN-6)","Self-tolerance, T cell effector mechanisms, and germinal center B cell responses (EN-10)","Patterns of chronic inflammation, including granulomatous inflammation and fibrosis"],"connections":[{"to":"Immune responses in endocrine disease (EN-10)","how":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is the model of organ-specific, T cell driven autoimmunity, with anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies serving as markers."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hyperfunction (EN-12-A)","how":"Graves disease and toxic nodular goiter are thyrotoxic with high radioiodine uptake, the key contrast with the low uptake of destructive thyroiditis."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hypofunction (EN-12-B)","how":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is the main cause of the primary hypothyroidism whose clinical features and test patterns are taught there."},{"to":"Endocrine neoplasms (EN-12-G)","how":"Primary thyroid B cell lymphoma arises in Hashimoto thyroiditis, and Riedel thyroiditis can mimic anaplastic carcinoma."},{"to":"Endocrine pathogens (EN-11)","how":"Viral upper respiratory infections precede subacute granulomatous thyroiditis, linking microbiology to a self-limited thyroid disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Thyrotoxicosis","def":"The clinical state produced by excess circulating thyroid hormone from any source; hyperthyroidism strictly means excess hormone production by the thyroid itself."},{"term":"Destructive thyroiditis","def":"Thyroid inflammation that ruptures follicles and releases preformed hormone, causing thyrotoxicosis without any increase in hormone synthesis."},{"term":"Radioactive iodine uptake (RAIU)","def":"The fraction of an administered radioiodine dose taken up by the thyroid over a set time, usually 24 hours, which reflects iodide trapping and active hormone synthesis."},{"term":"Anti-TPO antibodies","def":"Autoantibodies against thyroid peroxidase, the enzyme that oxidizes iodide and couples iodotyrosines, and the most sensitive serologic marker of autoimmune thyroid disease."},{"term":"Hürthle cell","def":"A follicular cell with abundant pink, granular cytoplasm packed with mitochondria (oncocytic or oxyphil change), typical of Hashimoto thyroiditis."},{"term":"Germinal center","def":"The central region of a secondary lymphoid follicle where antigen-stimulated B cells proliferate, undergo affinity maturation, and class switch."},{"term":"Hashitoxicosis","def":"Transient thyrotoxicosis early in Hashimoto thyroiditis, usually from release of stored hormone by injured follicles."},{"term":"Subclinical hypothyroidism","def":"A raised TSH with free T4 still within the reference range, reflecting a gland that keeps up only under extra TSH drive."},{"term":"Granuloma","def":"A focus of chronic inflammation made of activated macrophages (epithelioid cells), often with multinucleated giant cells, surrounded by lymphocytes."},{"term":"IgG4-related disease","def":"A fibroinflammatory disorder with many IgG4-positive plasma cells, storiform fibrosis, and obliterative phlebitis that can involve the thyroid, pancreas, bile ducts, retroperitoneum, and orbit."},{"term":"Postpartum thyroiditis","def":"Painless autoimmune destructive thyroiditis arising within the first year after delivery, when immune activity rebounds after pregnancy."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03-C01","obj":"EN-12-C.1","title":"Hashimoto thyroiditis: autoimmune pathogenesis and histology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hashimoto thyroiditis (chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis) is autoimmune destruction of thyroid follicular cells. It is the most common cause of hypothyroidism wherever dietary iodine is adequate; worldwide, iodine deficiency still causes more. The typical patient is a woman between about 45 and 65 years old with a painless, firm, symmetrically enlarged goiter, and women are affected roughly 10 times as often as men. Risk is also higher in Down syndrome and Turner syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"The disease begins with a failure of self-tolerance to thyroid antigens, chiefly thyroid peroxidase (TPO) and thyroglobulin. Susceptibility is polygenic. Variants of CTLA4 and PTPN22, genes that normally restrain T cell activation, and certain HLA alleles raise risk, and poorly defined environmental triggers act on that background. Once tolerance fails, autoreactive T cells enter the gland and destroy it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of Hashimoto thyroiditis","steps":["Genetic risk (CTLA4, PTPN22, HLA) plus environmental triggers","Loss of tolerance to TPO and thyroglobulin","CD4 T cells activate; Th1 cells release interferon-gamma","CD8 T cells kill follicular cells; activated macrophages add injury","B cells form germinal centers; anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin produced","Follicular cell mass shrinks; TSH rises; free T4 eventually falls"],"caption":"Cell-mediated killing destroys the follicles; the antibodies mainly mark the autoimmune response."},{"type":"p","text":"Injury is mainly cell mediated. CD8 cytotoxic T cells kill follicular cells with perforin and granzymes, and interferon-gamma from Th1 cells activates macrophages that damage follicles further. Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity contributes less. Anti-TPO antibodies are found in about 90% of patients and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies in fewer; both are better markers of disease than causes of it. Some patients also make TSH receptor blocking antibodies, which silence the surviving cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The goiter is painless because the process is slow and does not stretch the capsule acutely. The gland is diffusely enlarged, pale, and rubbery with an intact capsule; its bulk comes from the lymphoid infiltrate plus TSH-driven growth of the remaining epithelium. Years of destruction and fibrosis can later shrink it to a small atrophic gland."},{"type":"table","title":"Histology of Hashimoto thyroiditis","cols":["Finding","What you see","Why it forms"],"rows":[["Lymphoid infiltrate","Lymphocytes and plasma cells with well-formed germinal centers","Antigen-driven B cell responses inside a gland that normally has no lymphoid follicles"],["Atrophic follicles","Small follicles with scant colloid","Follicular cells are destroyed and make less hormone and thyroglobulin"],["Hürthle cells","Large cells with abundant pink, granular cytoplasm","Surviving injured follicular cells accumulate mitochondria (oncocytic metaplasia)"],["Fibrosis","Variable interstitial fibrosis confined within the capsule","Repair after chronic injury"]],"caption":"Germinal centers plus Hürthle cells identify Hashimoto; granulomas around colloid or fibrosis beyond the capsule point elsewhere."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03-C02","obj":"EN-12-C.2","title":"Course of Hashimoto thyroiditis, associated autoimmunity, and lymphoma risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The course of Hashimoto thyroiditis follows the number of working follicular cells. Early on, antibodies are present but thyroid function is normal, because a small rise in TSH pushes the surviving cells to work harder. As destruction outpaces that compensation, TSH rises above its reference range (about 0.4 to 4.0 mIU/L) while free T4 stays normal (about 0.8 to 1.8 ng/dL), which is subclinical hypothyroidism. Overt hypothyroidism follows when free T4 falls. Among people with subclinical hypothyroidism and anti-TPO antibodies, about 4% a year progress to overt disease."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of Hashimoto thyroiditis","steps":["Euthyroid: antibodies present, TSH and free T4 normal","Possible hashitoxicosis: injured follicles leak stored hormone","Subclinical hypothyroidism: TSH high, free T4 normal","Overt hypothyroidism: TSH high, free T4 low","Permanent: ongoing attack and fibrosis prevent regeneration"],"caption":"Not every patient passes through hashitoxicosis, but the end point is lasting primary hypothyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"Hashitoxicosis is a brief thyrotoxic phase when injured follicles release stored hormone. Because the gland is not making extra hormone, radioiodine uptake is usually low, which separates it from Graves disease, where TSH receptor stimulating antibodies drive synthesis and uptake is high. A few patients carry both stimulating and blocking antibodies, so the two diseases can overlap. The later hypothyroidism is permanent because the autoimmune attack continues and lost follicular cells are replaced by lymphoid tissue and fibrosis instead of new epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Patients often have other autoimmune diseases, because the same genetic variants (HLA, CTLA4, PTPN22) weaken tolerance in several organs. Classic companions are type 1 diabetes, autoimmune adrenalitis (Addison disease; the combination forms autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2), autoimmune gastritis with pernicious anemia, celiac disease, vitiligo, Sjögren syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, and myasthenia gravis."},{"type":"p","text":"Hashimoto thyroiditis also raises the risk of primary thyroid lymphoma, most of which arise in a background of the disease. The normal thyroid has no organized lymphoid tissue; Hashimoto creates it, and years of antigen-driven B cell proliferation in germinal centers give repeated chances for mutations, the same logic as gastric MALT lymphoma after Helicobacter pylori gastritis. Most cases are diffuse large B cell lymphoma or extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma. The typical presentation is an older woman with long-standing Hashimoto whose thyroid enlarges rapidly and painlessly, sometimes with dysphagia, dyspnea, or hoarseness from compression. Anaplastic carcinoma presents similarly, so tissue diagnosis is needed. The absolute risk remains low."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is the hypothyroidism of Hashimoto thyroiditis permanent while the hypothyroid phase of subacute thyroiditis usually resolves?","explain":"In Hashimoto the immune attack never switches off, so follicular cells keep dying and are replaced by lymphoid tissue and fibrosis. In subacute thyroiditis the trigger is a passing viral illness; once inflammation subsides, surviving follicular epithelium regenerates and refills its stores.","check":"The course flow above and the recovery phase in EN-12-C.4."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03-C03","obj":"EN-12-C.3","title":"Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis: recognition and low uptake","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis is the most common cause of a painful thyroid. It affects mainly women aged about 30 to 50, several times more often than men, and typically begins a few weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. Coxsackievirus, mumps, measles, adenovirus, and influenza have been implicated, and cases cluster seasonally. A strong association with HLA-B35 suggests that viral antigens, or thyroid antigens altered by viral injury, are presented to cytotoxic T cells that damage follicles. Because the trigger is temporary, the response is self-limited."},{"type":"p","text":"Each feature has a mechanism. Acute inflammation and edema stretch the thyroid capsule, producing anterior neck pain that radiates to the jaw, ear, or throat and worsens with swallowing or turning the head; the gland is firm and exquisitely tender, sometimes on one side first. Circulating cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) cause fever, malaise, and myalgia, and IL-6 drives hepatic acute phase proteins, so the ESR is markedly raised, often above 50 mm/h (reference roughly 0 to 20 mm/h), and CRP is high."},{"type":"p","text":"Histology is patchy, with areas at different stages. Early lesions show disrupted follicles with neutrophils, sometimes forming microabscesses. Later, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages predominate, and granulomas form in which multinucleated giant cells surround or engulf pools and fragments of colloid spilled from ruptured follicles. Healing leaves little fibrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why thyrotoxicosis comes with low radioiodine uptake","steps":["Inflamed follicles rupture and release stored T4 and T3","Free T4 and T3 rise; thyrotoxic symptoms appear","Negative feedback suppresses pituitary TSH","Without TSH, iodide trapping by the symporter falls","Damaged follicular cells cannot trap or organify iodide","24-hour radioiodine uptake is low, often under 5%"],"caption":"The gland is leaking hormone, not making it, so the scan is nearly blank while the patient is thyrotoxic."},{"type":"p","text":"This leaking-versus-making distinction is the core of the diagnosis. In Graves disease, stimulating antibodies keep the gland synthesizing hormone, so uptake is high and diffuse. Two further clues support destruction: serum thyroglobulin is high because it spills from ruptured follicles, and the T4 to T3 ratio is higher because stored hormone is mostly T4, whereas a stimulated gland releases proportionally more T3. Ingestion of exogenous thyroid hormone also gives thyrotoxicosis with low uptake, but thyroglobulin is low because the gland is suppressed and intact."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A thyrotoxic patient has a tender gland, but the 24-hour radioiodine uptake is 35%. Does this fit subacute thyroiditis?","explain":"No. A thyrotoxic patient with subacute thyroiditis has suppressed TSH and damaged follicles, so uptake should be very low. Normal or high uptake during thyrotoxicosis means the gland is synthesizing hormone, as in Graves disease or a toxic nodule.","check":"The low uptake flow and the comparison with Graves disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03-C04","obj":"EN-12-C.4","title":"The self-limited course of subacute thyroiditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The course of subacute thyroiditis is set by two things: the pool of stored hormone and the population of follicular cells. The thyroid normally stores enough hormone in colloid to supply the body for about two to three months. Inflammation empties that store into the blood over a few weeks, but it does not destroy the follicular cells permanently, so the gland can rebuild once the trigger has passed."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of subacute thyroiditis","cols":["Phase","Typical timing","Free T4","TSH","Radioiodine uptake","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Thyrotoxic","First about 3 to 6 weeks","High","Suppressed","Low","Ruptured follicles release stored hormone; TSH falls"],["Transition","Brief","Normal","Low or normal","Low","Stores are spent; suppressed thyrotrophs recover slowly"],["Hypothyroid (not in all)","Weeks to a few months","Low","High","Low at first, may exceed normal","Little stored hormone; rising TSH drives regenerating follicles"],["Recovery","Most by about 6 to 12 months","Normal","Normal","Normal","Follicles regenerate and refill colloid"]],"caption":"Free T4 and TSH move in opposite directions in each phase; uptake recovers only when TSH returns and follicles rebuild."},{"type":"p","text":"The thyrotoxic phase lasts only as long as stored hormone keeps leaking. Released T4 is cleared with a half-life of about 6 to 7 days and T3 in about a day, so once release stops, levels fall within weeks. Pain, tenderness, fever, and the raised ESR fade over the same period as the inflammation settles. TSH may stay low for a short time after free T4 normalizes, because pituitary thyrotrophs recover slowly from weeks of suppression."},{"type":"p","text":"Many patients then pass through a hypothyroid phase. The colloid is empty and the injured follicles are still rebuilding, so output falls below need and TSH rises. That TSH rise is useful: it stimulates the regenerating epithelium, and uptake can briefly exceed normal while the gland refills its stores. Thyroid function returns to normal in most patients within about a year, and recurrence is uncommon. A small minority, roughly 5 to 15% in most series, are left with permanent hypothyroidism because too many follicles were lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","steps":["Viral upper respiratory infection, then painful thyroid","Thyrotoxic phase: high free T4, low TSH, low uptake","Stores exhausted; inflammation and pain subside","Hypothyroid phase: low free T4, high TSH","TSH drives regenerating follicles to refill colloid","Most recover normal function within about a year"],"caption":"The disease is self-limited because the trigger passes and follicular cells regenerate."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading one patient across three visits","text":"A 42-year-old woman has a tender thyroid three weeks after a cold. Visit 1: free T4 3.1 ng/dL (reference about 0.8 to 1.8), TSH under 0.01 mIU/L, ESR 88 mm/h, 24-hour uptake 2%. High T4 with suppressed TSH and a near-blank scan means leaked stored hormone, the thyrotoxic phase. Visit 2, ten weeks later: pain gone, free T4 0.6 ng/dL, TSH 14 mIU/L. Stores are empty and follicles are rebuilding, so this is the transient hypothyroid phase, not new Hashimoto disease; the high TSH is driving recovery. Visit 3, eight months after onset: free T4 1.2 ng/dL, TSH 2.0 mIU/L, so function has recovered. Had TSH remained high at that visit, the most likely explanation would be permanent hypothyroidism from excessive follicle loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-12-03-C05","obj":"EN-12-C.5","title":"Painless, postpartum, and Riedel thyroiditis compared with Hashimoto and subacute thyroiditis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Painless (silent, or subacute lymphocytic) thyroiditis is an autoimmune destructive thyroiditis. Lymphocytes disrupt follicles and release stored hormone, so it follows the same thyrotoxic, hypothyroid, and recovery course as subacute thyroiditis, with low uptake in the thyrotoxic phase. The differences come from the type of inflammation: without the acute, granulomatous injury of de Quervain disease, the gland is painless, nontender, and normal sized or slightly enlarged, and the ESR is normal or only mildly raised. Anti-TPO antibodies are common. Histology shows lymphocytic infiltrates, sometimes with germinal centers, and follicle disruption, but not the prominent fibrosis and Hürthle cell change of Hashimoto, nor granulomas. The same destructive pattern can follow certain drugs, such as amiodarone, lithium, interferon-alpha, and immune checkpoint inhibitors."},{"type":"p","text":"Postpartum thyroiditis is painless thyroiditis arising within a year after delivery. It affects roughly 5 to 10% of women, most often those with anti-TPO antibodies before or early in pregnancy and those with type 1 diabetes. During pregnancy, maternal immune responses are damped to tolerate the fetus and antibody titers fall; after delivery, immunity rebounds and unmasks pre-existing thyroid autoimmunity. A thyrotoxic phase typically appears about 1 to 4 months postpartum and a hypothyroid phase about 4 to 8 months postpartum, though either can occur alone. It often recurs after later pregnancies, and a substantial minority develop permanent hypothyroidism over the following years."},{"type":"p","text":"Riedel thyroiditis is rare and works differently. Dense fibrous tissue replaces the gland and extends through the capsule into the strap muscles, trachea, esophagus, and sometimes the recurrent laryngeal nerve and parathyroid glands. Most cases are now regarded as IgG4-related disease: lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates rich in IgG4-positive plasma cells, storiform fibrosis, and obliterative phlebitis, with cytokines such as TGF-beta activating fibroblasts. The result is a rock-hard, woody, painless gland fixed to surrounding structures. Compression causes dysphagia, dyspnea or stridor, and hoarseness; hypothyroidism appears if enough gland is replaced, and hypocalcemia if the parathyroids are encased. Because a hard, fixed, compressive mass also suggests anaplastic carcinoma, biopsy is needed, and it shows fibrosis without malignant cells. Riedel thyroiditis may coexist with other fibrosing disorders, including retroperitoneal fibrosis, sclerosing cholangitis, autoimmune pancreatitis, and orbital pseudotumor. In the fibrous variant of Hashimoto thyroiditis, by contrast, the fibrosis stays within the capsule."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the four thyroiditides","cols":["Feature","Hashimoto","Subacute granulomatous","Painless or postpartum","Riedel"],"rows":[["Mechanism","T cell autoimmune destruction","Post-viral T cell injury (HLA-B35)","Autoimmune lymphocytic destruction","IgG4-related fibroinflammation"],["Gland on exam","Painless, firm, rubbery goiter","Painful, exquisitely tender","Painless, nontender, normal sized or mildly enlarged","Rock-hard, fixed, painless"],["ESR","Normal","Markedly raised","Normal or mildly raised","Normal or mildly raised"],["Uptake if thyrotoxic","Low (hashitoxicosis)","Low","Low","Not thyrotoxic"],["Long-term function","Permanent hypothyroidism","Usually recovers","Usually recovers; may recur","Euthyroid or hypothyroid"],["Antibodies","Anti-TPO in about 90%","Usually absent or low titer","Anti-TPO common","Anti-TPO in some"],["Histology","Germinal centers, atrophic follicles, Hürthle cells","Granulomas with giant cells around colloid","Lymphocytes; no granulomas, little fibrosis, few Hürthle cells","Dense fibrosis beyond the capsule, IgG4 plasma cells"]],"caption":"Pain and ESR separate subacute from painless thyroiditis; the capsule separates Riedel from fibrous Hashimoto; germinal centers with Hürthle cells mark Hashimoto."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Three months after delivery, a woman has palpitations, a suppressed TSH, high free T4, and a painless, nontender gland. What result separates postpartum thyroiditis from postpartum Graves disease?","explain":"TSH receptor stimulating antibodies: absent in postpartum thyroiditis, which leaks stored hormone, and present in Graves disease, which synthesizes new hormone. Uptake would be low versus high, but radioiodine is avoided during breastfeeding, so the antibody test usually decides. Pain and ESR do not help, because both disorders are painless.","check":"The comparison table and the leaking-versus-making distinction in EN-12-C.3."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Histology and physiology explain the laboratory patterns. The follicle stores weeks of hormone in colloid, and iodide trapping depends on TSH, so any process that ruptures follicles produces thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH and a low uptake, whatever the cause of the inflammation. Whether function recovers depends on whether follicular cells survive: they regenerate after a passing viral or postpartum injury, but not under the continuous autoimmune attack of Hashimoto disease or after replacement by fibrosis in Riedel disease. Immunology supplies the triggers. Hashimoto thyroiditis is T cell mediated autoimmunity with marker antibodies, Graves disease is antibody mediated stimulation, postpartum thyroiditis reflects immune rebound after pregnancy, subacute thyroiditis links a viral infection to an HLA-restricted T cell response, and Riedel thyroiditis belongs to the IgG4-related fibroinflammatory diseases."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology sorts these diseases by inflammatory pattern: lymphocytic with germinal centers (Hashimoto, painless thyroiditis), granulomatous around colloid (subacute), and fibrosing beyond the capsule (Riedel). Chronic antigen stimulation of acquired lymphoid tissue explains why Hashimoto thyroiditis predisposes to lymphoma, as Sjögren syndrome does in salivary glands and Helicobacter pylori gastritis does in the stomach. These categories overlap at the edges: some patients with Hashimoto lack detectable antibodies, and painless thyroiditis lies on a spectrum with Hashimoto."}],"summary":["Hashimoto thyroiditis is T cell mediated destruction of follicular cells after loss of tolerance to TPO and thyroglobulin; anti-TPO antibodies are markers present in about 90%.","Hashimoto histology shows lymphoid follicles with germinal centers, atrophic follicles, and mitochondria-rich Hürthle cells; the goiter is painless, firm, and diffuse.","Hashimoto may begin with brief hashitoxicosis (low uptake), then moves through subclinical to permanent overt hypothyroidism because the attack never stops.","Hashimoto clusters with other autoimmune diseases and raises the risk of primary thyroid B cell lymphoma, which presents as rapid painless enlargement.","Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis follows a viral illness and causes a painful, tender gland, fever, and a markedly raised ESR, with granulomas and giant cells around colloid.","Destructive thyroiditis leaks stored hormone: thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH, high thyroglobulin, and low radioiodine uptake, unlike the high uptake of Graves disease.","Subacute thyroiditis is self-limited: thyrotoxic for weeks, often transiently hypothyroid, then normal in most patients as follicles regenerate.","Painless and postpartum thyroiditis follow the same course with low uptake but are autoimmune, nontender, and anti-TPO associated; postpartum disease recurs with later pregnancies.","Riedel thyroiditis is IgG4-related fibrosis extending beyond the capsule, producing a rock-hard fixed gland that compresses neck structures and mimics anaplastic carcinoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, section on thyroiditis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic Tolerance and Autoimmunity"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Endocrine Glands, thyroid gland"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Thyroid Disease"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Thyroid Gland"}]},"EN-2":{"unit":"EN-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01","title":"Endocrine Histology and Derivations","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic structure of the thyroid, adrenal, pituitary, parathyroid, and endocrine pancreas, the hormone each cell type makes, and the germ layer each cell type comes from. Histology is a direct readout of physiology: cell height, lipid content, granule staining, and blood flow all reflect what a cell synthesizes and how hard it is being driven. Board items usually describe a slide or a tumor and ask for the hormone, the controlling signal, the disease, or the immunostain. If you can explain why a stimulated thyroid cell grows tall, why the fasciculata looks foamy, why basophils stain blue, and why a pheochromocytoma and a carcinoid tumor share stains, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Germ layers, pharyngeal pouches, and neural crest migration (EN-1)","Hormone classes (peptide, glycoprotein, steroid, amine) and how each is stored and secreted","Basic stains: hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), periodic acid-Schiff (PAS), and immunohistochemistry","Hypothalamic-pituitary axes and negative feedback","G protein signaling through Gs, Gq, and cAMP"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine embryology (EN-1)","how":"The pouch, foregut, Rathke pouch, and neural crest origins described there explain the derivations grouped in C05."},{"to":"Endocrine organ function and control (EN-5)","how":"The controlling signals named here (TSH, ACTH, angiotensin II, ionized Ca2+, glucose) are the regulators whose feedback loops EN-5 develops."},{"to":"Hormone synthesis and degradation (EN-8)","how":"Enzyme expression in each cell, such as aldosterone synthase in the glomerulosa or PNMT in chromaffin cells, decides the hormone that cell can make."},{"to":"Immune responses in endocrine disease (EN-10)","how":"Graves disease, Hashimoto thyroiditis, and type 1 diabetes each leave a characteristic histologic pattern produced by autoantibodies or T cells."},{"to":"Endocrine neoplasms (EN-12-G)","how":"Cell of origin and neuroendocrine markers identify medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, pituitary adenomas, and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors."}],"terms":[{"term":"Colloid","def":"The protein-rich material filling the thyroid follicle lumen, consisting mainly of iodinated thyroglobulin."},{"term":"Parafollicular C cell","def":"A thyroid cell outside the follicle lumen that secretes calcitonin in response to rising plasma Ca2+."},{"term":"Spongiocyte","def":"A zona fasciculata cell whose cytoplasm looks pale and foamy because lipid droplets of cholesteryl ester are dissolved during routine processing."},{"term":"Chromaffin cell","def":"An adrenal medullary catecholamine-secreting cell, a modified postganglionic sympathetic neuron without an axon, named for the brown color its granules turn with chromium salts."},{"term":"Acidophil and basophil","def":"Anterior pituitary cells whose secretory granules stain with acidic dyes such as eosin (GH, prolactin) or with basic dyes and PAS (FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH)."},{"term":"Chromophobe","def":"An anterior pituitary cell with few stainable granules, such as a degranulated secretory cell or a nonsecretory support or progenitor cell."},{"term":"Herring body","def":"A swollen segment or terminal of a hypothalamic axon in the posterior pituitary, packed with granules of ADH or oxytocin."},{"term":"Pituicyte","def":"A glial cell of the posterior pituitary that supports the neurosecretory axons and secretes no hormone."},{"term":"Oxyphil cell","def":"A large parathyroid cell with deeply eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria and no established secretory function."},{"term":"Neuroendocrine cell","def":"A cell that stores amine or peptide hormones in dense-core granules and expresses neuron-like secretory proteins such as chromogranin A and synaptophysin."},{"term":"Chromogranin A","def":"A protein of the dense-core granule matrix that is secreted with the hormone and serves as an immunostain and serum marker of neuroendocrine cells."},{"term":"Synaptophysin","def":"A membrane glycoprotein of small synaptic-like vesicles found in neurons and neuroendocrine cells."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01-C01","obj":"EN-2.1","title":"Thyroid follicles, C cells, and the stimulated gland","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid is built from follicles: spheres lined by a single layer of follicular cells resting on a basement membrane and surrounding colloid, which is stored thyroglobulin. A dense network of fenestrated capillaries surrounds each follicle. The thyroid is the only endocrine gland that stores large amounts of hormone outside its cells, enough for about 2 to 3 months of normal secretion."},{"type":"p","text":"Follicular cells are polarized. The basal surface faces the capillaries and carries the TSH receptor and the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS); the apical surface bears microvilli facing the colloid and carries pendrin and thyroid peroxidase (TPO). Thyroglobulin is made on rough endoplasmic reticulum and exocytosed into the lumen, iodinated at the apical surface, and later taken back into the cell for processing, so synthesis runs outward and release runs inward."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thyroid hormone synthesis and release in the follicle","steps":["NIS cotransports iodide with Na+ across the basal membrane","Pendrin moves iodide across the apical membrane into colloid","TPO oxidizes iodide and iodinates thyroglobulin tyrosines (MIT, DIT)","TPO couples DIT plus DIT to T4, MIT plus DIT to T3","TSH drives apical endocytosis of iodinated colloid","Lysosomes cleave thyroglobulin; T4 and T3 enter capillaries"],"caption":"Iodination happens in the colloid at the apical surface; hormone release requires the colloid to be taken back into the cell."},{"type":"p","text":"TSH acts mainly through Gs and cAMP (and at higher concentrations through Gq) to speed every step above, and it is also trophic, causing follicular cell hypertrophy and hyperplasia. Cell height and colloid volume therefore show how hard the gland is being driven. A resting gland has large follicles lined by flat to cuboidal cells and filled with dense colloid. A stimulated gland has small follicles lined by tall columnar cells, with scant pale colloid whose edge looks scalloped where it is being resorbed."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading follicular activity","cols":["Feature","Resting or suppressed","Stimulated (TSH or TSH receptor antibody)"],"rows":[["Follicular cell shape","Flat to cuboidal","Tall columnar, crowded"],["Follicle size","Large","Small, sometimes with infoldings"],["Colloid","Abundant, dense","Scant, pale"],["Colloid edge","Smooth","Scalloped from active resorption"],["Example","Iodine-replete gland with suppressed TSH","Graves disease, TSH-secreting adenoma"]],"caption":"Histology shows the degree of stimulation, not its source; serum TSH distinguishes TSH-driven from antibody-driven stimulation."},{"type":"p","text":"In Graves disease, IgG autoantibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins) mimic TSH but escape feedback. The gland enlarges diffusely, with tall columnar cells crowded into small papillae that project into the lumen; unlike papillary carcinoma, these papillae lack fibrovascular cores. Colloid is pale and scalloped, and lymphoid infiltrates, mainly T cells and sometimes with germinal centers, lie between follicles. The contrast with Hashimoto thyroiditis is useful: there, follicles are small and atrophic, lined by eosinophilic Hürthle cells, within dense lymphoid tissue with germinal centers."},{"type":"p","text":"Parafollicular C cells are larger, paler cells that sit inside the follicular basement membrane without touching the colloid, or in small clusters between follicles. They are concentrated in the middle to upper parts of the lateral lobes, where the ultimobranchial body fused with the thyroid. They secrete calcitonin when plasma Ca2+ rises, which inhibits osteoclasts. They are hard to see on H&E and are identified with a calcitonin immunostain; they are the cell of origin of medullary thyroid carcinoma (C05)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A thyroid biopsy shows tall columnar follicular cells and scant scalloped colloid, but serum TSH is undetectable. What is driving the gland?","explain":"A stimulator other than TSH. Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins activate the TSH receptor, raise T4 and T3, and suppress pituitary TSH through feedback, yet the gland keeps the stimulated appearance because the antibody is not regulated.","check":"The follicular activity table and the Graves disease paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01-C02","obj":"EN-2.2","title":"Adrenal cortex zones and the medulla","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal gland has a capsule, a cortex making up about 80 to 90% of its mass, and a central medulla. Blood from capsular arteries flows inward through sinusoids between cortical cords and drains into medullary veins, so the medulla is bathed in cortisol-rich blood. Cortical cells have the steroid-secreting phenotype: abundant smooth endoplasmic reticulum, mitochondria with tubular cristae, and lipid droplets of cholesteryl ester. Steroids diffuse out as they are made rather than being stored in granules, so cortical histology reflects synthetic capacity."},{"type":"table","title":"Adrenal layers from capsule to center","cols":["Layer","Histology","Key enzyme feature","Main product","Main control"],"rows":[["Zona glomerulosa (about 15% of cortex)","Thin subcapsular layer; cells in rounded clusters and arches; few lipid droplets","Aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2); no 17-alpha-hydroxylase","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II and plasma K+; ACTH minor"],["Zona fasciculata (about 75%)","Thickest layer; long straight cords one to two cells wide between sinusoids; pale foamy spongiocytes","17-alpha-hydroxylase and 11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1)","Cortisol","ACTH"],["Zona reticularis (about 10%)","Branching cords of smaller, more eosinophilic cells, often with lipofuscin","17,20-lyase activity of CYP17 high","DHEA, DHEA sulfate, androstenedione; some cortisol","ACTH"],["Medulla","Clusters of large, finely granular basophilic chromaffin cells around capillaries and venules; scattered ganglion cells","PNMT in most cells","Epinephrine (about 80%) and norepinephrine","Sympathetic preganglionic fibers (acetylcholine on nicotinic receptors)"]],"caption":"Each zone makes a different steroid because each expresses a different set of enzymes and receptors."},{"type":"p","text":"Zonation is enzymatic. Without 17-alpha-hydroxylase, the glomerulosa channels pregnenolone toward progesterone, deoxycorticosterone, and corticosterone, and only it has aldosterone synthase to finish aldosterone. The fasciculata and reticularis express 17-alpha-hydroxylase, so they make cortisol and androgens, and they depend on ACTH, acting on the melanocortin 2 receptor, for both secretion and growth."},{"type":"p","text":"This trophic dependence explains adrenal pathology. ACTH excess from a pituitary adenoma or ectopic source causes bilateral hyperplasia of the fasciculata and reticularis. A cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma suppresses ACTH, so the inner cortex of the adjacent and opposite gland atrophies, and long-term exogenous glucocorticoid does the same to both glands. The glomerulosa is spared in both cases because the renin-angiotensin system controls it, which is why secondary adrenal insufficiency lacks the salt wasting and hyperkalemia of primary adrenal destruction, where all zones are lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the medulla makes mostly epinephrine","steps":["Capsular arteries feed sinusoids running from cortex toward medulla","Sinusoidal blood collects high cortisol from the fasciculata","Cortisol induces PNMT in chromaffin cells","PNMT methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine","Preganglionic acetylcholine activates nicotinic receptors on chromaffin cells","Granules release mostly epinephrine into medullary veins"],"caption":"Extra-adrenal paraganglia lack this cortisol-rich blood, so paragangliomas usually secrete norepinephrine rather than epinephrine."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the adrenals in Cushing syndrome","text":"Two patients have cortisol excess. In patient A both adrenals are enlarged, with thick fasciculata and reticularis. In patient B one adrenal contains a yellow cortical nodule, and the remaining cortex of that gland and the opposite gland is thin. Start with ACTH. Both inner zones grow only under ACTH, so bilateral enlargement in A means ACTH is high, from a pituitary corticotroph adenoma or an ectopic source such as small cell lung carcinoma. In B the nodule makes cortisol on its own, feedback suppresses ACTH, and the ACTH-dependent cortex elsewhere atrophies. The nodule is yellow for the same reason the fasciculata is pale: its cells are full of cholesteryl ester. In both patients the glomerulosa looks normal because it answers to angiotensin II, not ACTH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01-C03","obj":"EN-2.3","title":"Anterior pituitary cells and the posterior pituitary","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pituitary has two parts with different origins and different histology. The adenohypophysis (pars distalis, pars intermedia, and pars tuberalis) is glandular epithelium from Rathke pouch. Its cells form cords and clusters among fenestrated sinusoidal capillaries of the hypophyseal portal system, which bring hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones from the median eminence. The neurohypophysis (pars nervosa and infundibular stalk) is an extension of the hypothalamus and looks like nervous tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"On H&E, anterior pituitary cells are grouped by granule staining, which reflects the chemistry of the stored hormone. GH and prolactin are nonglycosylated polypeptides that bind acidic dyes, so somatotrophs and lactotrophs are acidophils. TSH, FSH, and LH are glycoproteins, and ACTH is cleaved from proopiomelanocortin (POMC), a glycosylated precursor, so their cells bind basic dyes and are PAS positive; the familiar B-FLAT grouping (basophils: FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH) captures this. Chromophobes, roughly half the cells, have few stainable granules and include degranulated secretory cells and nonsecretory folliculostellate cells. Modern classification identifies each cell by immunostaining for its hormone."},{"type":"table","title":"Anterior pituitary cell types","cols":["Cell","Hormone","H&E staining","Approximate share of secretory cells"],"rows":[["Somatotroph","GH","Acidophil","About 50%"],["Lactotroph","Prolactin","Acidophil","About 15 to 25%; increases in pregnancy"],["Corticotroph","ACTH (from POMC)","Basophil, PAS positive","About 15 to 20%"],["Gonadotroph","FSH, LH","Basophil, PAS positive","About 10%"],["Thyrotroph","TSH","Basophil, PAS positive","About 5%"]],"caption":"Acidophils make the simple protein hormones; basophils make the glycoprotein hormones and ACTH."},{"type":"p","text":"Two staining changes have mechanistic explanations. Estrogen in pregnancy drives lactotroph hyperplasia that roughly doubles pituitary size, which helps explain the gland's vulnerability to ischemic necrosis after obstetric hemorrhage. Sustained glucocorticoid excess causes Crooke hyaline change, in which suppressed corticotrophs accumulate a pale, homogeneous ring of keratin filaments in place of their granules."},{"type":"p","text":"The posterior pituitary contains no hormone-synthesizing cells. It consists of unmyelinated axons of magnocellular neurons from the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, glial pituicytes, and fenestrated capillaries. ADH (made mainly in supraoptic neurons) and oxytocin (mainly paraventricular) are synthesized in the cell bodies, packaged with their carrier proteins, the neurophysins, and transported down the axons. Herring bodies are eosinophilic swellings of those axons and terminals filled with neurosecretory granules."},{"type":"flow","title":"ADH from hypothalamus to blood","steps":["Supraoptic neurons synthesize ADH with neurophysin II","Granules travel down axons through the infundibular stalk","Granules are stored in terminals and Herring bodies","Rising osmolality fires action potentials; Ca2+ enters terminals","Exocytosis releases ADH into fenestrated capillaries"],"caption":"The posterior lobe is a storage and release site; the hormone is made in the hypothalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the hormone is made above the lobe, the level of injury predicts the outcome. Loss of the posterior lobe alone often causes only transient central diabetes insipidus, since the cut axons in the stalk and median eminence can reorganize and release ADH there. Destruction of the hypothalamic nuclei or a high stalk section that kills most magnocellular neurons causes permanent deficiency."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor confined to the posterior lobe destroys it completely. Will ADH deficiency be permanent?","explain":"Usually not. ADH-producing cell bodies in the hypothalamus survive, and their axons can release ADH from the stalk and median eminence, so diabetes insipidus is often transient. Permanent deficiency requires loss of most supraoptic and paraventricular neurons.","check":"The posterior pituitary paragraphs and the ADH flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01-C04","obj":"EN-2.4","title":"Parathyroid chief and oxyphil cells and the islet cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each parathyroid gland is a small, encapsulated mass of cell cords and clusters wrapped around a rich capillary network, with fat cells in the stroma that become more numerous with age. Chief cells are the most numerous: small polygonal cells with a central round nucleus and pale to lightly eosinophilic cytoplasm containing glycogen, lipid, and small secretory granules of PTH. Oxyphil cells are larger, with a smaller dark nucleus and deeply eosinophilic, granular cytoplasm packed with mitochondria. They appear in later childhood, increase with age, and have no established secretory role; they may be altered chief cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Chief cells need no pituitary trophic hormone. They sense extracellular ionized Ca2+ directly through the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), a G protein-coupled receptor. Activation by rising Ca2+ inhibits PTH release within seconds, reduces PTH gene expression, and over weeks restrains chief cell proliferation; calcitriol acting on the vitamin D receptor also suppresses PTH gene transcription. Chief cell mass therefore tracks calcium demand. Chronic hypocalcemia, as in chronic kidney disease or vitamin D deficiency, drives hyperplasia of all four glands (secondary hyperparathyroidism), whereas a single autonomous adenoma raises Ca2+ and leaves the other glands normal or suppressed."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Calcium-sensing feedback on the chief cell","steps":["Ionized Ca2+ falls","Less CaSR activation on chief cells","PTH secretion rises","Bone resorption, renal Ca2+ reabsorption, and calcitriol synthesis increase","Ionized Ca2+ rises and CaSR activation restrains PTH"],"caption":"The same receptor sets both minute-to-minute secretion and long-term chief cell growth."},{"type":"p","text":"The islets of Langerhans, about one million in a human pancreas and about 1 to 2% of its mass, appear as pale, richly vascular clusters among darker basophilic acini whose color comes from the rough endoplasmic reticulum of enzyme-secreting cells. Islet cell types look alike on H&E and are identified by immunostaining for their hormones; by electron microscopy beta cell granules have a crystalline core of zinc-insulin surrounded by a clear halo. In rodents beta cells form a central core with a mantle of other cells, but human islets are more intermingled, with cells arranged along capillaries."},{"type":"table","title":"Parathyroid and islet endocrine cells","cols":["Cell","Appearance or share","Product","Main regulator"],"rows":[["Parathyroid chief cell","Small, pale to lightly eosinophilic; most numerous","PTH","Low ionized Ca2+ through the CaSR"],["Parathyroid oxyphil cell","Large, deeply eosinophilic, mitochondria rich; increases with age","No established hormone","Not known"],["Beta cell","Most numerous islet cell, about 60 to 70%","Insulin, with amylin (IAPP)","Rising glucose"],["Alpha cell","About 20%","Glucagon","Falling glucose and rising amino acids; restrained by insulin and somatostatin"],["Delta cell","About 5 to 10%","Somatostatin","Rising glucose and amino acids; inhibits alpha and beta cells locally"],["PP cell","Few; most common in islets of the posterior head (ventral bud)","Pancreatic polypeptide","Meals and vagal input"]],"caption":"Chief cells answer directly to calcium; islet cells answer to glucose and to each other."},{"type":"p","text":"Islet cells also regulate one another through local (paracrine) signals: insulin and zinc released with it restrain alpha cells, and somatostatin inhibits both alpha and beta cells. Islet histology records disease. In type 1 diabetes, T cell infiltration of islets (insulitis) destroys beta cells, leaving small islets composed of alpha, delta, and PP cells. In type 2 diabetes, amylin co-secreted with insulin can deposit as islet amyloid. Each cell type can also give rise to a functioning tumor, most often an insulinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-02-01-C05","obj":"EN-2.5","title":"Derivation of endocrine cells and neuroendocrine staining","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each endocrine cell type comes from a specific germ layer, and that origin predicts which cells share tumor types, inherited syndromes, and immunostains. The pattern is easiest to remember by tissue type: epithelial glands that bud from the gut tube are endoderm, the two cell types that migrate into other organs to secrete amines or calcitonin are neural crest, the pituitary is ectoderm from two sources, and the steroid-making adrenal cortex is mesoderm."},{"type":"table","title":"Germ layer origin of endocrine cells","cols":["Cell or tissue","Germ layer","Embryonic source"],"rows":[["Thyroid follicular cells","Endoderm","Thyroid diverticulum from the pharyngeal floor at the foramen cecum"],["Thyroid C cells","Neural crest (standard teaching)","Ultimobranchial body from the fourth pouch, which fuses with the thyroid"],["Inferior parathyroids","Endoderm","Third pharyngeal pouch, with the thymus"],["Superior parathyroids","Endoderm","Fourth pharyngeal pouch"],["Pancreatic islet cells","Endoderm","Dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds of the foregut"],["Adenohypophysis","Ectoderm (oral)","Rathke pouch from the roof of the stomodeum"],["Neurohypophysis","Ectoderm (neuroectoderm)","Infundibulum of the diencephalon"],["Adrenal cortex","Mesoderm","Coelomic epithelium near the urogenital ridge"],["Adrenal chromaffin cells","Neural crest","Sympathoadrenal cells that invade the fetal cortex"]],"caption":"Only the chromaffin cells and C cells of these glands come from neural crest."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroendocrine cells are defined by a secretory program shared with neurons, not by origin. They store amine or peptide hormones in dense-core granules whose matrix contains chromogranin A, and they carry small synaptic-like vesicles whose membrane contains synaptophysin. Chromaffin cells, C cells, and paraganglia are neural crest; islet cells, gut enteroendocrine cells, and pulmonary neuroendocrine cells are endoderm. An older view grouped all of these as neural crest derivatives, but lineage studies showed that gut and islet neuroendocrine cells arise from endoderm. A positive chromogranin or synaptophysin stain therefore shows neuroendocrine differentiation, and the specific hormone or the site tells you the cell of origin."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumors that stain for chromogranin and synaptophysin include pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma (nests of chromaffin cells rimmed by S100-positive sustentacular cells), medullary thyroid carcinoma (calcitonin positive, often with amyloid formed from calcitonin), neuroblastoma, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors, well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors of gut and lung, and small cell lung carcinoma, which has few dense-core granules and so often stains more consistently for synaptophysin than for chromogranin. Tumors of follicular cells (thyroglobulin positive) and of adrenal cortex (steroidogenic, not neuroendocrine) are chromogranin negative, although adrenocortical tumors can stain for synaptophysin."},{"type":"p","text":"Origin predicts much, though not all, of the inherited syndromes. In multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN2), an activating germline RET mutation affects neural crest derivatives and causes medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma; MEN2A also includes parathyroid hyperplasia even though the parathyroids are endodermal. MEN1, from loss of the tumor suppressor menin, involves parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and pituitary cells, which come from two different germ layers."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: naming a tumor from its stains","text":"A thyroid nodule shows nests of polygonal and spindle cells in a stroma containing amyloid. The cells stain for chromogranin, synaptophysin, and calcitonin, and are negative for thyroglobulin. Chromogranin and synaptophysin establish a neuroendocrine tumor but not its origin. Calcitonin identifies the C cell, and absent thyroglobulin excludes a follicular cell tumor, so this is medullary thyroid carcinoma. The amyloid is built from calcitonin, and serum calcitonin is a marker of tumor burden. Because C cells and chromaffin cells are both neural crest derivatives targeted by RET mutations, the finding raises the question of MEN2 and an associated pheochromocytoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A pancreatic tumor stains strongly for chromogranin A and synaptophysin. Does that show it arose from neural crest?","explain":"No. Islet cells are endoderm, yet they use the same dense-core granule and synaptic-like vesicle proteins as neural crest cells. The stains show neuroendocrine differentiation; the hormone made (insulin, glucagon, gastrin) and the location identify the cell of origin.","check":"The derivation table and the neuroendocrine paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Histology, biochemistry, and physiology describe the same cells. A cell's appearance follows from what it makes: steroid cells are full of smooth endoplasmic reticulum and lipid because they synthesize hormone on demand from cholesterol, peptide and amine cells are full of granules because they store hormone for regulated release, and the thyroid stores its hormone as colloid outside the cells. The regulator that drives each cell is also its growth signal, so TSH, ACTH, and low Ca2+ each enlarge their target tissue, and loss of the signal shrinks it."},{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and immunology add the rest. Germ layer origin groups cells into tumor families and inherited syndromes, and the shared neuroendocrine program explains why chromogranin and synaptophysin mark tumors from both neural crest and endoderm. Autoimmunity leaves distinct patterns: stimulating antibodies make the thyroid look overactive in Graves disease, while T cell destruction leaves atrophic follicles in Hashimoto thyroiditis and alpha and delta cell islets in type 1 diabetes. The limits matter as well: H&E cannot identify most individual hormone-secreting cells, cell proportions vary with age, physiologic state, and source, and immunostaining for the hormone itself is the definitive identification."}],"summary":["Thyroid follicles store hormone as colloid; tall columnar cells with scant scalloped colloid mean strong TSH receptor stimulation, as in Graves disease, while flat cells with abundant colloid mean rest.","C cells lie outside the follicle lumen, secrete calcitonin, come from the ultimobranchial body, and give rise to medullary thyroid carcinoma.","Adrenal glomerulosa, fasciculata, and reticularis make aldosterone, cortisol, and androgens because of their enzyme sets; ACTH drives the inner two zones, angiotensin II and K+ the glomerulosa.","Cortisol-rich sinusoidal blood induces PNMT, so the adrenal medulla secretes mostly epinephrine, while extra-adrenal paragangliomas usually secrete norepinephrine.","Acidophils make GH and prolactin; PAS-positive basophils make FSH, LH, ACTH, and TSH; chromophobes have few granules.","The posterior pituitary holds axons of supraoptic and paraventricular neurons, pituicytes, and Herring bodies; it stores ADH and oxytocin but makes neither.","Parathyroid chief cells secrete PTH under direct CaSR control; oxyphil cells are large mitochondria-rich cells with no established hormone.","Islet beta cells (insulin) are most numerous, alpha cells make glucagon, delta cells make somatostatin, and type 1 diabetes leaves islets of alpha and delta cells.","Chromaffin cells and C cells are neural crest; follicular cells, parathyroids, and islets are endoderm; the adenohypophysis is oral ectoderm; the adrenal cortex is mesoderm.","Chromogranin and synaptophysin mark neuroendocrine differentiation from either neural crest or endoderm; the hormone and site identify the cell of origin."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Endocrine Glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (pharyngeal pouches and thyroid); Central Nervous System (hypophysis)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones; Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine system sections on the adrenal gland and the parathyroid glands"}]},"EN-3":{"unit":"EN-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01","title":"Location and Relations of the Endocrine Glands","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the thyroid, parathyroid, pituitary, and adrenal glands in the body and explains what lies around each one. The neighbors matter more than the glands themselves on the exam: a question describes hoarseness or a lost high note after thyroid surgery, tingling and cramps the next day, loss of both temporal visual fields, or a difficult right adrenal dissection, and asks you to name the structure involved. If you can explain why the thyroid rises on swallowing, which nerve controls which laryngeal muscle, why the inferior parathyroids wander, what surrounds the sella, and how the two adrenals differ, you can reason through these questions instead of memorizing them.","prerequisites":["Layers of the deep cervical fascia and the visceral compartment of the neck","Pharyngeal pouch derivatives and descent of the thyroid and thymus (EN-1)","Branches of the vagus nerve and the intrinsic muscles of the larynx","The visual pathway from retina to optic tract","Retroperitoneal organs, the kidneys, and the renal fascia"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine embryology (EN-1)","how":"Third and fourth pouch migration explains why the inferior parathyroids are variable and the superior ones are constant."},{"to":"Endocrine blood supply (EN-4)","how":"The superior and inferior thyroid arteries are the landmarks for the two laryngeal nerves, and the short right adrenal vein shapes adrenal surgery."},{"to":"Endocrine organ function and control (EN-5)","how":"Parathyroid hormone and the hypothalamic-pituitary portal system explain the hypocalcemia and stalk effect described here."},{"to":"Endocrine neoplasms (EN-12-G)","how":"Pituitary adenomas, parathyroid adenomas, and adrenal masses produce mass effects predicted by the relations in this chapter."},{"to":"Neuroscience (NE units)","how":"The optic chiasm, cavernous sinus cranial nerves, and vagal branches to the larynx link this chapter to the visual pathway and cranial nerve anatomy."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pretracheal fascia","def":"The visceral layer of deep cervical fascia that encloses the thyroid, trachea, and esophagus and binds the thyroid to the larynx and trachea."},{"term":"Isthmus","def":"The narrow band of thyroid tissue that joins the two lobes across the front of the second and third tracheal rings."},{"term":"Pyramidal lobe","def":"An inconstant upward extension of thyroid tissue from the isthmus, a remnant of the thyroglossal duct."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal groove","def":"The furrow between the trachea and esophagus in which the recurrent laryngeal nerve ascends behind the thyroid lobe."},{"term":"Cricothyroid","def":"The only intrinsic laryngeal muscle supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve; it tilts the thyroid cartilage to lengthen and tense the vocal folds."},{"term":"Posterior cricoarytenoid","def":"The only muscle that abducts the vocal folds and opens the glottis; it is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve."},{"term":"Sella turcica","def":"The saddle-shaped depression on the upper surface of the sphenoid body whose central hypophyseal fossa holds the pituitary."},{"term":"Diaphragma sellae","def":"A circular fold of dura that roofs the sella turcica, with a central opening for the pituitary stalk."},{"term":"Cavernous sinus","def":"A paired dural venous sinus beside the sella containing the internal carotid artery and the abducens nerve, with cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and V2 in its lateral wall."},{"term":"Bitemporal hemianopia","def":"Loss of the temporal half of the visual field in both eyes, caused by damage to crossing nasal retinal fibers at the optic chiasm."},{"term":"Renal fascia","def":"The fibroareolar sheath that encloses each kidney, its perirenal fat, and the adrenal gland, with a thin septum between adrenal and kidney."},{"term":"Retroperitoneal","def":"Lying behind the parietal peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, covered by peritoneum only on the front."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01-C01","obj":"EN-3.1","title":"The thyroid: position, fascial sheath, and movement on swallowing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid is the largest purely endocrine gland, weighing about 15 to 25 g in adults. Its two lobes lie on either side of the lower larynx and upper trachea, each extending from about the level of the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage down to about the sixth tracheal ring. The isthmus joins the lobes across the front of the trachea, usually anterior to the second and third tracheal rings. In roughly half of people a pyramidal lobe rises from the isthmus toward the hyoid, marking the path of the thyroglossal duct along which the gland descended (EN-1)."},{"type":"p","text":"The gland has its own thin fibrous capsule, which sends septa into the parenchyma. Outside this lies a looser sheath formed by the pretracheal layer of deep cervical fascia, the visceral layer that also wraps the trachea and esophagus. Posteromedially the sheath thickens into a suspensory ligament that anchors each lobe to the cricoid cartilage and upper tracheal rings."},{"type":"table","title":"Relations of the thyroid","cols":["Direction","Structures","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Anterior","Infrahyoid (strap) muscles, investing fascia, platysma, skin","Sternothyroid attaches to the oblique line and stops a goiter rising, so enlargement extends down behind the sternum"],["Lateral","Carotid sheath: common carotid artery, internal jugular vein, vagus nerve","A large lobe displaces the sheath laterally"],["Medial","Larynx, trachea, pharynx, esophagus","A goiter can compress or deviate the trachea (stridor) or esophagus (dysphagia)"],["Posterior","Recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove; parathyroid glands","Nerve and parathyroids are at risk in thyroid surgery (C02, C03)"]],"caption":"The thyroid sits in the visceral compartment of the neck, enclosed with the airway and gut tube."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a thyroid mass rises on swallowing","steps":["Pharyngeal phase of swallowing begins","Suprahyoid and thyrohyoid muscles elevate hyoid and larynx","Pretracheal fascia and suspensory ligament tie thyroid to larynx","Thyroid and any mass within it move upward","Gland descends again as the larynx returns"],"caption":"Movement on swallowing places a mass in the thyroid or in a structure fixed to the larynx."},{"type":"p","text":"This makes swallowing a bedside localizing test. A thyroid nodule or goiter rises when the patient swallows, whereas an enlarged lymph node in the carotid sheath or the lateral neck usually does not, because it lies outside the pretracheal fascia. A thyroglossal duct cyst is also tethered to the larynx through the hyoid and so rises on swallowing, but it is usually midline near the hyoid and, unlike a thyroid nodule, also rises when the tongue is protruded because of its tract toward the foramen cecum. A thyroid cancer that has invaded surrounding muscle or the trachea may become fixed and lose this mobility."},{"type":"p","text":"The same anatomy explains several other findings. Because the sternothyroid muscles cap the gland above, a large goiter grows downward into the superior mediastinum as a retrosternal goiter, where it can compress the trachea and great veins. The isthmus lies over the upper tracheal rings, so it is encountered in a tracheostomy, and an inconstant thyroid ima artery, present in a small minority of people, may ascend on the front of the trachea to the isthmus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A firm lateral neck mass does not move when the patient swallows or protrudes the tongue. Is it likely to be thyroid?","explain":"Probably not. Thyroid tissue is bound to the larynx by the pretracheal fascia, so it rises with swallowing. A mass that does not move is more likely a lymph node or other structure outside that sheath, unless it is a thyroid cancer that has become fixed to surrounding tissue.","check":"The swallowing flow and the paragraph on bedside localization."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01-C02","obj":"EN-3.2","title":"The laryngeal nerves and voice changes after thyroid surgery","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Two branches of the vagus nerve pass close to the thyroid, and they divide the work of the larynx between them. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid, so it controls opening and closing of the glottis, and it carries sensation below the vocal folds. The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies only the cricothyroid, which tilts the thyroid cartilage forward on the cricoid, lengthening and tensing the vocal folds to raise pitch."},{"type":"p","text":"The right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the right subclavian artery; the left hooks under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum, giving it a longer thoracic course. Both ascend in or near the tracheoesophageal groove behind the thyroid lobe, cross close to the inferior thyroid artery (in front of, behind, or between its branches, which varies), and enter the larynx deep to the inferior constrictor near the cricothyroid joint, where the suspensory ligament binds them tightly to the gland. In under 1% of people the right nerve is non-recurrent, running directly from the vagus to the larynx, usually with an aberrant right subclavian artery."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior laryngeal nerve divides high in the neck. Its internal branch pierces the thyrohyoid membrane to carry sensation above the vocal folds; its external branch descends alongside the superior thyroid artery to the cricothyroid. Each nerve therefore travels with a thyroid artery, and ligating those vessels is where injury occurs."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerve injury during thyroidectomy","cols":["Nerve injured","Landmark at risk","Muscles lost","Result"],"rows":[["Recurrent laryngeal, one side","Inferior thyroid artery, suspensory ligament, tracheoesophageal groove","All intrinsic muscles on that side except cricothyroid","Fold fixed near the midline; hoarse, breathy, weak voice and weak cough; aspiration of liquids possible"],["Recurrent laryngeal, both sides","Both grooves","All abductors and adductors except cricothyroids","Both folds near the midline; inspiratory stridor and airway obstruction; voice may sound fairly normal"],["External branch of superior laryngeal","Superior thyroid artery at the upper pole","Cricothyroid","Weak, monotone voice that tires easily; loss of high pitch"],["Internal branch of superior laryngeal","Thyrohyoid membrane, above the operative field","None (sensory)","Loss of supraglottic sensation and cough reflex; aspiration"]],"caption":"The recurrent nerve controls glottic opening and closing; the external branch controls vocal fold tension."},{"type":"flow","title":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","steps":["Inferior thyroid artery branches ligated near the lower pole","Adjacent recurrent nerve cut, clamped, stretched, or heated","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid denervated","Fold cannot abduct or fully adduct; rests near midline","Unilateral: incomplete closure, hoarse breathy voice","Bilateral: glottis cannot open, stridor and obstruction"],"caption":"Stretch or heat often causes temporary injury; transection causes permanent paralysis."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor, the two injuries differ. With one nerve lost, the paralyzed fold rests near the midline and the opposite fold still opens the airway, but the glottis cannot close firmly, so the voice is hoarse and breathy. With both nerves lost, neither fold can open: the voice may be acceptable while the airway is dangerously narrow, typically recognized as stridor after extubation. Thyroid cancer, mediastinal tumors, an enlarged left atrium, or an aortic arch aneurysm cause the same hoarseness, more often on the left because of its longer course."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the nerve from the voice","text":"A singer has a total thyroidectomy. Afterward she speaks clearly and breathes normally, but cannot reach high notes and her voice tires by evening. Normal breathing and loudness mean the recurrent nerves are intact. Pitch depends on vocal fold tension from the cricothyroid, so the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve was injured, likely while the superior thyroid vessels were ligated. A second patient who is hoarse and breathy with a weak cough has one fold that cannot close: one recurrent nerve. A third with a near-normal voice but noisy inspiration after extubation has bilateral recurrent nerve injury and an airway emergency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01-C03","obj":"EN-3.3","title":"The parathyroid glands and their variable position","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most people have four parathyroid glands, each a flattened yellow-brown body about 5 to 6 mm long weighing about 30 to 50 mg. They usually lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, outside the thyroid capsule but within its fascial sheath, and are easily mistaken for fat, lymph nodes, or thyroid nodules. A minority of people have five or more, and a few have fewer than four. Both pairs are usually supplied mainly by the inferior thyroid artery (EN-4)."},{"type":"p","text":"Position is set by embryology, and the names involve a reversal. The inferior parathyroids arise from the third pharyngeal pouch with the thymus, which pulls them down as it migrates toward the mediastinum, so they end up below the glands from the fourth pouch. Because they can separate from the thymus anywhere along that path, they are the variable pair. The superior glands, from the fourth pouch, move only a short distance and stay fairly constant."},{"type":"table","title":"Superior versus inferior parathyroid glands","cols":["Feature","Superior glands","Inferior glands"],"rows":[["Origin","Fourth pharyngeal pouch","Third pharyngeal pouch, with the thymus"],["Usual position","Posterior thyroid lobe near the level of the cricoid cartilage, close to where the recurrent nerve enters the larynx","Near the lower pole of the thyroid lobe"],["Relation to recurrent nerve","Usually posterior (dorsal) to the nerve","Usually anterior (ventral) to the nerve"],["Variability","Fairly constant; occasionally behind the pharynx or esophagus","Highly variable: thyrothymic tissue, thymus, anterior mediastinum, carotid sheath, or high in the neck if undescended"]],"caption":"Longer migration with the thymus makes the inferior glands the ones most often ectopic."},{"type":"p","text":"Variable position matters in two opposite operations. In thyroidectomy the parathyroids and their small arteries must be preserved, or parathyroid hormone (PTH) secretion stops. In surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism an overactive gland, usually a single adenoma, must be found; an ectopic adenoma, most often an inferior gland in the thymus or anterior mediastinum, is easily missed, leaving PTH and calcium high. This is why the glands are localized by imaging, such as sestamibi scanning or ultrasound, beforehand."},{"type":"flow","title":"From thyroidectomy to hypocalcemia","steps":["Parathyroids removed, bruised, or devascularized","PTH secretion falls; circulating PTH clears within minutes","Less bone resorption and renal Ca2+ reabsorption","Less calcitriol, so less intestinal Ca2+ absorption","Plasma Ca2+ falls over one to three days","Neuromuscular excitability rises: tingling, spasm, tetany"],"caption":"Low PTH also lets renal phosphate reabsorption rise, so phosphate is high."},{"type":"p","text":"The symptoms follow from a membrane effect. Extracellular Ca2+ binds the outer membrane surface and stabilizes voltage-gated Na+ channels; when ionized Ca2+ falls, they activate at a membrane potential closer to rest, so nerves fire spontaneously. The result is perioral and fingertip tingling, cramps, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, and in severe cases seizures. Tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes facial muscles twitch (Chvostek sign), and a blood pressure cuff inflated above systolic pressure for a few minutes provokes carpal spasm (Trousseau sign). The ventricular action potential plateau lengthens, prolonging the QT interval. Normal total serum calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L); ionized calcium is about 4.5 to 5.3 mg/dL (1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After removal of what looked like a single enlarged inferior parathyroid, a patient's calcium stays high. Where should the missing adenoma be sought, and why there?","explain":"In the path of the third pouch: the thymus, the anterior mediastinum, the thyrothymic tissue, or the carotid sheath. The inferior glands migrate with the thymus and can stop anywhere along that route, so an abnormal gland may not be at the lower thyroid pole. A supernumerary gland is another possibility.","check":"The superior versus inferior table and the opening paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01-C04","obj":"EN-3.4","title":"The pituitary in the sella turcica and its neighbors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pituitary, about 1 cm across and about 0.5 to 0.6 g, hangs from the hypothalamus by the infundibulum (stalk). It sits in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica on the sphenoid body, between the tuberculum sellae in front and the dorsum sellae behind. The diaphragma sellae, a circular dural fold, roofs the fossa, with a central opening for the stalk. Stretching of this pain-sensitive dura is one reason a pituitary mass causes headache."},{"type":"table","title":"Neighbors of the pituitary and the effect of tumor extension","cols":["Direction","Structure","Effect of a growing mass"],"rows":[["Above","Optic chiasm, just above the diaphragma sellae and in front of the stalk","Bitemporal visual field loss, starting in the upper temporal quadrants"],["Above","Stalk, hypothalamus, third ventricle","Mild hyperprolactinemia from lost dopamine delivery (stalk effect); hydrocephalus if the mass is very large"],["Lateral","Cavernous sinus: internal carotid artery and CN VI within; CN III, IV, V1, V2 in the lateral wall","Double vision, ptosis, a dilated pupil, and numbness of the forehead or cheek"],["Below","Sphenoid sinus","Thin bony floor; the route of transsphenoidal surgery"],["Within the sella","Normal anterior and posterior pituitary","Compression causes loss of pituitary hormones (hypopituitarism)"]],"caption":"Bone forms the floor and front and back walls; the dural roof and side walls yield, so a tumor grows upward and sideways."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibers from the nasal half of each retina, which see the temporal visual field, cross in the chiasm; temporal retinal fibers do not. A mass pressing on the center of the chiasm damages only crossing fibers, so the temporal field is lost in both eyes: bitemporal hemianopia. Inferior nasal fibers, serving the upper temporal fields, cross in the lower chiasm, so a pituitary tumor pushing from below usually takes the upper temporal quadrants first. A craniopharyngioma, often arising above the sella from Rathke pouch remnants, tends to take the lower quadrants first. A lesion of one optic tract, behind the chiasm, causes homonymous hemianopia instead."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pituitary macroadenoma to bitemporal hemianopia","steps":["Adenoma of 10 mm or more fills the sella","Bony floor resists; dural roof and side walls yield","Mass rises above the sella","Optic chiasm compressed from below","Crossing inferior nasal fibers fail first","Upper temporal quadrants lost, then full bitemporal hemianopia"],"caption":"A microadenoma (under 10 mm) is usually too small to cause mass effect."},{"type":"p","text":"Lateral extension into the cavernous sinus compresses cranial nerves III, IV, and VI, causing diplopia, with ptosis and a dilated pupil when III is involved, and V1 and V2, causing facial numbness. The thick-walled internal carotid artery is often encased but rarely narrowed. Sudden hemorrhage or infarction into an adenoma (pituitary apoplexy) produces severe headache, visual loss, and ophthalmoplegia together. Diabetes insipidus is uncommon with adenomas, because vasopressin is made in the hypothalamus and can still be released above the compression; it suggests a hypothalamic or stalk lesion such as a craniopharyngioma."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the field defect","text":"A 45-year-old man bumps into door frames on both sides. Both eyes have lost their upper temporal quadrants, and his prolactin is mildly raised. Temporal loss in both eyes places the lesion at the chiasm, where crossing nasal fibers run. Upper quadrants first means compression from below, pointing to the pituitary rather than a mass above the chiasm. A mildly raised prolactin with a large mass suggests the stalk effect, a nonfunctioning adenoma interrupting dopamine flow in the portal vessels, rather than a prolactinoma, which usually raises prolactin far higher. Later diplopia and a drooping lid would mean lateral extension into the cavernous sinus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-03-01-C05","obj":"EN-3.5","title":"The adrenal glands: position and right versus left differences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adrenal (suprarenal) glands are retroperitoneal organs on the posterior abdominal wall, each weighing about 4 to 5 g in adults. They lie on the superomedial aspect of the kidneys at about the level of the eleventh and twelfth thoracic vertebrae, resting posteriorly on the crura of the diaphragm. Each gland is enclosed within the renal fascia and surrounded by perirenal fat, but a thin fascial septum separates it from the kidney. Each has an outer cortex, derived from mesoderm, and an inner medulla, derived from neural crest (EN-1, EN-2)."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the gland has its own fascial compartment and develops separately from the kidney, it stays in place when the kidney does not: a pelvic kidney leaves the adrenal in its normal position, and a kidney can be removed while the adrenal is left behind."},{"type":"table","title":"Right versus left adrenal gland","cols":["Feature","Right adrenal","Left adrenal"],"rows":[["Shape","Pyramidal","Crescent (semilunar)"],["Position on kidney","Caps the superior pole","Along the medial border from the superior pole toward the hilum"],["Anterior relations","Inferior vena cava medially; bare area of the liver laterally","Stomach across the omental bursa, body of the pancreas, splenic vessels"],["Posterior relation","Right crus of the diaphragm","Left crus of the diaphragm"],["Venous drainage","Very short vein directly into the posterior inferior vena cava","Longer vein into the left renal vein, often joined by the left inferior phrenic vein"]],"caption":"Neighbors explain both the shapes and the difference in surgical difficulty."},{"type":"p","text":"The asymmetry has practical consequences. The right adrenal lies behind the inferior vena cava, partly hidden by it and wedged against the liver, and its vein is only a few millimeters long before it enters the cava. Dissecting the right gland therefore risks tearing the caval wall, and catheterizing that short vein for adrenal venous sampling is technically harder on the right. The left gland is reached behind the stomach and pancreas, and its vein drains into the left renal vein, which crosses in front of the aorta; this longer route makes the left vein easier to identify and sample."},{"type":"p","text":"Each gland receives many small arteries from three sources, the inferior phrenic artery, the aorta, and the renal artery, but drains through a single vein (EN-4). Its close relation to the kidney, liver, and vena cava also explains why adrenal masses are often found incidentally on abdominal imaging done for other reasons, and why a large right adrenal mass can push the liver forward or invade the cava."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During removal of a right adrenal tumor, sudden heavy venous bleeding occurs. Which vessel is most likely injured, and why is the right side more vulnerable than the left?","explain":"The inferior vena cava, at the entry of the right adrenal vein. The right vein is very short and enters the posterior cava directly, so traction on the gland tears the caval wall. The left vein is longer and drains into the left renal vein, away from the cava.","check":"The right versus left table and the paragraph on asymmetry."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains most of these relations. The thyroid descends from the tongue base and stays bound to the airway by the pretracheal fascia, so it moves with the larynx. The long thymic migration of the third pouch makes the inferior parathyroids the variable pair. The recurrent laryngeal nerves first hook under the sixth arch arteries. On the right the distal sixth and fifth arch arteries regress, so the nerve rises to hook under the right subclavian artery, a fourth arch derivative; on the left the sixth arch persists as the ductus arteriosus, so the nerve stays under the arch at the ligamentum arteriosum. When the right subclavian arises aberrantly from the distal arch, nothing holds the right nerve down, and it is non-recurrent. The adrenal cortex and medulla form separately from the kidney, so the gland keeps its place when the kidney is displaced."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology then turn anatomy into presentations. Loss of the cricothyroid changes pitch, loss of the posterior cricoarytenoid threatens the airway, loss of the parathyroids removes PTH and lowers ionized calcium until nerves fire spontaneously, and compression of the chiasm or stalk produces field loss and mild hyperprolactinemia. These rules have limits: relations of the recurrent nerve to the inferior thyroid artery vary widely, parathyroid number and position are inconstant, and the chiasm can sit forward or backward over the sella, so a classic pattern supports localization but does not prove it."}],"summary":["The thyroid isthmus lies over tracheal rings 2 and 3; the lobes flank the larynx and trachea within the pretracheal fascia.","Fascial and ligamentous attachment to the larynx makes the thyroid rise on swallowing; a thyroglossal duct cyst also rises on tongue protrusion.","The recurrent laryngeal nerve ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove near the inferior thyroid artery and supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid.","Unilateral recurrent nerve injury causes hoarseness; bilateral injury leaves both folds near the midline with stridor, because the posterior cricoarytenoid is the only abductor.","The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve runs with the superior thyroid artery to the cricothyroid; injury causes a monotone voice and loss of high pitch.","Inferior parathyroids come from the third pouch and migrate with the thymus, so they are the variable pair; superior ones from the fourth pouch are constant.","Parathyroid loss at thyroidectomy lowers PTH and ionized calcium, raising neuromuscular excitability and prolonging the QT interval.","The pituitary sits in the sella beneath the diaphragma sellae, with the optic chiasm above, the cavernous sinuses lateral, and the sphenoid sinus below.","Upward pituitary extension compresses crossing nasal fibers, causing bitemporal hemianopia that begins in the upper temporal quadrants.","The right adrenal is pyramidal, behind the IVC, with a short vein into the cava; the left is crescentic on the medial kidney border and drains to the left renal vein."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck, thyroid and parathyroid glands and the laryngeal nerves"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, cranial cavity, pituitary, and cavernous sinus"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, kidneys and suprarenal glands"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck, pharyngeal pouches and thyroid development"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, pituitary and parathyroid sections"}]},"EN-4":{"unit":"EN-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01","title":"Blood Supply of the Endocrine Glands","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Endocrine glands secrete into blood, so the way blood enters and leaves each gland shapes how its hormones work and how the gland fails. This chapter traces the vessels of the thyroid, pituitary, adrenals, parathyroids, and pancreatic islets, then uses that anatomy to explain four classic exam problems: why the anterior pituitary infarcts after postpartum hemorrhage, why the adrenal medulla makes mostly epinephrine, why calcium falls after thyroid surgery, and why the liver sees insulin before any other tissue. Board questions usually give the clinical event and ask you to name the vessel, the hormone that changed, or the lab pattern that follows.","prerequisites":["Branches of the external carotid, subclavian, and abdominal aorta","Embryology of the pituitary (Rathke pouch and neurohypophysis) and pharyngeal pouches","Hypothalamic releasing hormones and anterior pituitary axes (EN-3)","PTH and vitamin D control of plasma calcium","Insulin synthesis from proinsulin and hepatic glucose metabolism"],"connections":[{"to":"Hypothalamic and pituitary physiology (EN-3)","how":"The portal system is the delivery route that makes hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary possible."},{"to":"Calcium and bone physiology","how":"Loss of parathyroid blood supply removes PTH and produces the predictable lab pattern of low calcium with high phosphate."},{"to":"Biochemistry of catecholamines","how":"Cortisol carried from cortex to medulla induces PNMT, the final enzyme of epinephrine synthesis."},{"to":"Hepatic portal circulation (GI units)","how":"Pancreatic venous drainage into the portal vein sets up hepatic first-pass extraction of insulin."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy","how":"The thyroid arteries run with the external laryngeal and recurrent laryngeal nerves, linking vascular anatomy to voice changes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Portal system","def":"A circulation in which blood passes through two capillary beds in series, connected by veins, before returning to the heart."},{"term":"Median eminence","def":"The base of the hypothalamus where hypothalamic axons end on fenestrated capillaries outside the blood-brain barrier."},{"term":"Fenestrated capillary","def":"A capillary whose endothelium has pores that let hormones and other solutes pass freely between cells and blood."},{"term":"End artery","def":"An artery that is the only blood supply to its territory, with too few anastomoses to rescue the tissue if it is blocked."},{"term":"Thyroid ima artery","def":"An inconstant midline artery, present in about 10% of people, that ascends in front of the trachea to the thyroid isthmus."},{"term":"Sheehan syndrome","def":"Ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary after severe postpartum hemorrhage, causing hypopituitarism."},{"term":"Chromaffin cell","def":"A neural crest derived cell of the adrenal medulla that synthesizes and secretes catecholamines."},{"term":"PNMT","def":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, the cytosolic enzyme that methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine using S-adenosylmethionine."},{"term":"Hepatic first-pass extraction","def":"Removal of a substance by the liver during its first passage through the portal circulation, before it reaches the systemic blood."},{"term":"C-peptide","def":"The connecting peptide cut from proinsulin and secreted in equimolar amounts with insulin, with little hepatic clearance."},{"term":"Tetany","def":"Involuntary muscle spasm caused by increased nerve excitability, classically from low ionized calcium."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01-C01","obj":"EN-4.1","title":"Arteries and veins of the thyroid gland","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid has one of the highest blood flows per gram of any organ. Each follicle is wrapped in fenestrated capillaries that deliver iodide for hormone synthesis and carry thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) away. Two arteries reach each lobe, and they anastomose freely with each other and across the midline, so tying a single artery rarely leaves the gland ischemic."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior thyroid artery is usually the first anterior branch of the external carotid artery. It descends to the upper pole of the lobe alongside the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, which supplies the cricothyroid muscle. The inferior thyroid artery comes from the thyrocervical trunk, a branch of the first part of the subclavian artery. It ascends, then turns medially behind the carotid sheath to enter the lower posterior surface of the lobe, where its branches cross the recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove. In about 10% of people a thyroid ima artery, most often from the brachiocephalic trunk, ascends in front of the trachea to the isthmus."},{"type":"table","title":"Vessels of the thyroid","cols":["Vessel","Origin or destination","Key relation"],"rows":[["Superior thyroid artery","External carotid artery","External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve"],["Inferior thyroid artery","Thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian artery","Recurrent laryngeal nerve; main supply of the parathyroids"],["Thyroid ima artery (inconstant)","Usually brachiocephalic trunk; sometimes aortic arch or other neck arteries","Lies in front of the trachea below the isthmus"],["Superior thyroid vein","Internal jugular vein","Travels with the superior thyroid artery"],["Middle thyroid vein","Internal jugular vein","Short vein with no accompanying artery"],["Inferior thyroid veins","Brachiocephalic veins, mainly the left","Form a plexus in front of the trachea; no accompanying artery"]],"caption":"Arteries and veins do not mirror each other: the inferior thyroid artery comes from the subclavian system, but the inferior veins drain to the brachiocephalic veins."},{"type":"p","text":"Several findings follow directly from this anatomy. In Graves disease, antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor cause follicular hyperplasia and greatly increased vascularity, which can produce a palpable thrill and an audible bruit over the gland. The inferior thyroid veins and, when present, the thyroid ima artery lie in front of the trachea below the isthmus, so they are the vessels at risk in a low midline tracheostomy. Because each artery travels with a nerve, injury near the superior thyroid artery can paralyze the cricothyroid and cause a weak voice that cannot reach high pitch, and injury near the inferior thyroid artery can damage the recurrent laryngeal nerve and cause hoarseness."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01-C02","obj":"EN-4.2","title":"The hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system and anterior pituitary ischemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The two pituitary lobes have different origins and different blood supplies. The posterior lobe (neurohypophysis) grows down from the diencephalon and contains the axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons, which release antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin into capillaries fed directly by the inferior hypophyseal arteries from the cavernous part of the internal carotid artery. The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) comes from Rathke pouch, has no neural connection to the hypothalamus, and is controlled entirely through blood."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior hypophyseal arteries leave the internal carotid artery just above the cavernous sinus and form a primary capillary plexus in the median eminence and upper stalk. These capillaries are fenestrated and lie outside the blood-brain barrier, so peptides and dopamine released by hypothalamic neurons enter them freely. Long portal veins carry this blood down the stalk to a second bed of sinusoids among the anterior lobe cells, and short portal veins bring additional portal blood from the lower stalk. Anterior pituitary hormones enter the same sinusoids and leave through the cavernous sinuses and petrosal sinuses."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypophyseal portal transport","steps":["Superior hypophyseal arteries supply the median eminence","Hypothalamic neurons release releasing hormones and dopamine there","Hormones enter the fenestrated primary capillary plexus","Portal veins carry them down the pituitary stalk","Secondary sinusoids deliver them to anterior pituitary cells","Pituitary hormones drain to the cavernous sinuses"],"caption":"Two capillary beds in series let small amounts of hypothalamic hormone reach the anterior lobe undiluted by the systemic circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"The portal route matters because releasing hormones are secreted in tiny amounts and last only minutes. Diluted in the whole blood volume they would be ineffective, but in portal blood they arrive concentrated and their pulsatile pattern is preserved. Dopamine reaching lactotrophs by this route tonically inhibits prolactin release, so cutting or compressing the stalk lowers every anterior pituitary hormone except prolactin, which rises."},{"type":"p","text":"The same arrangement makes the anterior lobe vulnerable. Nearly all of its blood arrives through portal veins after passing through one capillary bed already, so its perfusion pressure is low and it has almost no direct arterial supply to fall back on. In pregnancy, estrogen-driven lactotroph hyperplasia enlarges the gland inside the rigid sella turcica, raising its demand for blood. If severe postpartum hemorrhage causes hypotension (vasospasm of the supplying arteries may contribute), the anterior lobe can infarct (Sheehan syndrome). The posterior lobe, with its own arterial supply, is usually spared. Clinical hypopituitarism appears once most of the anterior lobe is destroyed."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormone losses in Sheehan syndrome","cols":["Hormone lost","Result","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Prolactin","Failure to lactate, often the first sign","No stimulus to milk production after delivery"],["LH and FSH","Amenorrhea, loss of libido","No gonadal stimulation, low estrogen"],["TSH","Secondary hypothyroidism","Low T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH"],["ACTH","Secondary adrenal insufficiency: fatigue, hypotension, hypoglycemia, hyponatremia","Low cortisol; aldosterone preserved by renin-angiotensin, so no hyperkalemia; low POMC, so no hyperpigmentation"],["GH","Hypoglycemia, reduced muscle mass","Loss of GH counterregulation and anabolic effects"]],"caption":"Every anterior lobe axis can fail; ADH and oxytocin are usually preserved because the posterior lobe has a separate arterial supply."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor compresses the pituitary stalk without touching the gland. Which anterior pituitary hormone rises?","explain":"Prolactin. Compression interrupts portal delivery of dopamine, removing the tonic inhibition of lactotrophs, while the other axes lose their releasing hormones and fall.","check":"The paragraph on why the portal route matters."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01-C03","obj":"EN-4.3","title":"Adrenal vessels and the cortex-to-medulla route that induces PNMT","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each adrenal gland receives three sets of arteries, which break into many small branches forming a plexus under the capsule, but drains through a single vein."},{"type":"table","title":"Adrenal vessels","cols":["Vessel","Source or destination"],"rows":[["Superior suprarenal arteries","Inferior phrenic artery"],["Middle suprarenal artery","Abdominal aorta"],["Inferior suprarenal artery","Renal artery"],["Right suprarenal vein","Directly into the inferior vena cava (short vein)"],["Left suprarenal vein","Left renal vein, often joined by the left inferior phrenic vein"]],"caption":"Three arterial sources and one vein per side; like the left gonadal vein, the left suprarenal vein ends in the left renal vein, while the right drains straight into the IVC."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the gland, blood follows three routes. Capsular vessels supply the capsule. Most blood enters fenestrated cortical sinusoids and flows centripetally through the zona glomerulosa, fasciculata, and reticularis into the medullary capillaries, collecting cortisol on the way. A smaller set of medullary arteries passes straight through the cortex, giving the medulla fresh arterial blood as well. All routes empty into a central medullary vein whose wall contains bundles of longitudinal smooth muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"The venous asymmetry has practical meaning. In adrenal vein sampling, used to decide whether excess aldosterone comes from one gland or both, the short right vein entering the back of the inferior vena cava is much harder to catheterize than the left vein, which is reached through the left renal vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Chromaffin cells make catecholamines from tyrosine. Tyrosine hydroxylase (the rate-limiting step) forms DOPA, which becomes dopamine; dopamine enters secretory granules and is converted to norepinephrine by dopamine beta-hydroxylase. Norepinephrine then leaks into the cytosol, where PNMT transfers a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine to form epinephrine, which is pumped back into granules. PNMT expression depends on high glucocorticoid levels. Because sinusoidal blood reaching the medulla has just passed through the cortex, its cortisol concentration is many times the systemic level, and this local cortisol keeps PNMT induced. As a result, about 80% of adult adrenal medullary output is epinephrine."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cortical cortisol to medullary epinephrine","steps":["Suprarenal arteries feed the subcapsular plexus","Blood flows inward through cortical sinusoids","Zona fasciculata adds cortisol at very high concentration","Cortisol-rich blood bathes medullary chromaffin cells","Cortisol induces PNMT in the cytosol","PNMT methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine","Central vein drains to IVC or left renal vein"],"caption":"The intra-adrenal portal-like flow from cortex to medulla links glucocorticoid output to epinephrine synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Several distinctions follow. Chromaffin tissue outside the adrenal (paraganglia, including the organ of Zuckerkandl near the aortic bifurcation) does not receive cortisol-rich blood, so it secretes mainly norepinephrine, and extra-adrenal paragangliomas are usually norepinephrine-predominant. A tumor secreting large amounts of epinephrine points to an adrenal origin. When cortisol production falls, as in ACTH deficiency or destruction of the cortex, PNMT activity and epinephrine output can fall as well."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A catecholamine-secreting tumor sits beside the aortic bifurcation. Would you expect it to secrete mainly epinephrine or norepinephrine?","explain":"Mainly norepinephrine. Outside the adrenal, chromaffin cells do not receive blood that has passed through the cortex, so cortisol is too low to sustain PNMT expression.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on extra-adrenal chromaffin tissue."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01-C04","obj":"EN-4.4","title":"Parathyroid blood supply and hypocalcemia after thyroidectomy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"There are usually four parathyroid glands, each weighing only a few tens of milligrams, on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes. The superior pair develops from the fourth pharyngeal pouch and stays in a fairly constant position near the cricothyroid junction. The inferior pair develops from the third pouch, descends with the thymus, and varies more in position, sometimes lying low in the neck or in the mediastinum."},{"type":"p","text":"Each gland is supplied by a single tiny parathyroid artery that enters at its hilum and behaves as an end artery. For the inferior glands, and for most superior glands, this artery is a branch of the inferior thyroid artery, because that artery divides on the posterior surface of the lobe exactly where the glands lie; in some people the superior glands are supplied by the superior thyroid artery or by an anastomosis between the two. Venous blood drains into the thyroid venous plexus. Because the glands are small, tan, and easily mistaken for fat or lymph nodes, and because their arteries run on the gland surface where dissection occurs, thyroid surgery can remove them or cut off their blood supply. A devascularized gland stops secreting just as a removed gland does."},{"type":"p","text":"PTH has a plasma half-life of only a few minutes, so when secretion stops, PTH falls within hours. Plasma calcium then falls over the next 1 to 3 days as each PTH action is lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypocalcemia after loss of parathyroid supply","steps":["Parathyroid arteries are divided, or glands removed","PTH secretion stops; PTH falls within hours","Osteoclastic bone resorption decreases","Distal tubule calcium reabsorption decreases","Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase falls, so calcitriol falls","Intestinal calcium absorption decreases","Ionized calcium falls; phosphate rises from reduced phosphaturia"],"caption":"Bone, kidney, and gut all deliver less calcium to plasma once PTH is gone."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal total calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L), and ionized calcium about 4.5 to 5.3 mg/dL (1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L). Low extracellular calcium lets voltage-gated Na+ channels open at less depolarized potentials, lowering the firing threshold of nerves. Peripheral nerves then fire spontaneously, giving perioral and fingertip tingling, muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, and in severe cases laryngospasm or seizures. Tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear causes twitching of the facial muscles (Chvostek sign), and inflating a blood pressure cuff above systolic pressure for about 3 minutes causes carpal spasm (Trousseau sign). On the ECG the ST segment lengthens, prolonging the QT interval."},{"type":"p","text":"Two other causes of low calcium after neck surgery must be separated. In hungry bone syndrome, after surgery for long-standing hyperparathyroidism or hyperthyroidism, demineralized bone rapidly takes up calcium and phosphate, so calcium and phosphate are both low while PTH is normal or high. Hyperventilation from pain or anxiety causes respiratory alkalosis, which increases calcium binding to albumin and lowers ionized calcium without changing total calcium."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting calcium 36 hours after thyroidectomy","text":"A patient reports perioral tingling 36 hours after total thyroidectomy. Total calcium is 7.0 mg/dL, albumin 3.0 g/dL, phosphate 5.8 mg/dL (normal about 2.5 to 4.5), and PTH is undetectable. Step 1: correct for albumin. Each 1 g/dL of albumin below 4.0 lowers total calcium by about 0.8 mg/dL, so corrected calcium is 7.0 + 0.8 = 7.8 mg/dL, still low. Step 2: read phosphate. It is high, which means PTH is not driving phosphaturia. Step 3: read PTH. It is inappropriately low for the hypocalcemia. Low calcium, high phosphate, and low PTH together indicate hypoparathyroidism from devascularized or removed glands, not hungry bone syndrome, where phosphate would be low and PTH normal or high."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-04-01-C05","obj":"EN-4.5","title":"Portal drainage of islet hormones and hepatic first-pass extraction of insulin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Islets make up only about 1 to 2% of pancreatic mass but receive a much larger share of pancreatic blood flow. Their arteries come from pancreatic branches of the splenic artery and from the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries (branches of the gastroduodenal and superior mesenteric arteries). Within each islet an afferent arteriole breaks into a dense network of fenestrated capillaries, so hormone enters blood almost as soon as it is secreted. Some efferent blood passes on to surrounding acinar capillaries, but all of it eventually leaves through pancreatic veins that join the splenic vein (body and tail) and the superior mesenteric vein (head). These two veins unite behind the neck of the pancreas to form the hepatic portal vein."},{"type":"flow","title":"Route of secreted insulin","steps":["Beta cells secrete insulin and C-peptide into islet capillaries","Pancreatic veins drain to splenic and superior mesenteric veins","Hepatic portal vein carries hormones to the liver","Hepatocytes bind and degrade about half the insulin","Hepatic veins carry the remainder into the systemic circulation","Muscle and fat receive lower insulin; kidneys clear much of it"],"caption":"The liver is both the first target and the first clearance site for secreted insulin; C-peptide passes through largely untouched."},{"type":"p","text":"This anatomy places the liver, the main organ buffering blood glucose, first in line. Portal insulin concentration is normally about two to three times the peripheral level, and portal glucagon is also higher than peripheral. The liver responds to the portal insulin-to-glucagon ratio: insulin suppresses glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis and promotes glycogen storage, while glucagon acts through cAMP to do the opposite. Glucagon acts mainly on the liver, because skeletal muscle has essentially no glucagon receptors, so portal drainage delivers it straight to its main target."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes take up insulin by receptor-mediated endocytosis and degrade it, largely through insulin-degrading enzyme. About half of the insulin in portal blood is removed on the first pass, though the fraction varies with physiological state. The kidneys clear much of the insulin that reaches the systemic circulation, and plasma insulin has a half-life of about 5 minutes. C-peptide is cut from proinsulin inside the same secretory granules and released in equimolar amounts, but the liver extracts very little of it. It is cleared mainly by the kidney and has a half-life of about 30 minutes, so peripheral C-peptide exceeds insulin on a molar basis and tracks secretion more faithfully."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading insulin and C-peptide","cols":["Situation","Peripheral insulin","C-peptide","Reason"],"rows":[["Normal secretion","Lower than portal","Higher than insulin (molar)","Hepatic first-pass extraction of insulin but not C-peptide"],["Insulinoma or sulfonylurea effect","High","High","Beta cells are secreting proinsulin-derived insulin"],["Injected insulin","High","Low","Exogenous insulin contains no C-peptide and suppresses beta cells"],["Cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting","High","Normal or high","Less hepatic extraction lets more insulin reach the periphery"]],"caption":"Insulin reflects secretion minus hepatic and renal clearance; C-peptide reflects secretion."},{"type":"p","text":"Injected insulin reverses the normal gradient: it enters the systemic circulation first, so muscle and fat receive relatively more and the liver relatively less than with secreted insulin. Renal failure slows systemic insulin clearance and so prolongs insulin action."},{"type":"example","title":"Using C-peptide to locate the source of insulin","text":"Two adults have fasting hypoglycemia with glucose about 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L). In both, insulin is high, which is inappropriate because hypoglycemia should switch secretion off. Patient A has high C-peptide and proinsulin. Because C-peptide is released only when beta cells cleave proinsulin, the excess insulin is being secreted, as with an insulinoma or a sulfonylurea effect. Patient B has suppressed C-peptide. The high insulin cannot come from beta cells, so it must be exogenous. The portal anatomy explains why C-peptide is the better marker: insulin is variably extracted by the liver before it is measured, but C-peptide is not."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology explain the supply: the anterior pituitary, derived from oral ectoderm with no neural link to the hypothalamus, depends on portal blood; the parathyroids, from the pharyngeal pouches, sit on the thyroid and borrow its inferior artery; and the pancreas drains to the portal vein because it develops from foregut endoderm within the territory of the gut circulation. Physiology and biochemistry explain why the arrangement is useful, because portal and portal-like routes deliver hormones at high concentration to the cells that need them most: releasing hormones to the anterior lobe, cortisol to the medulla, and insulin and glucagon to the liver."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology shows the cost of the same designs. Low-pressure portal supply makes the enlarged anterior pituitary infarct when blood pressure falls, end arteries make the parathyroids easy to devascularize, and portal drainage makes peripheral insulin an imperfect measure of secretion. The limits matter as well: arterial patterns vary between people, the posterior pituitary is spared only because it has a separate supply, and lab values must be read together, since calcium, phosphate, PTH, insulin, and C-peptide each change for more than one reason."}],"summary":["The superior thyroid artery comes from the external carotid and the inferior thyroid artery from the thyrocervical trunk; a thyroid ima artery is present in about 10% of people.","Superior and middle thyroid veins drain to the internal jugular; inferior thyroid veins drain to the brachiocephalic veins in front of the trachea.","Superior hypophyseal arteries feed the median eminence plexus, and portal veins carry releasing hormones and dopamine to the anterior lobe's sinusoids.","The anterior pituitary depends on low-pressure portal blood, so the pregnancy-enlarged lobe infarcts with postpartum hypotension (Sheehan syndrome), while the arterially supplied posterior lobe is spared.","Stalk compression lowers every anterior pituitary hormone except prolactin, which rises when portal dopamine is lost.","Each adrenal has superior (inferior phrenic), middle (aorta), and inferior (renal) arteries and one vein: right to the IVC, left to the left renal vein.","Blood flows from cortex to medulla, and the high local cortisol induces PNMT, so the medulla secretes mainly epinephrine while extra-adrenal chromaffin tissue secretes norepinephrine.","The parathyroids depend on small end arteries, mainly from the inferior thyroid artery, so thyroidectomy can cause hypocalcemia with high phosphate and low PTH within 1 to 3 days.","Islet hormones drain into the portal vein, so the liver sees insulin and glucagon first and removes about half the insulin on first pass.","C-peptide escapes hepatic extraction and reflects beta-cell secretion: high in insulinoma, low after injected insulin."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Neck (thyroid and parathyroid glands) and Abdomen (suprarenal glands and pancreas)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Endocrine Glands, sections on the hypophysis and adrenal glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, sections on hypopituitarism and the adrenal medulla"}]},"EN-5":{"unit":"EN-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01","title":"Endocrine Organ Function and Control","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what switches the major hormones on and off and what each one does at its target. It covers hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary and the effect of cutting the pituitary stalk, ADH control of water balance, PTH and calcium, aldosterone and potassium, the steps of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion, and the hormones that defend blood glucose. Board questions usually give a lab pattern or a lesion and ask you to predict the hormone change or name the cell, receptor, or channel responsible. If you can trace each hormone from its stimulus to its cellular action, you can reason through the electrolyte, glucose, and pituitary questions in this area.","prerequisites":["G protein signaling (Gs, Gi, Gq), cAMP, protein kinase A, and intracellular steroid receptors","Nephron segments and the medullary osmotic gradient","Membrane potential, K+ channels, and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","Glycogen metabolism, gluconeogenesis, and the fed and fasting states","Anatomy and blood supply of the hypothalamus and pituitary (EN-3, EN-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hormone action, transport, and feedback (EN-6)","how":"The feedback loops in this chapter are what let a pair of lab values locate a disorder at the hypothalamus, pituitary, or target gland."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"ADH and aldosterone both act on collecting duct principal cells, one controlling water and the other sodium and potassium."},{"to":"Endocrine biochemistry (EN-7)","how":"Insulin, glucagon, and epinephrine act by phosphorylating the enzymes of glycogen metabolism and gluconeogenesis."},{"to":"Endocrine hyperfunction and hypofunction (EN-12-A, EN-12-B)","how":"SIADH, diabetes insipidus, hyperparathyroidism, hyperaldosteronism, and hypopituitarism are failures of the control systems taught here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hypophyseal portal system","def":"Capillaries in the median eminence drained by veins in the pituitary stalk into a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary."},{"term":"Stalk effect","def":"Rise in prolactin when a lesion interrupts dopamine delivery from the hypothalamus to the lactotrophs."},{"term":"Effective osmole","def":"A solute that cannot freely cross cell membranes and therefore moves water between compartments, such as sodium or glucose."},{"term":"Aquaporin-2","def":"Water channel stored in vesicles in collecting duct principal cells and inserted into the luminal membrane in response to ADH."},{"term":"Calcium-sensing receptor","def":"G protein coupled receptor on parathyroid chief cells and thick ascending limb cells that is activated by extracellular ionized calcium."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"Osteoblast surface protein that binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives osteoclast formation and activity."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active vitamin D hormone made by 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule."},{"term":"ENaC","def":"Epithelial sodium channel in the luminal membrane of collecting duct principal cells, increased by aldosterone."},{"term":"Glucokinase","def":"Hexokinase IV of liver and beta cells, with low glucose affinity and no product inhibition, which lets it act as a glucose sensor."},{"term":"K-ATP channel","def":"ATP-sensitive K+ channel of the beta cell, built from Kir6.2 and SUR1 subunits, that closes when the ATP to ADP ratio rises."},{"term":"Incretin effect","def":"The greater insulin response to oral glucose than to intravenous glucose producing the same blood glucose, caused by gut hormones."},{"term":"Counterregulation","def":"The hormonal responses that prevent or correct a fall in blood glucose."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C01","obj":"EN-5.1","title":"Hypothalamic control of the anterior pituitary and pituitary stalk section","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The hypothalamus controls the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) through blood rather than nerves. Small neurons in the paraventricular, arcuate, and nearby nuclei release peptides and dopamine into the primary capillary plexus of the median eminence, supplied by the superior hypophyseal arteries. Portal veins in the pituitary stalk carry this blood to a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe, so the hypothalamic hormones reach their target cells at high concentration before they are diluted in the general circulation. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) works differently: it is made of axons from the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei that release ADH and oxytocin directly into its capillaries."},{"type":"p","text":"Four hypothalamic hormones are stimulatory. TRH, CRH, GnRH, and GHRH each drive a main target cell, so TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, and GH all depend on continuous hypothalamic input. GnRH must arrive in pulses; continuous exposure downregulates its receptor and suppresses LH and FSH. Two hormones inhibit. Somatostatin restrains GH and, to a lesser degree, TSH. Dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular neurons of the arcuate nucleus acts on D2 receptors (Gi, lowering cAMP) on lactotrophs and is the dominant control of prolactin, which makes prolactin the only anterior pituitary hormone held mainly under tonic inhibition. TRH also stimulates lactotrophs, which is why primary hypothyroidism, with high TRH, can raise prolactin."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypothalamic hormones and their anterior pituitary targets","cols":["Hypothalamic hormone","Main source","Receptor signal","Target cell","Effect","After stalk section"],"rows":[["TRH","Paraventricular nucleus","Gq","Thyrotrophs (also lactotrophs)","Raises TSH (and prolactin)","TSH falls"],["CRH","Paraventricular nucleus","Gs","Corticotrophs","Raises ACTH","ACTH falls"],["GnRH","Preoptic area and arcuate nucleus","Gq","Gonadotrophs","Raises LH and FSH when pulsatile","LH and FSH fall"],["GHRH","Arcuate nucleus","Gs","Somatotrophs","Raises GH","GH falls"],["Somatostatin","Periventricular nucleus","Gi","Somatotrophs and thyrotrophs","Lowers GH and TSH","Inhibition lost, but outweighed by loss of GHRH and TRH"],["Dopamine","Arcuate nucleus (tuberoinfundibular)","Gi (D2)","Lactotrophs","Lowers prolactin","Prolactin rises"]],"caption":"After stalk section every anterior pituitary hormone falls except prolactin, which escapes dopamine inhibition."},{"type":"p","text":"Pituitary stalk section, from trauma, surgery, or a mass compressing the stalk, interrupts portal flow. TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, and GH fall, followed by their target hormones: secondary hypothyroidism, low cortisol, hypogonadism, and low IGF-1. Prolactin rises because the lactotrophs are freed from dopamine, usually only modestly. Aldosterone is largely preserved because angiotensin II and potassium, not ACTH, are its main controls (C04)."},{"type":"p","text":"Section also cuts the supraoptic and paraventricular axons, so ADH can no longer reach the posterior lobe and central diabetes insipidus follows, with large volumes of dilute urine. The classic course is triphasic: diabetes insipidus within a day or two as the axons stop conducting, then several days of unregulated ADH release with water retention as the cut terminals degenerate and leak stored hormone, then permanent diabetes insipidus if enough cell bodies die. A cut close to the hypothalamus kills more neurons and makes permanent diabetes insipidus more likely; after a low cut, surviving axons can release ADH from the stump and median eminence."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the lab pattern after stalk section","text":"A man's pituitary stalk is transected in a car crash. Work hormone by hormone. TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, and GH all need a stimulatory releasing hormone, so all fall, and free T4, cortisol, testosterone, and IGF-1 fall with them. Prolactin is held down by dopamine, so losing portal blood raises it. Aldosterone stays near normal because angiotensin II and potassium still drive the zona glomerulosa, so he does not develop hyperkalemia. ADH axons are cut, so within a day or two he passes several liters of dilute urine and his serum sodium rises if he cannot drink enough. About a week later his sodium may fall as degenerating terminals release stored ADH, before diabetes insipidus returns."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C02","obj":"EN-5.2","title":"Control and renal action of ADH","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ADH (arginine vasopressin) is made in large neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei as a prohormone with its carrier protein neurophysin II, transported down the axons, and released from the posterior pituitary when the neurons fire. Its job is to set free water excretion by the kidney so that plasma osmolality stays near about 285 mOsm/kg (reference about 275 to 295 mOsm/kg)."},{"type":"h","text":"Osmotic control"},{"type":"p","text":"Osmoreceptors are neurons in the organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis and the subfornical organ, circumventricular organs that lack a blood brain barrier and so sense plasma directly. When effective osmolality rises, water leaves these cells, they shrink, stretch-inactivated cation channels open, and they signal the ADH neurons and the thirst center. Above a threshold near 280 mOsm/kg, plasma ADH rises in proportion to osmolality, and a change of about 1% is enough to alter release. Only effective osmoles shrink the osmoreceptors. Sodium and its anions, glucose in insulin deficiency, and mannitol stimulate ADH, but urea crosses cell membranes freely and is a weak stimulus even though it raises measured osmolality."},{"type":"h","text":"Volume and pressure control"},{"type":"p","text":"Stretch receptors in the atria and great veins and arterial baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch tonically inhibit ADH release through vagal and glossopharyngeal afferents to the nucleus tractus solitarius. A fall in blood volume or pressure removes this inhibition. This pathway is insensitive, needing roughly a 5% to 10% fall, but once triggered it raises ADH far above the levels osmolality produces, and it lowers the osmotic threshold. That is why volume wins when the two signals conflict: a hypovolemic patient keeps releasing ADH and retains water even while hyponatremic. Nausea, pain, stress, angiotensin II, and hypoglycemia also stimulate ADH, and ethanol inhibits it."},{"type":"flow","title":"From stimulus to concentrated urine","steps":["Plasma osmolality rises or blood volume falls","Osmoreceptors fire; baroreceptor inhibition is removed","ADH released from posterior pituitary","ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells (Gs, cAMP, PKA)","PKA phosphorylates aquaporin-2; vesicles fuse with luminal membrane","Water exits to blood through basolateral aquaporin-3 and aquaporin-4","Urine concentrates; plasma osmolality falls toward normal"],"caption":"Water moves only if the medullary interstitium is hypertonic; ADH supplies the permeability, and the loop of Henle supplies the gradient."},{"type":"p","text":"Without ADH the luminal membrane of the principal cell is nearly impermeable to water, so dilute fluid leaving the thick ascending limb passes through and urine osmolality can fall to about 50 mOsm/kg. With maximal ADH, collecting duct fluid equilibrates with the medullary interstitium and urine reaches about 1200 mOsm/kg. Sustained ADH also increases aquaporin-2 synthesis, inserts urea transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct, and stimulates NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, which together maintain the medullary gradient. At higher concentrations ADH acts on V1 receptors (Gq) on vascular smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction."},{"type":"p","text":"Absent ADH release (central diabetes insipidus) or absent renal response (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, from V2 or aquaporin-2 mutations, lithium, hypercalcemia, or hypokalemia) produces polyuria of dilute urine and a tendency to hypernatremia. Only the central form concentrates urine after exogenous ADH, because the receptor pathway is intact. ADH release unchecked by osmolality (SIADH) produces inappropriately concentrated urine and dilutional hyponatremia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients each have a plasma osmolality of 310 mOsm/kg. In one the cause is hypernatremia; in the other it is a high blood urea from kidney failure. Which one has strong thirst and high ADH?","explain":"The hypernatremic patient. Sodium is an effective osmole that pulls water out of osmoreceptor cells and shrinks them; urea enters cells as easily as it leaves, so it raises measured osmolality without shrinking the receptors.","check":"Osmotic control: the paragraph on effective osmoles."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C03","obj":"EN-5.3","title":"Calcium homeostasis: PTH, calcitriol, and calcitonin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Total serum calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (about 2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L). About 45% is ionized, about 40% is bound to albumin, and the rest is complexed with anions such as citrate and phosphate. Only ionized calcium (about 4.5 to 5.3 mg/dL, or 1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L) is sensed by the parathyroid and controls nerve and muscle excitability. Low albumin lowers total calcium without changing the ionized fraction. Alkalosis removes H+ from albumin, exposing negative sites that bind more calcium, so acute hyperventilation lowers ionized calcium and can cause tetany."},{"type":"p","text":"Parathyroid chief cells carry the calcium-sensing receptor, a G protein coupled receptor (Gq and Gi) activated by ionized calcium. Activation suppresses PTH secretion and PTH gene transcription. The relation is a steep inverse sigmoid curve, so a small fall in ionized calcium releases stored PTH within minutes. Severe hypomagnesemia impairs both PTH secretion and PTH action, causing hypocalcemia that persists while magnesium stays low."},{"type":"table","title":"Actions of PTH","cols":["Site","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Bone","PTH1 receptor on osteoblasts raises RANKL and lowers osteoprotegerin, activating osteoclasts","Calcium and phosphate released into blood"],["Distal convoluted tubule","More luminal TRPV5 calcium channels and basolateral calcium extrusion","More calcium reabsorbed"],["Proximal tubule","Removes sodium-phosphate cotransporters (NaPi-IIa, NaPi-IIc) from the luminal membrane","Phosphate excreted in urine"],["Proximal tubule","Induces 1-alpha-hydroxylase, converting 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol","Gut calcium and phosphate absorption rises over a day or more"]],"caption":"PTH acts on osteoclasts only indirectly, through osteoblasts. The kidney responds within minutes, osteoclastic bone resorption builds over hours to days, and gut absorption through calcitriol is slowest."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Calcium feedback loop","steps":["Ionized calcium falls","Calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells less active","PTH secretion rises","Bone resorption and distal calcium reabsorption increase","Calcitriol rises and gut absorbs more calcium","Ionized calcium rises and suppresses PTH"],"caption":"Calcitriol also suppresses PTH gene transcription, adding a second negative feedback signal."},{"type":"p","text":"Phosphate wasting is built into PTH action. Bone resorption releases calcium and phosphate together; if the phosphate were retained, it would complex with calcium and blunt the rise in free calcium. By blocking proximal phosphate reabsorption, PTH raises serum calcium and lowers serum phosphate. Calcitriol, acting on the gut, raises both. FGF23 from osteocytes adds phosphate control by promoting phosphaturia and inhibiting 1-alpha-hydroxylase."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcitonin from the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid is released when calcium rises. It binds receptors on osteoclasts (mainly Gs, cAMP), which retract and stop resorbing bone, and it mildly increases renal calcium excretion. Its role in adult calcium balance is minor: serum calcium stays normal after total thyroidectomy and in medullary thyroid carcinoma, despite absent or very high calcitonin."},{"type":"p","text":"In primary hyperparathyroidism, calcium is high, phosphate low, and PTH high or inappropriately normal. Urinary calcium is often high, because the filtered calcium load exceeds the increase in distal reabsorption. In hypoparathyroidism calcium is low and phosphate high. An inactivating calcium-sensing receptor mutation (familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia) raises the set point, giving mild hypercalcemia with low urinary calcium, because the same receptor in the thick ascending limb no longer limits calcium reabsorption."},{"type":"example","title":"Correcting total calcium for albumin","text":"A malnourished patient has total calcium 7.6 mg/dL and albumin 2.0 g/dL, with no tetany. Each 1 g/dL fall in albumin below 4.0 g/dL lowers total calcium by about 0.8 mg/dL, because less calcium is bound. Corrected calcium = 7.6 + 0.8 x (4.0 minus 2.0) = 9.2 mg/dL, which is normal. The ionized fraction, the part the calcium-sensing receptor detects, is probably normal, so PTH should not be raised and there are no symptoms. The correction is only an estimate; when the result matters, measure ionized calcium directly."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C04","obj":"EN-5.4","title":"Regulation and collecting duct actions of aldosterone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone is made only in the zona glomerulosa, the one adrenal cortical zone that expresses aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2). Two signals control it. Angiotensin II binds AT1 receptors (Gq), raising intracellular calcium and increasing both the early steps of steroid synthesis and aldosterone synthase. A rise in serum potassium acts directly: glomerulosa cell membrane potential is set mainly by K+ conductance, so a small increase in extracellular K+ depolarizes the cell, opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, and stimulates synthesis. This gives the body a potassium feedback loop that works independently of volume. ACTH raises aldosterone only briefly, which is why aldosterone is preserved in pituitary failure but lost when the adrenal cortex itself is destroyed."},{"type":"table","title":"Regulators of aldosterone secretion","cols":["Regulator","Signal in glomerulosa cell","Effect","Consequence"],"rows":[["Angiotensin II","AT1 receptor, Gq, Ca2+","Strong stimulation","Aldosterone rises when renal perfusion or volume falls"],["High serum K+","Depolarization, Ca2+ influx","Strong stimulation","K+ feedback loop separate from volume"],["ACTH","MC2 receptor, Gs, cAMP","Small, transient stimulation","Aldosterone near normal in secondary adrenal insufficiency"],["ANP","Natriuretic peptide receptor, cGMP","Inhibition","Volume expansion lowers aldosterone"]],"caption":"Angiotensin II and potassium are the main controls; ACTH is a minor one."},{"type":"flow","title":"Aldosterone action in the collecting duct","steps":["Aldosterone enters principal cell and binds mineralocorticoid receptor","Receptor complex enters nucleus; SGK1 and other genes induced","More luminal ENaC channels and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps","Na+ reabsorbed; lumen becomes electrically negative","K+ secreted through luminal ROMK channels","Alpha intercalated cells secrete more H+","Net: Na+ retained; K+ and H+ lost in urine"],"caption":"The lumen-negative voltage created by sodium reabsorption links sodium retention to potassium and hydrogen ion loss."},{"type":"p","text":"The mineralocorticoid receptor is an intracellular receptor that acts as a transcription factor. Cortisol binds it as well as aldosterone does and circulates at much higher concentration, so principal cells express 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which converts cortisol to inactive cortisone and leaves the receptor to aldosterone. Inhibiting this enzyme, as glycyrrhetinic acid in licorice does, lets cortisol act as a mineralocorticoid."},{"type":"p","text":"Aldosterone induces SGK1, a kinase that phosphorylates the ubiquitin ligase Nedd4-2 and so prevents ENaC from being removed from the luminal membrane. It also increases Na+/K+-ATPase, which pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ into the cell across the basolateral membrane, keeping intracellular Na+ low and K+ high. Na+ then enters through ENaC down its gradient. Because Na+ is reabsorbed faster than anions follow, the lumen becomes negative, and this voltage drives K+ out of the cell through ROMK channels. Potassium secretion also rises with distal Na+ delivery and tubular flow."},{"type":"p","text":"Alpha intercalated cells secrete H+ into the lumen through an apical H+-ATPase and H+/K+-ATPase and return new bicarbonate to the blood through a basolateral Cl-/HCO3- exchanger (AE1). Aldosterone stimulates the H+-ATPase directly, and the lumen-negative voltage makes H+ secretion easier."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary hyperaldosteronism therefore causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with low renin. Edema is usually absent because of aldosterone escape: after a few days, pressure natriuresis and ANP raise sodium excretion, while the potassium and hydrogen ion losses continue. Aldosterone deficiency causes the reverse: hyperkalemia, a mild non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (type 4 renal tubular acidosis), and, in primary adrenal failure, salt wasting and hypotension."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Compare serum potassium and acid-base status in a patient with autoimmune destruction of both adrenal glands and in a patient with isolated ACTH deficiency.","explain":"Adrenal destruction removes aldosterone, so K+ and H+ secretion fall and hyperkalemia with metabolic acidosis follows. In ACTH deficiency the zona glomerulosa still responds to angiotensin II and potassium, so aldosterone, potassium, and pH stay near normal; only cortisol is lost.","check":"Regulators of aldosterone secretion table and the potassium and H+ secretion paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C05","obj":"EN-5.5","title":"Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion and the incretin effect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The beta cell couples blood glucose to insulin release through its own metabolism. Glucose enters through high-capacity, low-affinity transporters (GLUT2 is the classic answer; human beta cells also rely on GLUT1), so intracellular glucose follows plasma glucose and transport is not the limiting step. The sensor is glucokinase (hexokinase IV), which has a half-saturating glucose concentration of about 8 mmol/L (about 145 mg/dL) and is not inhibited by its product. Its activity therefore rises in step with glucose across the physiological range, and glycolytic flux and ATP production rise with it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Glucose-stimulated insulin secretion","steps":["Glucose enters beta cell through GLUT transporters","Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose in proportion to blood glucose","Glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation raise the ATP to ADP ratio","ATP closes K-ATP channels (Kir6.2 with SUR1)","Less K+ efflux depolarizes the membrane","Voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels open; Ca2+ enters","Ca2+ triggers granule exocytosis: insulin and C-peptide released"],"caption":"Each step follows from the one before: metabolism sets ATP, ATP sets membrane voltage, and voltage sets calcium entry."},{"type":"p","text":"The K-ATP channel is the link between metabolism and voltage. At low glucose it is open, holding the membrane near about -70 mV. ATP binding to the Kir6.2 pore closes it, while MgADP acting on SUR1 opens it, so the ATP to ADP ratio sets its activity. Drugs show the mechanism: sulfonylureas bind SUR1 and close the channel, releasing insulin even when glucose is low, and diazoxide opens it and blocks release. Gene defects show it too: activating K-ATP mutations cause neonatal diabetes, inactivating mutations cause congenital hyperinsulinism, and heterozygous glucokinase loss raises the set point and causes mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia (GCK MODY)."},{"type":"p","text":"Release is biphasic: a first phase lasting about 10 minutes from granules already docked at the membrane, then a sustained second phase from recruited granules. Loss of first-phase secretion is an early defect in type 2 diabetes. Proinsulin is cleaved in the granule, so insulin and C-peptide are released in equal amounts, and C-peptide measures the body's own insulin secretion. Acetylcholine (M3, during the cephalic phase), amino acids such as arginine, and beta2 stimulation increase release; somatostatin and sympathetic alpha2 stimulation inhibit it."},{"type":"h","text":"The incretin effect"},{"type":"p","text":"Oral glucose releases more insulin than an intravenous infusion that produces the same blood glucose curve. This difference, the incretin effect, accounts for about 50% to 70% of the insulin response to an oral glucose load in healthy people. Nutrients in the lumen release GIP from K cells of the duodenum and proximal jejunum and GLP-1 from L cells of the distal ileum and colon. Both bind Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells and raise cAMP, which through protein kinase A and Epac2 increases granule recruitment and the calcium sensitivity of exocytosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Incretins amplify secretion but do not trigger it. At low glucose the K-ATP channels stay open and the membrane stays polarized, so extra cAMP has little effect; the incretin action is glucose dependent. GLP-1 also suppresses glucagon, slows gastric emptying, and promotes satiety. Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inactivates both hormones within minutes. The incretin effect is reduced in type 2 diabetes, largely because beta cells respond poorly to GIP."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Blood glucose is 60 mg/dL (3.3 mmol/L). What happens to insulin release if a drug closes K-ATP channels, and what happens if GLP-1 is raised instead?","explain":"Closing K-ATP channels bypasses the glucose sensor, so the cell depolarizes and releases insulin despite hypoglycemia. GLP-1 only amplifies a calcium signal that already exists; with the channels open and the membrane polarized, it adds little insulin.","check":"The K-ATP channel paragraph and the incretin paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-05-01-C06","obj":"EN-5.6","title":"Glucose counterregulation in fasting and hypoglycemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The brain uses glucose as its main fuel and cannot store it, so blood glucose is held at about 70 to 99 mg/dL (3.9 to 5.5 mmol/L) during fasting. Counterregulation is a hierarchy of responses that switch on as glucose falls. The first defense is a fall in insulin; glucagon and epinephrine then provide rapid correction, while cortisol and growth hormone (GH) support glucose production over hours."},{"type":"flow","title":"Glycemic thresholds for counterregulation","steps":["About 80 to 85 mg/dL: insulin secretion falls","About 65 to 70 mg/dL: glucagon and epinephrine rise","About 65 to 70 mg/dL: cortisol and GH rise, acting over hours","About 50 to 55 mg/dL: autonomic warning symptoms appear","Below about 50 mg/dL: cognitive impairment, then seizures and coma"],"caption":"Approximate arterialized venous glucose thresholds in healthy people (1 mmol/L = 18 mg/dL). Hormones respond before symptoms, so normal people rarely notice mild falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Counterregulatory hormones","cols":["Hormone","Onset","Signal","Main glucose-raising actions"],"rows":[["Glucagon","Minutes","Glucagon receptor, Gs, cAMP, PKA","Hepatic glycogenolysis, then gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis; little effect on muscle"],["Epinephrine","Minutes","Beta2 and alpha1 (liver), beta (fat), alpha2 (islets)","Hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis; lipolysis; muscle glycogenolysis supplies lactate; inhibits insulin release"],["Cortisol","Hours","Glucocorticoid receptor, gene transcription","Induces gluconeogenic enzymes; muscle proteolysis supplies amino acids; less peripheral glucose uptake; permissive for glucagon and epinephrine"],["Growth hormone","Hours","GH receptor, JAK2 and STAT","Lipolysis; fatty acids reduce muscle glucose uptake and spare glucose"]],"caption":"The fast hormones change the activity of existing enzymes; the slow hormones change how much enzyme and substrate exist."},{"type":"p","text":"The difference in speed comes from mechanism. Glucagon and epinephrine raise hepatic cAMP, and protein kinase A phosphorylates enzymes that already exist: phosphorylase kinase and glycogen phosphorylase are activated, glycogen synthase is inhibited, and the bifunctional enzyme lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, which slows glycolysis and favors gluconeogenesis. Glucose output rises within minutes. Cortisol and GH act through gene transcription and protein synthesis, so their effects take hours, but they sustain glucose production by providing gluconeogenic enzymes and substrate. Deficiency of cortisol or GH therefore causes fasting hypoglycemia, as in adrenal insufficiency and hypopituitarism, especially in children."},{"type":"p","text":"In the postabsorptive state, falling insulin and a modest rise in glucagon let hepatic glycogenolysis supply most glucose. As liver glycogen runs low over about the first day, gluconeogenesis from lactate, alanine, and glycerol takes over. Low insulin also releases fatty acids from fat; muscle and liver burn them, and glucagon promotes hepatic ketogenesis by lowering malonyl-CoA. In prolonged fasting the brain takes up ketones, which reduces the need to break down muscle protein, and the kidney contributes more gluconeogenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Epinephrine and sympathetic activation produce the autonomic warning symptoms of hypoglycemia: tremor, palpitations, and anxiety (adrenergic), and sweating and hunger (cholinergic). Neuroglycopenic symptoms such as confusion and abnormal behavior reflect brain glucose lack. In long-standing type 1 diabetes the glucagon response to hypoglycemia is lost, leaving epinephrine as the key defense. Repeated hypoglycemia then lowers the threshold for epinephrine release as well, so warning symptoms appear late or not at all (hypoglycemia unawareness)."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology meet in the hypothalamus and pituitary: the portal circulation explains why stalk lesions raise prolactin while lowering the other anterior pituitary hormones, and the separate axonal path to the posterior lobe explains diabetes insipidus after the same injury. The collecting duct principal cell links two systems, receiving ADH through a Gs receptor that controls water and aldosterone through a nuclear receptor that controls sodium and potassium, which is why water and sodium disorders are read together."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology complete the picture. The beta cell uses glucokinase and ATP as a sensor, so a single gene defect in glucokinase or the K-ATP channel shifts the glucose set point, while autoimmune destruction of beta cells in type 1 diabetes eventually removes the fall in intra-islet insulin that is thought to signal alpha cells to release glucagon during hypoglycemia. Each hormone in this chapter is judged against its controlling signal: a PTH level means little without the ionized calcium, aldosterone without renin and potassium, ADH without plasma osmolality and volume, and insulin without the simultaneous glucose."}],"summary":["TRH, CRH, GnRH, and GHRH stimulate; somatostatin inhibits GH and TSH; dopamine on D2 receptors is the main control of prolactin.","Stalk section lowers TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, and GH, raises prolactin, spares aldosterone, and causes diabetes insipidus that can follow a triphasic course.","Osmoreceptors respond to about a 1% rise in effective osmolality; baroreceptor input needs a larger fall in volume but overrides osmotic control.","ADH acts on V2 receptors (Gs, cAMP, PKA) to insert aquaporin-2 into principal cell luminal membranes; V1 receptors (Gq) cause vasoconstriction.","Low ionized calcium releases PTH through the calcium-sensing receptor; PTH resorbs bone through osteoblast RANKL, reabsorbs calcium distally, excretes phosphate proximally, and makes calcitriol.","PTH raises calcium and lowers phosphate; calcitriol raises both; calcitonin lowers calcium but matters little in adults.","Aldosterone is driven by angiotensin II and hyperkalemia, with a minor ACTH effect, and increases ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase, causing K+ secretion by principal cells and H+ secretion by alpha intercalated cells.","Insulin release follows glucose entry, glucokinase, rising ATP, K-ATP closure, depolarization, Ca2+ influx, and exocytosis.","GLP-1 and GIP raise beta cell cAMP and amplify glucose-driven insulin release, explaining why oral glucose releases more insulin than intravenous glucose.","Falling insulin is the first defense against hypoglycemia; glucagon and epinephrine act within minutes, while cortisol and GH act over hours through gene expression."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus; Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation of the hypothalamus and pituitary, calcium and phosphate, and the endocrine pancreas"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolic effects of insulin and glucagon; the feed and fast cycle"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the hypothalamus and pituitary gland; disorders of the parathyroids and calcium metabolism"}]},"EN-6":{"unit":"EN-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01","title":"Hormone Action, Transport, and Feedback","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how hormones reach their targets, how they signal once they arrive, and how negative feedback keeps each axis stable. It covers receptor classes and second messengers, plasma binding proteins and the free hormone principle, feedback logic for localizing a lesion from paired lab values, and the actions and regulation of thyroid hormone, growth hormone, cortisol, and prolactin. Board questions often give two hormone levels and ask where the problem lies, or name a hormone and ask which messenger it uses. Reasoning from receptor class, free hormone, and feedback direction answers most of them.","prerequisites":["G protein coupled receptor structure and the GTPase cycle","Lipid solubility and membrane transport of small molecules","Hypothalamic nuclei and the hypophyseal portal system","Hepatic plasma protein synthesis","Gene transcription: promoters, response elements, and transcription factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine organ function and control (EN-5)","how":"The portal circulation and pituitary stalk explain why releasing and inhibiting hormones control the anterior pituitary and why stalk damage raises prolactin."},{"to":"Hormone synthesis and degradation (EN-8)","how":"The hormones whose actions are taught here are made by the biosynthetic pathways covered in the gland chapters."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Cholera toxin locks Gs on and pertussis toxin blocks Gi, showing the same G protein switches that hormones use."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Cortisol suppresses cytokine genes and shifts leukocyte counts, which links stress physiology to infection risk."},{"to":"Reproductive physiology","how":"Pulsatile GnRH drives LH and FSH, so anything that disrupts GnRH pulses, such as high prolactin, causes hypogonadism."}],"terms":[{"term":"Second messenger","def":"An intracellular signal, such as cAMP, IP3, calcium, or DAG, generated when a hormone binds a surface receptor."},{"term":"Hormone response element","def":"A DNA sequence near a target gene to which a hormone-receptor complex binds to change transcription."},{"term":"Free hormone","def":"The small fraction of a hormone not bound to plasma proteins, which is the fraction that enters cells, acts, and is sensed by feedback."},{"term":"Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)","def":"A hepatic glycoprotein that carries most circulating T4 and T3 and whose level changes total but not free hormone."},{"term":"Cortisol-binding globulin (CBG)","def":"A hepatic glycoprotein, also called transcortin, that carries most circulating cortisol."},{"term":"Trophic hormone","def":"A pituitary hormone, such as TSH or ACTH, that stimulates a target endocrine gland to secrete and grow."},{"term":"Negative feedback","def":"Inhibition of hypothalamic and pituitary secretion by the hormone the axis produces, which keeps that hormone near a set point."},{"term":"Primary disorder","def":"A disorder of the target gland itself, in which the target hormone and its trophic hormone move in opposite directions."},{"term":"Deiodinase","def":"A selenium-containing enzyme that removes iodine from thyroid hormones, activating T4 to T3 or inactivating it."},{"term":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1)","def":"A peptide made mainly by the liver under GH stimulation that mediates most of the growth-promoting actions of GH."},{"term":"Permissive action","def":"An effect by which one hormone, without acting strongly itself, allows another hormone to produce its full response."},{"term":"Circadian rhythm","def":"A roughly 24-hour cycle of secretion set by the suprachiasmatic nucleus, as in the morning peak of ACTH and cortisol."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C01","obj":"EN-6.1","title":"Hormone receptors and signaling pathways","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A hormone's chemistry decides where its receptor sits. Peptide hormones and catecholamines are water soluble, cannot cross the lipid bilayer, and bind surface receptors that generate second messengers within seconds to minutes. Steroids and calcitriol are lipid soluble and diffuse into the cell; thyroid hormone, although an amine, enters through membrane transporters (such as MCT8). Both groups bind intracellular receptors that act as transcription factors, so their effects develop over hours to days."},{"type":"h","text":"G protein coupled receptors"},{"type":"p","text":"These receptors have seven membrane-spanning segments and couple to a heterotrimeric G protein. Hormone binding makes the alpha subunit exchange GDP for GTP and separate from the beta-gamma dimer; the signal ends when the alpha subunit hydrolyzes its GTP. Gs stimulates adenylyl cyclase, as shown below. Gq activates phospholipase C, which cleaves membrane PIP2 into IP3, which releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum, and DAG, which with calcium activates protein kinase C. Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, which is how dopamine at D2 receptors and somatostatin suppress pituitary secretion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Gs and cAMP signaling","steps":["Hormone binds a Gs-coupled receptor","Gs alpha releases GDP, binds GTP, separates","Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cAMP","cAMP activates protein kinase A","Protein kinase A phosphorylates enzymes, channels, and CREB","GTP hydrolysis and phosphodiesterase end the signal"],"caption":"Each step amplifies the signal, so a few hormone molecules change many target proteins."},{"type":"h","text":"Receptor tyrosine kinases and JAK/STAT"},{"type":"p","text":"The insulin and IGF-1 receptors are preformed dimers with a kinase domain in their own beta subunits. Ligand binding triggers autophosphorylation, then phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins, which activate the PI3K and Akt pathway (glucose uptake, glycogen and protein synthesis) and the Ras and MAP kinase pathway (growth). GH and prolactin receptors belong to the cytokine receptor family and have no kinase of their own; the receptor exists as a dimer, and hormone binding changes its shape so that the associated Janus kinase 2 (JAK2) molecules activate each other and the receptor, which phosphorylates STAT proteins that dimerize and enter the nucleus."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormone signaling pathways","cols":["Pathway","Hormones","Key mechanism"],"rows":[["Gs, cAMP","ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, glucagon; also CRH, GHRH, calcitonin, hCG, ADH at V2","Protein kinase A phosphorylation"],["Gq, IP3 and calcium","GnRH, TRH, oxytocin; also ADH at V1, angiotensin II","Phospholipase C; IP3 releases calcium, DAG activates protein kinase C"],["Gi, lower cAMP","Dopamine (D2), somatostatin","Inhibits adenylyl cyclase"],["Intrinsic tyrosine kinase","Insulin, IGF-1","Autophosphorylation, IRS, PI3K and Akt, MAP kinase"],["JAK/STAT","GH, prolactin","Receptor-associated JAK2 phosphorylates STAT"],["Intracellular receptor","Cortisol, aldosterone, sex steroids, thyroid hormone, calcitriol","Hormone-receptor complex binds response elements on DNA"]],"caption":"Some hormones also use a second pathway (PTH and TSH can activate Gq); the table lists the main one tested."},{"type":"p","text":"Intracellular receptors share a ligand-binding domain and a zinc finger DNA-binding domain. Glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and androgen receptors wait in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins and move to the nucleus when hormone binds. Thyroid hormone and vitamin D receptors already sit on DNA as heterodimers with the retinoid X receptor; unliganded thyroid receptor actively represses its genes, and T3 converts it to an activator."},{"type":"p","text":"Defects in these switches cause disease that follows the pathway, not the gland. Loss-of-function mutation of the Gs alpha gene (GNAS) in pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a causes resistance to PTH and often to TSH and gonadotropins, because all use Gs. Mosaic activating GNAS mutation in McCune-Albright syndrome causes autonomous function of cAMP-driven tissues."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has high PTH, low calcium, high phosphate, and mildly high TSH. Why might both hormones fail?","explain":"PTH and TSH both signal through Gs. A loss-of-function GNAS mutation blunts cAMP generation in both target tissues, so the glands secrete more hormone without effect. This is end-organ resistance, not gland failure.","check":"The Gs row of the signaling table and the GNAS paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C02","obj":"EN-6.2","title":"Plasma transport and the free hormone principle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lipid-soluble hormones dissolve poorly in plasma, so most of each travels bound to proteins made by the liver. Binding creates a circulating reservoir, protects the hormone from filtration and metabolism, and so lengthens its half-life. Peptide hormones are mostly unbound and are cleared within minutes; T4, mostly bound, has a half-life of about 7 days, and T3 about 1 day."},{"type":"table","title":"Plasma binding of thyroid hormone and cortisol","cols":["Hormone","Main carriers","Free fraction","Half-life"],"rows":[["T4","TBG (about 70%), transthyretin, albumin","About 0.03%","About 7 days"],["T3","TBG (most), albumin","About 0.3%","About 1 day"],["Cortisol","CBG (about 80 to 90%), albumin","About 5 to 10%","About 60 to 90 minutes"]],"caption":"TBG binds T4 more tightly than T3, so T3 has a larger free fraction and acts faster."},{"type":"p","text":"Only free hormone crosses membranes, binds receptors, and is sensed by the hypothalamus and pituitary. Bound hormone is in rapid equilibrium with the free pool and replaces free hormone as cells take it up, but it is not directly active. The feedback system therefore regulates free hormone, not total hormone, and total levels can change widely while the patient's hormone status stays normal."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Adjustment to a rise in TBG","steps":["Estrogen raises hepatic TBG synthesis and slows its clearance","Extra TBG binds T4; free T4 dips briefly","Pituitary senses less free T4; TSH rises","Thyroid secretes more T4 until new TBG fills","Free T4 restored; TSH returns to normal"],"caption":"The new steady state has a higher total T4 with normal free T4 and TSH."},{"type":"p","text":"Pregnancy, oral estrogen, and hepatitis raise TBG, so total T4 and T3 rise while free T4 and TSH stay normal and the patient is euthyroid. Androgens, glucocorticoids, nephrotic syndrome (urinary protein loss), and severe liver disease lower TBG and total T4, again with normal free T4. Estrogen raises CBG in the same way, so total cortisol rises in pregnancy or with oral contraceptives without features of cortisol excess. In the first trimester, hCG weakly stimulates the TSH receptor, so TSH can be slightly low; this is a separate effect from TBG."},{"type":"example","title":"A pregnant patient with high total T4","text":"At 20 weeks of pregnancy a woman has total T4 of 15 micrograms/dL (reference about 5 to 12), free T4 of 1.2 ng/dL (about 0.8 to 1.8), and TSH of 1.8 mU/L (about 0.4 to 4.0). Step 1: free T4 is normal, so tissues receive normal hormone. Step 2: TSH is normal, so the pituitary also senses normal free hormone; a primary thyroid disorder would move TSH opposite to free T4. Step 3: the raised total must be extra bound hormone. Estrogen has raised TBG, and the thyroid has filled it. She is euthyroid. The error to avoid is judging thyroid status from total T4."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C03","obj":"EN-6.3","title":"Localizing endocrine disorders by feedback logic","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each classic axis has three levels: a hypothalamic releasing hormone (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH), a pituitary trophic hormone (TSH, ACTH, LH and FSH, GH), and a target hormone (T4, cortisol, sex steroids, or hepatic IGF-1). The target hormone feeds back to inhibit both higher levels, so in health the trophic hormone moves opposite to any change in free target hormone. That fixed relationship is what lets a pair of values locate a lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"In a primary disorder, the target gland itself fails or secretes autonomously. Its hormone changes first, and an intact pituitary responds in the opposite direction: low free T4 with high TSH, or high cortisol with suppressed ACTH. In a secondary (pituitary) or tertiary (hypothalamic) disorder, the trophic signal changes first and the target hormone follows in the same direction: low cortisol with low ACTH, or high cortisol with high ACTH. A trophic hormone in the normal range is inappropriate when the target hormone is clearly low, so it still counts as low."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing by feedback","cols":["Pattern","Site","Example"],"rows":[["Low free T4, high TSH","Thyroid (primary)","Hashimoto thyroiditis"],["Low free T4, low or normal TSH","Pituitary or hypothalamus","Sheehan syndrome, pituitary tumor"],["High free T4, low TSH","Thyroid (primary) or exogenous","Graves disease, toxic nodule"],["High free T4, high or normal TSH","Pituitary (rare)","TSH-secreting adenoma"],["High cortisol, low ACTH","Adrenal or exogenous","Adrenal adenoma, glucocorticoid use"],["High cortisol, high ACTH","Pituitary or ectopic source","Cushing disease, small cell lung cancer"],["Low cortisol, high ACTH","Adrenal (primary)","Addison disease"],["Low cortisol, low ACTH","Pituitary or hypothalamus","Withdrawal after long glucocorticoid use"]],"caption":"Opposite directions point to the target gland; the same direction points above it."},{"type":"p","text":"Two further steps refine the answer. A releasing-hormone stimulation test separates pituitary from hypothalamic failure: a pituitary that cannot respond shows no rise, while a pituitary deprived only of its releasing hormone still responds. When cortisol and ACTH are both high, the high-dose dexamethasone suppression test separates a pituitary adenoma, whose corticotrophs still partly obey glucocorticoid feedback, from an ectopic ACTH tumor, which usually does not. Exogenous hormone mimics a primary disorder of excess and suppresses the patient's own gland; after long glucocorticoid treatment the suppressed axis, and atrophic adrenal cortex, take months to recover."},{"type":"example","title":"Locating hypercortisolism","text":"A patient has central obesity and striae. Late-night salivary cortisol is high, consistent with cortisol excess. Case A: ACTH is undetectable. Cortisol is high and ACTH low, opposite directions, so the source is the adrenal or an ingested glucocorticoid; the pituitary is responding normally to feedback. Case B: ACTH is high. Same direction, so excess ACTH drives both adrenals. High-dose dexamethasone lowers cortisol by more than half: the pituitary adenoma still senses feedback, so this is Cushing disease. Case C: ACTH is very high and dexamethasone has no effect, pointing to ectopic ACTH, as from small cell lung carcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has low free T4 and a TSH of 2.0 mU/L (normal range). Is the thyroid the problem?","explain":"Probably not. With low free T4, an intact pituitary should raise TSH well above normal. A normal TSH is inappropriately low, which points to pituitary or hypothalamic failure.","check":"The paragraph on inappropriately normal trophic hormone and the feedback table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C04","obj":"EN-6.4","title":"Thyroid hormone actions and peripheral activation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid secretes mostly T4 (about 90%), which is largely a prohormone. About 80% of circulating T3, the form that binds the nuclear receptor with roughly ten times greater affinity, is made in peripheral tissues by deiodinases, selenium-containing enzymes that remove one iodine. Which deiodinase acts decides whether T4 is activated or inactivated, so tissues can set their own thyroid status within limits."},{"type":"table","title":"The deiodinases","cols":["Enzyme","Main sites","Reaction","Role"],"rows":[["Type 1 (D1)","Liver, kidney, thyroid","T4 to T3 (outer ring)","Contributes plasma T3"],["Type 2 (D2)","Pituitary, brain, brown fat, muscle","T4 to T3 (outer ring)","Local T3, including the T3 that feeds back on thyrotrophs"],["Type 3 (D3)","Placenta, brain, fetal tissues","T4 to reverse T3; T3 to T2 (inner ring)","Inactivation"]],"caption":"Fasting and severe illness reduce T4 to T3 conversion and favor reverse T3, lowering T3 without true hypothyroidism."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the nucleus, T3 binds the thyroid hormone receptor, which sits on thyroid response elements as a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor. Binding releases corepressors and recruits coactivators, so effects build over hours to days, which is why symptoms lag behind changes in hormone levels."},{"type":"flow","title":"From T4 to a metabolic effect","steps":["Thyroid releases mostly T4","Transporters carry T4 into target cells","Deiodinase removes one outer-ring iodine, forming T3","T3 binds nuclear receptor on response elements","Transcription of Na/K-ATPase, beta-1 receptor, and other genes changes","Oxygen use, heat production, and heart rate rise"],"caption":"Activation happens mostly in target tissues, not in the gland."},{"type":"p","text":"Metabolic actions follow from gene induction. T3 increases Na/K-ATPase synthesis, and pumping ions consumes ATP; it also increases mitochondrial number and enzymes of oxidative metabolism. The result is higher basal metabolic rate, oxygen consumption, and heat production, explaining heat intolerance, weight loss, and warm skin in hyperthyroidism, and cold intolerance and weight gain in hypothyroidism. T3 also increases LDL receptors, so hypothyroidism raises LDL cholesterol."},{"type":"p","text":"In the heart, T3 increases beta-1 adrenergic receptor number, SERCA2 (faster calcium reuptake), and alpha myosin heavy chain, while lowering phospholamban. Heart rate, contractility, and relaxation speed rise, and peripheral vasodilation from heat production raises cardiac output and pulse pressure. Thyrotoxic tachycardia therefore reflects direct T3 effects on cardiac genes and heightened catecholamine sensitivity, while catecholamine levels are normal or low."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormone is required for growth and development. It works with GH and IGF-1 at the growth plate and promotes skeletal maturation, so childhood hypothyroidism causes short stature with delayed bone age. In the fetal and neonatal brain it drives neuronal migration, synapse formation, and myelination; the fetal thyroid begins making hormone at about 10 to 12 weeks, but the fetus depends heavily on maternal T4 until mid-gestation. Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes irreversible intellectual disability, which is why newborn screening exists."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C05","obj":"EN-6.5","title":"Growth hormone, IGF-1, and their regulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"GH is a peptide from somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary. It acts at two levels: direct metabolic actions through its own JAK2-coupled receptor on fat, muscle, and liver, and growth actions mainly through IGF-1, which the liver makes under GH stimulation and which tissues such as bone also make locally. Most circulating IGF-1 travels bound to IGF-binding protein 3, which gives it a long half-life."},{"type":"table","title":"Direct and indirect actions of GH","cols":["Action","Mediator","Mechanism and result"],"rows":[["Lipolysis","GH directly","Activates adipocyte lipolysis; free fatty acids rise and become fuel"],["Insulin antagonism","GH directly","Less glucose uptake in muscle and fat, more hepatic glucose output; blood glucose rises"],["Linear growth","IGF-1","Chondrocyte proliferation at the epiphyseal growth plate"],["Protein synthesis","IGF-1 (and GH)","Amino acid uptake and protein synthesis; lean mass and organ growth"]],"caption":"The direct actions spare glucose and protein during fasting; the IGF-1 actions build tissue when nutrition is adequate."},{"type":"p","text":"The direct actions explain why GH is called diabetogenic: in acromegaly, insulin resistance commonly causes glucose intolerance or diabetes. The split between GH and IGF-1 also explains Laron syndrome, in which a defective GH receptor gives high GH with low IGF-1 and short stature. Before epiphyseal closure, GH excess causes gigantism; after closure, bones cannot lengthen, and acromegaly enlarges hands, feet, jaw, and soft tissues."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Regulation of GH secretion","steps":["Hypothalamus releases GHRH; somatostatin falls","Somatotrophs secrete GH in pulses","Liver makes IGF-1","IGF-1 inhibits somatotrophs and stimulates somatostatin release","GH secretion falls until IGF-1 declines"],"caption":"IGF-1 feeds back on both the pituitary and the hypothalamus; GH also inhibits its own release."},{"type":"p","text":"GHRH acts through Gs and cAMP to stimulate synthesis and release, and somatostatin acts through Gi to inhibit release. Ghrelin from the stomach also stimulates GH. Secretion is pulsatile, with the largest pulses in slow-wave sleep early in the night, and rises with exercise, fasting, stress, and puberty, when sex steroids amplify pulses. Hypoglycemia is a strong stimulus because GH is a counterregulatory hormone that raises glucose; hyperglycemia and high free fatty acids suppress it, and obesity, glucocorticoid excess, and aging reduce it."},{"type":"p","text":"These rules shape testing. A single GH level is uninterpretable because of pulses, so IGF-1, which is steady through the day, screens for GH excess. In a healthy person an oral glucose load suppresses GH; failure to suppress confirms autonomous secretion in acromegaly. For deficiency, provocative stimuli that should raise GH, such as insulin-induced hypoglycemia, test the reserve."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C06","obj":"EN-6.6","title":"Cortisol actions and the circadian rhythm of the HPA axis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol, the main glucocorticoid, is made in the zona fasciculata under ACTH stimulation. It binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which moves to the nucleus and changes transcription in nearly every tissue. Its overall purpose is to keep glucose available and blood pressure maintained during fasting and stress while restraining inflammation. Its metabolic effects complement glucagon: cortisol increases the amount of gluconeogenic enzyme and supplies amino acid substrate from muscle, while glucagon acutely activates the pathway through cAMP. This is why cortisol deficiency causes fasting hypoglycemia even when glucagon is normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Actions of cortisol","cols":["Action","Mechanism","Consequence of excess"],"rows":[["Gluconeogenesis","Induces hepatic PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase; increases hepatic glycogen","Hyperglycemia, insulin resistance"],["Proteolysis","Breaks down muscle protein, supplying amino acids to the liver","Proximal muscle weakness and wasting"],["Lipolysis","Permissive for catecholamine and GH lipolysis; redistributes fat","Central obesity, thin limbs"],["Anti-inflammatory, immunosuppressive","Inhibits NF-kB; induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2; reduces COX-2, IL-1, IL-2, TNF","Infection risk, lymphopenia, eosinopenia, neutrophilia"],["Permissive vasoconstriction","Maintains vascular adrenergic receptor expression and catecholamine response","Hypertension (with mineralocorticoid effect)"],["Reduced collagen synthesis","Inhibits fibroblasts and osteoblasts","Thin skin, striae, bruising, poor healing, osteoporosis"]],"caption":"The same actions in deficiency produce hypoglycemia, hypotension, and poor stress tolerance."},{"type":"p","text":"The anti-inflammatory effect works at several points. Blocking phospholipase A2 and COX-2 limits arachidonic acid supply to prostaglandins and leukotrienes, and inhibiting NF-kB lowers cytokine and adhesion molecule genes. Lymphocyte counts fall by redistribution and apoptosis, eosinophils fall, and neutrophils rise because they leave the marrow faster and demarginate from vessel walls."},{"type":"p","text":"Permissive action means cortisol allows catecholamines to constrict vessels effectively. Without it, vascular tone falls, so adrenal insufficiency causes hypotension that responds poorly to pressors, and adrenal crisis can cause shock during stress. Cortisol also induces PNMT in the adrenal medulla, linking it to epinephrine synthesis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Circadian drive of the HPA axis","steps":["Suprachiasmatic nucleus entrained by light","CRH pulse amplitude rises in late sleep","ACTH and cortisol peak around waking","Cortisol falls through the day","Nadir near midnight, then cycle repeats"],"caption":"Stress overrides the rhythm at any time; cortisol feeds back on CRH and ACTH throughout."},{"type":"p","text":"Because ACTH and cortisol pulses follow this rhythm, morning cortisol is therefore used to screen for deficiency, while the loss of the late-night nadir is one of the earliest signs of Cushing syndrome, detected by late-night salivary cortisol."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-06-01-C07","obj":"EN-6.7","title":"Prolactin regulation and hyperprolactinemic hypogonadism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Prolactin is the one anterior pituitary hormone whose dominant hypothalamic control is inhibitory. Dopamine from tuberoinfundibular neurons of the arcuate nucleus travels in portal blood to lactotroph D2 receptors, which couple to Gi, lower cAMP, and suppress prolactin synthesis and release. Prolactin raises dopamine release (short-loop feedback), keeping its own level low outside pregnancy and lactation. Its main action is milk synthesis in a breast prepared by estrogen and progesterone; the high levels of those steroids in pregnancy block lactation until they fall after delivery."},{"type":"p","text":"Stimuli act mostly by relieving this brake or adding drive. TRH stimulates lactotrophs through Gq, which is why primary hypothyroidism, with high TRH, can raise prolactin and cause galactorrhea. Estrogen causes lactotroph hyperplasia during pregnancy, and suckling inhibits dopamine release to sustain milk production. Because the control is inhibitory, cutting the pituitary stalk lowers every other anterior pituitary hormone but raises prolactin; a nonfunctioning macroadenoma that compresses the stalk mildly raises prolactin for the same reason. Kidney failure raises prolactin by reducing clearance."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hyperprolactinemia by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Autonomous secretion","Prolactinoma"],["Loss of dopamine reaching lactotrophs","Stalk compression or section, D2 antagonists (antipsychotics, metoclopramide), dopamine-depleting drugs such as reserpine"],["Excess stimulation","High TRH in primary hypothyroidism, pregnancy (estrogen), suckling, nipple or chest wall stimulation"],["Reduced clearance","Chronic kidney disease"]],"caption":"Prolactinomas usually give the highest levels; stalk compression usually gives only modest elevation."},{"type":"flow","title":"How hyperprolactinemia causes hypogonadism","steps":["Prolactin rises","Hypothalamic kisspeptin and GnRH pulses are suppressed","LH and FSH fall","Ovarian estrogen or testicular testosterone falls","Anovulation, amenorrhea, infertility, low libido, erectile dysfunction"],"caption":"This is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism: gonadal hormones and gonadotropins are both low."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperprolactinemia shuts down the gonadal axis at the hypothalamus. Pulsatile GnRH, which the pituitary requires to secrete LH and FSH, is suppressed, largely by prolactin acting on kisspeptin neurons. Women develop oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, infertility, and galactorrhea; men develop low libido, erectile dysfunction, and infertility, and present later, often with larger tumors and visual field loss. Low estrogen or testosterone also reduces bone density. Lactational amenorrhea is the physiologic version of the same mechanism. The feedback pattern, low sex steroid with low gonadotropins, places the problem above the gonad, consistent with C03."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After head trauma that transects the pituitary stalk, which anterior pituitary hormone rises while the others fall?","explain":"Prolactin. The others depend on stimulatory releasing hormones that no longer reach the pituitary, but prolactin is held down by dopamine, so loss of portal dopamine releases it.","check":"The first two paragraphs of this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pharmacology meet in the receptor classes: the same Gs, Gi, and Gq switches serve neurotransmitters and hormones, so bacterial toxins (cholera toxin activating Gs, pertussis toxin blocking Gi) and GNAS mutations alter whole groups of hormones at once. The liver links to endocrinology through binding proteins and IGF-1, so liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, and estrogen change total hormone levels, and malnutrition lowers IGF-1 even when GH is high. Immunology connects through cortisol, whose suppression of cytokines and lymphocytes explains infection risk in cortisol excess."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from feedback. Nearly every endocrine disease is a disorder of excess or deficiency at one level of an axis, and the direction of the trophic and target pair locates it. The logic has limits: binding protein changes alter total but not free levels, nonthyroidal illness lowers T3 without true hypothyroidism, assays and reference ranges vary, and hormones with pulsatile or circadian secretion (GH, ACTH, cortisol) must be measured at the right time or by a stable surrogate such as IGF-1."}],"summary":["Peptide hormones use surface receptors and second messengers; steroids, thyroid hormone, and calcitriol use intracellular receptors that change transcription over hours.","Gs raises cAMP (ACTH, TSH, LH, FSH, PTH, glucagon); Gq raises IP3 and calcium (GnRH, TRH, oxytocin); insulin and IGF-1 use intrinsic tyrosine kinases; GH and prolactin use JAK/STAT.","Only free hormone acts and feeds back; raised TBG in pregnancy raises total T4 with normal free T4 and TSH.","Target and trophic hormones moving in opposite directions localize a disorder to the gland; moving together places it in the pituitary or hypothalamus.","Peripheral deiodinases convert T4 to more active T3; T3 raises metabolic rate through Na/K-ATPase, upregulates cardiac beta-1 receptors, and is needed for bone growth and brain maturation.","GH directly causes lipolysis and insulin antagonism; IGF-1 from the liver mediates linear growth and protein synthesis.","GH rises with GHRH, ghrelin, deep sleep, exercise, and hypoglycemia, and falls with somatostatin, IGF-1, and hyperglycemia.","Cortisol promotes gluconeogenesis, proteolysis, and lipolysis, suppresses inflammation and collagen synthesis, permits catecholamine vasoconstriction, and peaks around waking.","Tonic dopamine inhibits prolactin and TRH stimulates it; high prolactin suppresses GnRH and causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Introduction to Endocrinology"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pituitary Hormones and Their Control by the Hypothalamus; Thyroid Metabolic Hormones; Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation and signal transduction; the hypothalamic-pituitary axes"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Hormonal regulation and signal transduction"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Hypothalamus and Pituitary Gland; Thyroid Disease; Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex"}]},"EN-7":{"unit":"EN-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01","title":"Endocrine Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter connects hormones to the biochemistry and nutrients they depend on. It explains how insulin and glucagon switch the body between storing and releasing fuel, how losing that switch produces diabetic ketoacidosis, and how iodine, selenium, vitamin D, and magnesium are built into or required by endocrine pathways. It closes with leptin and ghrelin in appetite control and the link between visceral fat and insulin resistance. Board questions typically give a lab pattern or a deficiency and ask which enzyme, hormone, or feedback step explains it, so focus on the direction of each change and why it happens.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, gluconeogenesis, glycogen metabolism, and the TCA cycle","Fatty acid synthesis, beta-oxidation, and the carnitine shuttle","G protein and receptor tyrosine kinase signaling (cAMP, protein kinase A, PI3K-Akt)","Hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis and thyroid follicle structure (EN-3)","Calcium and phosphate regulation by PTH and calcitonin (EN-5)"],"connections":[{"to":"Diabetes mellitus (EN-8)","how":"Absolute insulin deficiency in type 1 diabetes produces the ketoacidosis described here, while the insulin resistance of type 2 diabetes builds on visceral adiposity."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"The anion gap, bicarbonate buffering, and respiratory compensation explain the laboratory picture of ketoacidosis."},{"to":"Thyroid disorders (EN-12)","how":"Iodine deficiency is the leading cause of goiter and hypothyroidism worldwide, and deiodinase activity decides how much active T3 tissues receive."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal pathology","how":"Vitamin D deficiency produces rickets and osteomalacia, defects of mineralization that differ from the loss of bone mass in osteoporosis."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal physiology","how":"Gut hormones such as ghrelin signal hunger to the hypothalamus, and the gut absorbs the vitamin D, calcium, and magnesium these pathways need."}],"terms":[{"term":"Insulin to glucagon ratio","def":"The balance of the two hormones reaching the liver, which sets whether hepatic metabolism stores or releases fuel."},{"term":"Malonyl-CoA","def":"The product of acetyl-CoA carboxylase that both supplies fatty acid synthesis and inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1 (CPT-1)."},{"term":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","def":"A regulatory metabolite that activates phosphofructokinase-1 and inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, favoring glycolysis over gluconeogenesis."},{"term":"Ketone bodies","def":"Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, water-soluble fuels made from acetyl-CoA in liver mitochondria; acetone is a nonmetabolized byproduct."},{"term":"Anion gap","def":"Serum sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, an estimate of unmeasured anions that rises when an organic acid accumulates."},{"term":"Organification","def":"The thyroid peroxidase reaction that attaches oxidized iodine to tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin."},{"term":"Deiodinase","def":"A selenocysteine-containing enzyme that removes iodine from thyroid hormones, activating T4 to T3 or inactivating T4 and T3."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormonal form of vitamin D made mainly in the renal proximal tubule."},{"term":"Osteoid","def":"The unmineralized organic bone matrix, mainly type I collagen, laid down by osteoblasts before calcium phosphate is deposited."},{"term":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism","def":"A rise in PTH that is an appropriate response to a low serum calcium or high phosphate caused by a problem outside the parathyroid glands."},{"term":"Leptin resistance","def":"Reduced hypothalamic response to leptin, so high circulating leptin fails to suppress appetite."},{"term":"Arcuate nucleus","def":"A hypothalamic nucleus next to the median eminence whose POMC and NPY/AgRP neurons integrate hormonal signals of energy stores and hunger."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C01","obj":"EN-7.1","title":"Insulin and glucagon in the fed and fasting states","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Insulin and glucagon act as a switch on fuel metabolism. After a meal, glucose and amino acids stimulate pancreatic beta cells to release insulin, while glucagon from alpha cells falls, so the high insulin to glucagon ratio favors storage. During fasting the ratio reverses and the liver releases glucose and ketones. The liver sees both hormones first through the portal vein, so it responds most strongly to the ratio."},{"type":"h","text":"How insulin signals storage"},{"type":"p","text":"The insulin receptor is a receptor tyrosine kinase. Its activated beta subunits phosphorylate insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins, which activate phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) and then Akt (protein kinase B). In skeletal muscle and adipose tissue, Akt signaling moves GLUT4 transporters from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane, which is the main way insulin lowers blood glucose. Liver (GLUT2), brain, and red cells take up glucose without insulin. Insulin also activates phosphodiesterase 3B, which lowers cAMP, and protein phosphatase 1, which removes the phosphates that glucagon signaling added. Dephosphorylation activates glycogen synthase and acetyl-CoA carboxylase and inactivates glycogen phosphorylase and hormone-sensitive lipase, so fuel is stored rather than released."},{"type":"p","text":"Insulin also raises hepatic fructose-2,6-bisphosphate and induces glucokinase, which speed glycolysis, and the acetyl-CoA produced is used for fatty acid synthesis (lipogenesis). In adipose tissue insulin increases lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium so triglyceride from chylomicrons and VLDL is taken up and stored. Through mTOR signaling it increases protein synthesis and reduces proteolysis, and it stimulates Na+/K+-ATPase, shifting potassium into cells."},{"type":"h","text":"How glucagon signals release"},{"type":"p","text":"Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled receptor, mainly on hepatocytes, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A (PKA). PKA activates phosphorylase kinase and therefore glycogen phosphorylase while inactivating glycogen synthase. It phosphorylates the bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase, so fructose-2,6-bisphosphate falls, slowing phosphofructokinase-1 and releasing fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase; with pyruvate kinase also inhibited and PEPCK induced, carbon flows toward glucose (gluconeogenesis). In the fasting state acetyl-CoA carboxylase is held phosphorylated and inactive (AMP-activated protein kinase is the main kinase), so malonyl-CoA falls and no longer inhibits CPT-1; fatty acids then enter mitochondria for beta-oxidation and ketogenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal muscle has few glucagon receptors and lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so muscle glycogen fuels only the muscle itself and is mobilized by epinephrine and contraction, not glucagon. Lipolysis in adipose tissue is driven mainly by the fall in insulin together with catecholamines; glucagon contributes little in humans."},{"type":"table","title":"Insulin and glucagon on fuel metabolism","cols":["Pathway","Insulin (fed)","Glucagon (fasting)","Key regulated step"],"rows":[["Glucose uptake, muscle and adipose","Increased","No direct effect","GLUT4 translocation"],["Glycogen","Synthesis increased (liver, muscle)","Breakdown increased (liver)","Glycogen synthase versus phosphorylase"],["Glycolysis","Increased","Decreased","PFK-1 via fructose-2,6-bisphosphate; pyruvate kinase"],["Gluconeogenesis","Decreased","Increased","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase; PEPCK"],["Fatty acid synthesis","Increased","Decreased","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"],["Fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis","Decreased","Increased (liver)","Malonyl-CoA inhibition of CPT-1"],["Lipolysis in adipose","Decreased","Small effect; low insulin and catecholamines dominate","Hormone-sensitive lipase"],["Protein","Synthesis increased, breakdown decreased","Hepatic amino acid uptake for gluconeogenesis","mTOR; transaminases"]],"caption":"Every storage pathway belongs to insulin and every release pathway to glucagon; phosphorylation by PKA generally favors release, dephosphorylation favors storage."},{"type":"p","text":"As fasting continues, the sources of blood glucose shift. Liver glycogen supplies most glucose for the first several hours and is largely used within about a day, after which gluconeogenesis from lactate, glycerol, and amino acids (especially alanine) dominates. Over days, rising ketone use by the brain spares muscle protein."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C02","obj":"EN-7.2","title":"Ketogenesis and diabetic ketoacidosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ketogenesis is a normal fasting adaptation. When insulin is low, fatty acids reach the liver, are oxidized, and the resulting acetyl-CoA is packaged as ketone bodies that the brain, heart, and skeletal muscle can burn. In a healthy fast the remaining insulin limits lipolysis, so ketones rise only modestly. In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), absolute insulin deficiency with glucagon excess removes that brake, and production far outruns use. DKA occurs mainly in type 1 diabetes because the residual insulin of type 2 diabetes is usually enough to restrain lipolysis, so type 2 diabetes more often presents with hyperosmolar hyperglycemia without major ketosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From insulin deficiency to anion gap acidosis","steps":["Insulin absent; counterregulatory hormones high","Hormone-sensitive lipase releases fatty acids from adipose","Low malonyl-CoA frees CPT-1 in liver","Beta-oxidation floods mitochondria with acetyl-CoA and NADH","Oxaloacetate diverted; acetyl-CoA exceeds TCA cycle capacity","HMG-CoA pathway forms acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate","Ketoacids dissociate; H+ consumes bicarbonate","Unmeasured anions accumulate: anion gap rises"],"caption":"Lipolysis supplies the substrate and glucagon opens the mitochondrial gate; the result is overproduction of ketoacids."},{"type":"h","text":"Why acetyl-CoA becomes ketones"},{"type":"p","text":"Beta-oxidation generates acetyl-CoA faster than the TCA cycle can accept it. Oxaloacetate, which acetyl-CoA needs to enter the cycle as citrate, is drawn off into gluconeogenesis, and the high NADH to NAD+ ratio from beta-oxidation shifts the remainder to malate. Two acetyl-CoA condense to acetoacetyl-CoA, then mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase (the rate-limiting step) and HMG-CoA lyase release acetoacetate. Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase reduces much of it to beta-hydroxybutyrate using NADH, so in DKA beta-hydroxybutyrate exceeds acetoacetate several-fold. Some acetoacetate decarboxylates spontaneously to acetone, which is exhaled and gives a fruity breath odor. The liver cannot use ketones because it lacks succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (thiophorase), the enzyme that reactivates acetoacetate in extrahepatic tissues."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the acidosis has an anion gap"},{"type":"p","text":"Acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acids are strong enough to dissociate almost completely at body pH. Each H+ released is buffered by bicarbonate, which falls, while the ketoacid anion stays in plasma unmeasured. Chloride does not rise to replace the lost bicarbonate, so the anion gap, calculated as Na+ minus (Cl- plus HCO3-), increases above its reference range of about 8 to 12 mEq/L. The fall in pH stimulates peripheral and central chemoreceptors, producing deep, rapid Kussmaul respiration that lowers PaCO2 as respiratory compensation."},{"type":"table","title":"Other manifestations of DKA and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hyperglycemia","Gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis increase while GLUT4 uptake falls"],["Polyuria, dehydration","Filtered glucose exceeds tubular reabsorption, causing osmotic diuresis"],["Normal or high serum K+ with total body K+ deficit","K+ leaves cells without insulin and with hyperosmolality, then is lost in urine"],["Weak urine ketone test early","Nitroprusside detects acetoacetate and acetone, not beta-hydroxybutyrate"]],"caption":"Volume depletion lowers GFR, which reduces renal excretion of glucose and ketoacids and worsens both."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the labs in DKA","text":"A 19-year-old has glucose 520 mg/dL, Na+ 132, Cl- 96, HCO3- 10 mEq/L, arterial pH 7.24, and PaCO2 24 mm Hg. The anion gap is 132 minus (96 plus 10), or 26 mEq/L, well above about 12, so an unmeasured anion has accumulated. Winter's formula predicts PaCO2 of 1.5 times 10 plus 8, or 23 plus or minus 2 mm Hg; the measured 24 shows appropriate respiratory compensation from Kussmaul breathing, not an added respiratory disorder. Serum K+ of 5.4 mEq/L reflects shift out of cells, not a body surplus."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient fasting for three days have ketosis without acidosis, while a patient with untreated type 1 diabetes develops severe acidosis?","explain":"In fasting, residual insulin restrains lipolysis and ketone production stays roughly matched to use by brain and muscle, so bicarbonate buffering keeps up. With no insulin, lipolysis and ketogenesis are unrestrained, production far exceeds use, and bicarbonate is consumed faster than the kidney regenerates it.","check":"The first paragraph and the DKA flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C03","obj":"EN-7.3","title":"Iodine, selenium, and thyroid hormone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid hormones are iodinated tyrosines, so dietary iodine is a structural building block: T4 (thyroxine) carries four iodine atoms and T3 (triiodothyronine) three. Selenium is not part of the hormone but is required by the enzymes that activate and inactivate it. Iodine therefore limits how much hormone the gland can make, while selenium limits how much active T3 tissues can generate."},{"type":"table","title":"Steps of thyroid hormone synthesis","cols":["Step","Location","Protein or enzyme"],"rows":[["Iodide trapping (2 Na+ with 1 I-), secondary active","Basolateral membrane","Sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)"],["Iodide transport into colloid","Apical membrane","Pendrin"],["Oxidation and organification onto tyrosines (MIT, DIT)","Apical membrane and colloid","Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) with H2O2 from DUOX2"],["Coupling: DIT plus DIT forms T4; MIT plus DIT forms T3","Thyroglobulin in colloid","Thyroid peroxidase"],["Endocytosis and proteolysis; release of T4 and T3","Follicular cell lysosomes","Lysosomal proteases"],["Recycling of iodide from MIT and DIT","Follicular cell cytoplasm","Iodotyrosine deiodinase"]],"caption":"TSH stimulates nearly every step through Gs and cAMP; the gland secretes mostly T4 with a smaller amount of T3."},{"type":"h","text":"Selenium and the deiodinases"},{"type":"p","text":"Most circulating T3 is made outside the thyroid by removing one iodine from the outer ring of T4. The three iodothyronine deiodinases contain selenocysteine at the active site. Type 1 (liver, kidney, thyroid) and type 2 (brain, pituitary, brown fat, muscle) perform outer ring deiodination to make T3. Type 3 removes an inner ring iodine, converting T4 to inactive reverse T3 and T3 to T2. Because the pituitary thyrotroph makes its own T3 from T4 through type 2 deiodinase, TSH secretion tracks serum T4 closely. Selenium also forms the active site of glutathione peroxidases, which protect follicular cells from the hydrogen peroxide they generate for TPO. Severe selenium deficiency impairs T4 to T3 conversion and is also linked to Keshan disease, a cardiomyopathy."},{"type":"h","text":"Iodine deficiency and goiter"},{"type":"cycle","title":"Iodine deficiency goiter","steps":["Dietary iodine is inadequate","Thyroid makes less T4","Less negative feedback on hypothalamus and pituitary","TRH and TSH rise","Follicular cell hypertrophy and hyperplasia","Gland enlarges and traps iodide more efficiently"],"caption":"The loop restores hormone output partly at the cost of a goiter, diffuse at first and often multinodular later."},{"type":"p","text":"When iodine is scarce, the gland adapts. TSH increases NIS expression and iodide clearance, a greater share of MIT relative to DIT shifts output toward T3, which needs less iodine, and peripheral type 2 deiodinase activity rises. Serum T4 is therefore typically low, T3 normal or near normal, and TSH high; many patients remain clinically euthyroid with a goiter. Deficiency during pregnancy deprives the fetal brain of maternal T4 before the fetal thyroid works, causing congenital hypothyroidism with intellectual disability, short stature, and deafness in its severe endemic form. Goitrogens such as thiocyanate from poorly processed cassava compete with iodide at NIS and worsen deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Excess iodine has an opposite, transient effect: a large load acutely inhibits organification (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect), and normal glands escape within days by reducing NIS expression."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In iodine deficiency, why can T3 stay near normal while T4 is low?","explain":"The gland shifts toward making MIT and T3, which uses three rather than four iodine atoms, and increased deiodinase activity converts more of the available T4 to T3.","check":"The paragraph on adaptation to iodine deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C04","obj":"EN-7.4","title":"Vitamin D activation and deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D is a prohormone. It becomes active only after two hydroxylations, and only the second is tightly regulated. Its active form, calcitriol, raises calcium and phosphate absorption so bone can be mineralized; without it, collagen matrix is laid down but not calcified."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin D activation","steps":["UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in skin to previtamin D3","Body heat isomerizes previtamin D3 to cholecalciferol (D3)","Binding protein (skin D3) or chylomicrons (dietary D) carry it to liver","Hepatic 25-hydroxylase forms 25-hydroxyvitamin D (storage form)","Renal proximal tubule 1-alpha-hydroxylase forms calcitriol","Calcitriol binds the nuclear vitamin D receptor in target cells"],"caption":"Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is driven by substrate supply; renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation is the regulated step."},{"type":"p","text":"Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and by low serum phosphate, and hypocalcemia stimulates it indirectly through PTH. It is suppressed by calcitriol itself, by high phosphate, and by FGF23 from osteocytes; when calcitriol is not needed, 24-hydroxylase diverts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to inactive products. Because 25-hydroxyvitamin D circulates at high concentration with a half-life of about 2 to 3 weeks, it is the best measure of vitamin D status. Calcitriol, with a half-life of hours, can be normal or even high early in deficiency because rising PTH drives its synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcitriol acts through the vitamin D receptor, a nuclear receptor. In the intestine it induces the apical calcium channel TRPV6, the binding protein calbindin, and the basolateral Ca2+-ATPase, and it increases sodium-phosphate cotransport. In bone it supports mineralization by supplying calcium and phosphate and, at high levels, increases osteoblast RANKL expression to promote resorption. It also feeds back to suppress PTH gene transcription."},{"type":"h","text":"Deficiency: secondary hyperparathyroidism and failed mineralization"},{"type":"p","text":"With too little vitamin D, intestinal calcium absorption falls and ionized calcium dips. The parathyroids respond with more PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism), which mobilizes calcium from bone and increases renal calcium reabsorption, so serum calcium is held low-normal. PTH also inhibits proximal tubule phosphate reabsorption, so phosphate is lost in urine and serum phosphate falls. The low calcium times phosphate product leaves newly formed osteoid unmineralized, and osteoblasts working against it raise alkaline phosphatase."},{"type":"table","title":"Deficiency at different ages and its laboratory pattern","cols":["Feature","Rickets (open growth plates)","Osteomalacia (adult)"],"rows":[["Defect","Failed mineralization of growth plate cartilage and osteoid","Failed mineralization of osteoid during remodeling"],["Findings","Bowed legs, rachitic rosary, widened wrists, frayed metaphyses, craniotabes, delayed growth","Diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, pseudofractures (Looser zones)"],["Labs","Low 25-hydroxyvitamin D; calcium low or low-normal; phosphate low; PTH high; alkaline phosphatase high","Same pattern"]],"caption":"Osteoporosis differs: bone mass is reduced but what remains is normally mineralized, and serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH are usually normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Causes follow the activation pathway: limited sun exposure, darker skin, or high latitude at the skin step; fat malabsorption for dietary vitamin D; severe liver disease or drugs that induce vitamin D catabolism; and chronic kidney disease, where lost 1-alpha-hydroxylase and phosphate retention raise PTH. The reverse also teaches the regulation: granulomas such as sarcoidosis express unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylase and cause hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting labs from where the pathway fails","text":"Nutritional deficiency: 25-hydroxyvitamin D low, calcitriol normal or high early, PTH high, phosphate low. Chronic kidney disease: 25-hydroxyvitamin D may be normal, but calcitriol is low because renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is lost; PTH is high, and phosphate is high rather than low because the failing kidney cannot excrete it. Hereditary vitamin D receptor defect: calcitriol is very high because PTH keeps stimulating its synthesis and the receptor cannot respond. Sarcoidosis: calcitriol high from granulomas, calcium high, PTH suppressed. In each case, find the failed or overactive step, then follow PTH and phosphate from it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C05","obj":"EN-7.5","title":"Hypomagnesemia and refractory hypocalcemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Magnesium is mostly intracellular and in bone, with only about 1% in the extracellular fluid, so serum magnesium (reference about 1.7 to 2.2 mg/dL) can underestimate total body depletion. Mg2+ is a cofactor for ATP-dependent enzymes, including adenylyl cyclase, which explains much of its role in the parathyroid axis."},{"type":"p","text":"Magnesium has a graded effect on PTH. Mg2+ acts as a weak agonist at the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on chief cells, so a mild or acute fall in magnesium can slightly increase PTH release, much as low calcium does. Severe depletion, usually below about 1 mg/dL, has the opposite effect and paradoxically suppresses secretion. The current explanation is that intracellular Mg2+ normally restrains the Gi and Gq signaling by which the CaSR inhibits PTH release; without it, the receptor pathway signals as if calcium were high, so PTH release is inhibited. Hypermagnesemia also suppresses PTH, through direct CaSR activation."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe depletion also causes end-organ resistance. The PTH receptor signals through Gs and adenylyl cyclase, and both cyclase activity and Mg-ATP substrate depend on magnesium, so bone and kidney respond poorly to whatever PTH is present. Renal calcitriol production falls as well, reducing intestinal calcium absorption."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why calcium stays low in severe magnesium deficiency","steps":["Severe intracellular Mg2+ depletion","CaSR signaling disinhibited; PTH secretion falls","Remaining PTH acts poorly on bone and kidney","Less bone calcium release and renal reabsorption","Calcitriol and gut calcium absorption fall","Hypocalcemia with low or inappropriately normal PTH"],"caption":"Calcium given alone cannot correct the defect because the parathyroid signal and its effect both remain impaired."},{"type":"p","text":"Restoring magnesium reverses the secretory block quickly, and PTH rises within minutes, while bone and kidney responsiveness and serum calcium recover over days. Hypokalemia often coexists for a parallel reason: intracellular Mg2+ normally blocks outward K+ flow through renal ROMK channels, so depletion increases potassium secretion in the distal nephron."},{"type":"table","title":"Common causes of hypomagnesemia by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Low intake or intestinal loss","Alcohol use disorder, malnutrition, chronic diarrhea, malabsorption"],["Reduced intestinal absorption via TRPM6/7","Long-term proton pump inhibitor use"],["Renal wasting","Loop and thiazide diuretics, aminoglycosides, cisplatin, amphotericin B, Gitelman syndrome"],["Shift into cells","Refeeding after starvation, recovery after parathyroidectomy (hungry bone)"]],"caption":"Alcohol use disorder combines poor intake, gut loss, and renal wasting."},{"type":"p","text":"Manifestations reflect the combined low magnesium, calcium, and potassium: neuromuscular irritability with tremor, tetany, and positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs, seizures, and arrhythmias including torsades de pointes from a prolonged QT."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-07-01-C06","obj":"EN-7.6","title":"Leptin, ghrelin, and the link from obesity to insulin resistance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Appetite and energy expenditure are integrated in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, which lies next to the median eminence, where the blood-brain barrier is relatively permeable to circulating hormones. Two opposing neuron groups sit there. POMC/CART neurons cleave pro-opiomelanocortin to alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH), which activates melanocortin 4 receptors (MC4R) in the paraventricular nucleus to reduce intake and increase energy expenditure. NPY/AgRP neurons release neuropeptide Y, which drives feeding, and agouti-related peptide, which blocks MC4R, and they also inhibit POMC neurons directly."},{"type":"table","title":"Leptin and ghrelin compared","cols":["Feature","Leptin","Ghrelin"],"rows":[["Source","Adipocytes, in proportion to fat mass","Gastric fundus endocrine cells"],["Pattern","Reflects long-term energy stores; falls with fasting","Rises before meals, falls after eating"],["Receptor and signal","Leptin receptor; JAK2-STAT3","Growth hormone secretagogue receptor (GHSR1a), after acylation"],["Arcuate effect","Stimulates POMC; inhibits NPY/AgRP","Stimulates NPY/AgRP"],["Net effect","Satiety, higher energy expenditure","Hunger; also stimulates GH release"]],"caption":"Leptin is a slow signal of stored fat; ghrelin is a fast signal tied to meals. Gut hormones such as PYY and GLP-1 add short-term satiety."},{"type":"flow","title":"Leptin signaling to the arcuate nucleus","steps":["Fat mass rises; adipocytes secrete more leptin","Leptin reaches arcuate leptin receptors","POMC neurons activated; NPY/AgRP neurons inhibited","Alpha-MSH activates MC4R, AgRP no longer blocks it","Food intake falls; energy expenditure rises"],"caption":"A fall in leptin during fasting runs the loop in reverse, increasing hunger and suppressing the reproductive axis."},{"type":"h","text":"Leptin resistance and common obesity"},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital leptin deficiency is rare and causes extreme early obesity, hyperphagia, and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism; MC4R mutations are the most common single-gene cause of obesity. Common obesity is different: leptin is high in proportion to fat mass, but appetite is not suppressed. This leptin resistance reflects reduced leptin transport into the brain and impaired receptor signaling, with negative regulators such as SOCS3 and PTP1B dampening the JAK2-STAT3 pathway. Because the low-leptin signal is strong but the high-leptin signal is weak, the system defends against weight loss far better than against weight gain. Prader-Willi syndrome shows the ghrelin side: ghrelin levels are markedly elevated, accompanying insatiable hunger."},{"type":"h","text":"Visceral fat and insulin resistance"},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral adipose tissue is more lipolytically active than subcutaneous fat and drains into the portal vein, so it delivers free fatty acids directly to the liver. Excess fatty acids raise hepatic gluconeogenesis and VLDL output, and in liver and muscle their intermediates, especially diacylglycerol, activate protein kinase C isoforms that phosphorylate IRS-1 on serine, blocking its tyrosine phosphorylation by the insulin receptor. Enlarged visceral adipocytes and infiltrating macrophages secrete TNF-alpha and IL-6, which activate JNK and IKK-beta and impair IRS signaling further. Visceral fat also secretes less adiponectin, which normally improves insulin sensitivity by activating AMP-activated protein kinase. The result is reduced GLUT4 translocation in muscle and failure to suppress hepatic glucose output, the core defects of type 2 diabetes and the metabolic syndrome."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An obese patient has a very high serum leptin. Would giving more leptin reduce appetite?","explain":"Usually not. Common obesity reflects leptin resistance, so the arcuate signaling pathway is already saturated or blunted; extra leptin adds little. Only congenital leptin deficiency responds strongly.","check":"The paragraph on leptin resistance."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Phosphorylation by PKA and dephosphorylation by insulin-activated phosphatases set the direction of liver metabolism, and DKA is what happens when that switch is stuck on release; the acid-base findings then follow from bicarbonate buffering, the anion gap, and respiratory compensation. Iodine, selenium, vitamin D, and magnesium show that endocrine pathways depend on nutrients at specific enzymatic steps, so a deficiency can be predicted from where the nutrient sits: iodine limits synthesis, selenium limits activation, vitamin D limits the mineral supply for bone, and magnesium limits both PTH release and its signaling."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and immunology complete the picture. Rickets and osteomalacia are mineralization failures, unlike osteoporosis, and granulomatous disease shows unregulated vitamin D activation. Visceral adiposity links the hypothalamic control of appetite to the cytokine-driven insulin resistance of type 2 diabetes. Limits: serum magnesium poorly reflects body stores, early calcitriol can be misleadingly normal, and reference ranges vary by laboratory, so each value is read alongside PTH, phosphate, and the clinical setting."}],"summary":["Insulin, through PI3K-Akt and dephosphorylation, stores fuel: GLUT4 uptake, glycogen synthesis, glycolysis, lipogenesis, and protein synthesis.","Glucagon, through cAMP and PKA in the liver, releases fuel: glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis via lower fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, and ketogenesis via lower malonyl-CoA.","In DKA, unrestrained lipolysis and an open CPT-1 gate overload liver mitochondria with acetyl-CoA, which becomes acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate.","Ketoacids consume bicarbonate and leave unmeasured anions, giving an anion gap metabolic acidosis with Kussmaul respiration and a total body potassium deficit.","Iodine is built into T4 and T3; deficiency lowers T4, raises TSH, and causes goiter, while selenium-containing deiodinases convert T4 to T3.","Vitamin D is activated in skin, liver, and kidney; renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is the regulated step, stimulated by PTH and low phosphate.","Vitamin D deficiency causes secondary hyperparathyroidism, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, and unmineralized osteoid: rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults.","Severe hypomagnesemia suppresses PTH secretion and blunts its action, so hypocalcemia persists until magnesium is restored.","Leptin from fat activates POMC and inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons to cause satiety; ghrelin from the stomach stimulates NPY/AgRP to cause hunger.","Common obesity shows high leptin with leptin resistance, and visceral fat drives insulin resistance through portal fatty acids, inflammatory cytokines, and low adiponectin."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolic Effects of Insulin and Glucagon; The Feed/Fast Cycle"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Diabetes Mellitus; Obesity"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation of energy balance and fuel metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases, vitamin D"}]},"EN-8":{"unit":"EN-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01","title":"Hormone Synthesis and Degradation","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows five hormone families from raw material to inactivation: thyroid hormone built on thyroglobulin, adrenal steroids built from cholesterol, catecholamines built from tyrosine, insulin cut from a single-chain precursor, and the posterior pituitary peptides carried down axons. It closes with 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, an enzyme that decides which steroid reaches a receptor. Board questions name a blocked enzyme, a missing transporter, or an unusual lab pattern and ask what accumulates, what falls, and how the patient presents. If you know where each step happens and which enzyme controls it, you can predict those answers instead of memorizing them.","prerequisites":["Tyrosine metabolism and the role of tetrahydrobiopterin, pyridoxal phosphate, and S-adenosylmethionine","Cholesterol sources (LDL receptor uptake, de novo synthesis, stored esters) and the steroid nucleus","The secretory pathway: rough ER, Golgi, secretory granules, and regulated exocytosis","Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A signaling","Gross and microscopic structure of the thyroid, adrenal, pancreatic islets, and pituitary (EN-2, EN-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine organ function and control (EN-5)","how":"TSH, ACTH, angiotensin II, plasma osmolality, and glucose act on the specific synthetic steps described here."},{"to":"Hormone action, transport, and feedback (EN-6)","how":"Binding proteins, receptor specificity, and negative feedback explain why a synthetic block raises ACTH or TSH and enlarges the gland."},{"to":"Endocrine genetics (EN-9)","how":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia, Pendred syndrome, apparent mineralocorticoid excess, and familial central diabetes insipidus are single-gene defects in these pathways."},{"to":"Diseases of endocrine hyperfunction and neoplasms (EN-12-A, EN-12-G)","how":"Pheochromocytoma, insulinoma, and ectopic ACTH are diagnosed by the metabolites and processing products taught here."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"Mineralocorticoid receptor activation in the collecting duct links steroid metabolism to sodium balance, potassium, and acid-base status."}],"terms":[{"term":"Organification","def":"Covalent attachment of oxidized iodine to tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin by thyroid peroxidase, forming MIT and DIT."},{"term":"Coupling","def":"The thyroid peroxidase reaction that joins two iodotyrosines within thyroglobulin to form T4 (DIT plus DIT) or T3 (MIT plus DIT)."},{"term":"Deiodinase","def":"A selenoprotein enzyme that removes iodine from thyroid hormones, activating T4 to T3 or inactivating T4 and T3."},{"term":"StAR protein","def":"Steroidogenic acute regulatory protein, which moves cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane; the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis."},{"term":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","def":"Inherited deficiency of a cortisol-synthesizing enzyme in which low cortisol raises ACTH, enlarging the adrenals and diverting precursors into open pathways."},{"term":"PNMT","def":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, the cytosolic enzyme that methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine and is induced by high local cortisol."},{"term":"Metanephrines","def":"Metanephrine and normetanephrine, the COMT products of epinephrine and norepinephrine, measured to detect pheochromocytoma."},{"term":"C-peptide","def":"The connecting segment removed from proinsulin and secreted in equimolar amounts with insulin, used as a marker of endogenous insulin secretion."},{"term":"Neurophysin","def":"A carrier protein cleaved from the same precursor as ADH (neurophysin II) or oxytocin (neurophysin I) that helps package the hormone into granules."},{"term":"11-beta-HSD2","def":"11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which oxidizes cortisol to inactive cortisone in aldosterone-target cells."},{"term":"Apparent mineralocorticoid excess","def":"Mineralocorticoid-type hypertension and hypokalemia with low renin and low aldosterone caused by cortisol acting on the mineralocorticoid receptor."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C01","obj":"EN-8.1","title":"Thyroid hormone synthesis, release, activation, and breakdown","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each thyroid follicle is a sphere of follicular cells surrounding colloid, which is mostly thyroglobulin, a large glycoprotein made on the rough ER and secreted into the lumen. The basolateral surface faces capillaries and the apical surface faces colloid. Thyroid hormone is assembled on thyroglobulin at the apical cell and colloid interface and stored in the colloid, which holds enough hormone for about 2 to 3 months."},{"type":"flow","title":"Thyroid hormone synthesis and release","steps":["NIS brings 2 Na+ and 1 iodide in basolaterally","Pendrin moves iodide across the apical membrane into colloid","DUOX2 generates hydrogen peroxide at the apical surface","TPO oxidizes iodide and iodinates thyroglobulin tyrosines (MIT, DIT)","TPO couples DIT plus DIT to T4, MIT plus DIT to T3","TSH stimulates endocytosis of iodinated colloid","Lysosomal proteases free T4 and T3 into the blood","Iodotyrosine deiodinase recycles iodide from leftover MIT and DIT"],"caption":"Uptake is basolateral, iodination and coupling are apical, and release requires reuptake and digestion of the stored thyroglobulin."},{"type":"p","text":"The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) is secondary active transport: it uses the Na+ gradient created by the Na+/K+-ATPase to concentrate iodide about 30 times above plasma. Perchlorate and thiocyanate compete for NIS. Pendrin, an apical anion exchanger, then moves iodide into the colloid; its loss causes Pendred syndrome, a goiter with sensorineural deafness."},{"type":"p","text":"Thyroid peroxidase (TPO), an apical membrane enzyme, uses hydrogen peroxide to oxidize iodide to a reactive form and attach it to tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin, producing monoiodotyrosine (MIT) and diiodotyrosine (DIT). The same enzyme then couples iodotyrosines still bound to thyroglobulin. Thionamide drugs illustrate the mechanism by inhibiting TPO, and antibodies against TPO are the serologic marker of Hashimoto thyroiditis. A large iodide load transiently inhibits organification (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect) until NIS is downregulated and synthesis escapes."},{"type":"p","text":"TSH, acting through Gs and cAMP, increases every step: NIS, TPO, and thyroglobulin expression, hydrogen peroxide generation, colloid endocytosis, and follicular cell growth. About 90% of secreted hormone is T4. Iodotyrosine deiodinase, a separate enzyme from the thyroid hormone deiodinases, recycles iodide from MIT and DIT (adult requirement about 150 micrograms per day)."},{"type":"table","title":"Deiodinases: activation and inactivation","cols":["Enzyme","Main reaction","Main sites","Significance"],"rows":[["Type 1 (D1)","Outer ring (5 prime) deiodination, T4 to T3; also clears reverse T3","Liver, kidney, thyroid","Supplies plasma T3; inhibited by propylthiouracil"],["Type 2 (D2)","Outer ring deiodination, T4 to T3 inside the cell","Pituitary, brain, brown fat, muscle","Sets intracellular T3; pituitary D2 lets T4 suppress TSH"],["Type 3 (D3)","Inner ring deiodination, T4 to reverse T3 and T3 to T2","Placenta, brain, fetal tissues","Inactivates hormone; protects the fetus from excess"]],"caption":"About 80% of circulating T3 comes from peripheral conversion of T4, not from the thyroid."},{"type":"p","text":"Because T4 and T3 travel mostly bound to thyroxine-binding globulin, transthyretin, and albumin, they are degraded slowly: the half-life of T4 is about 6 to 7 days and of T3 about 1 day. Degradation proceeds by deiodination and by hepatic glucuronidation and sulfation with biliary excretion. When any synthetic step fails (iodine deficiency, TPO or thyroglobulin defects), T4 falls, TSH rises, and the gland enlarges into a goiter."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has a goiter, high TSH, and high radioiodine uptake at 2 hours, but most of the trapped radioiodine leaves the gland when perchlorate is given. Which step is defective?","explain":"Organification (TPO, its hydrogen peroxide supply, or pendrin). NIS works, so iodide is trapped, but it stays as free iodide that perchlorate displaces instead of being bound to thyroglobulin.","check":"The separation between basolateral uptake and apical organification."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C02","obj":"EN-8.2","title":"Adrenal steroid synthesis and enzyme blocks","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"All adrenal steroids come from cholesterol, taken up mainly as LDL and stored as cholesterol esters. ACTH, through the melanocortin 2 receptor, Gs, and cAMP, frees cholesterol from esters and activates StAR protein, which moves it to the inner mitochondrial membrane. This transfer is rate limiting. There, side-chain cleavage enzyme (CYP11A1, desmolase) converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, the parent of every steroid. Later steps alternate between smooth ER and mitochondria. Over days, ACTH also increases enzyme expression and adrenal size."},{"type":"table","title":"Adrenal zones: enzymes and products","cols":["Zone","Pathway after pregnenolone","Key enzyme feature","Main regulator"],"rows":[["Glomerulosa","Progesterone, 11-deoxycorticosterone (21-hydroxylase), corticosterone, aldosterone","Lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase; has aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2)","Angiotensin II and plasma K+"],["Fasciculata","17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-alpha-hydroxylase), 11-deoxycortisol (21-hydroxylase), cortisol (11-beta-hydroxylase, CYP11B1)","Full cortisol pathway","ACTH"],["Reticularis","17-hydroxypregnenolone to DHEA, then DHEA sulfate and androstenedione","High 17,20-lyase activity of CYP17A1","ACTH"]],"caption":"3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase converts pregnenolone and its derivatives to the progesterone series. CYP17A1 carries both 17-alpha-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase activity."},{"type":"p","text":"Cortisol is the only adrenal product that feeds back on CRH and ACTH. Any block on the cortisol pathway therefore raises ACTH, which drives more cholesterol in and enlarges both glands (congenital adrenal hyperplasia). Precursors accumulate above the block and spill into any branch that remains open. To predict the result, ask three questions: Is cortisol made? Is a salt-retaining steroid (aldosterone or 11-deoxycorticosterone) made in excess or lacking? Can precursors reach 17,20-lyase to form androgens?"},{"type":"cycle","title":"Why an enzyme block amplifies itself","steps":["Enzyme block lowers cortisol","Less negative feedback on hypothalamus and pituitary","CRH and ACTH rise","ACTH drives StAR, enzyme expression, and adrenal growth","More precursor builds up above the block and spills into open branches","Cortisol stays low, so ACTH stays high"],"caption":"Rising ACTH cannot overcome the block; it only increases precursor flow toward the pathways still open."},{"type":"table","title":"Enzyme blocks: what accumulates and what falls","cols":["Deficient step","Accumulates","Deficient","Blood pressure, K+, renin","Sexual development"],"rows":[["StAR or side-chain cleavage","Cholesterol esters in lipid-laden cells (lipoid hyperplasia, typical of StAR loss)","All adrenal and gonadal steroids","Salt wasting, high K+, high renin","46,XY undervirilized; 46,XX normal at birth"],["21-hydroxylase (about 90% of CAH)","17-hydroxyprogesterone, progesterone, androgens","Cortisol; aldosterone in the salt-wasting form","Salt-wasting form: low BP, high K+, high renin","46,XX virilized; early pubic hair and growth in both sexes"],["11-beta-hydroxylase","11-deoxycortisol, 11-deoxycorticosterone, androgens","Cortisol, corticosterone, aldosterone","High BP, low K+, low renin","46,XX virilized; early virilization in both sexes"],["17-alpha-hydroxylase","11-deoxycorticosterone, corticosterone, progesterone","Cortisol, androgens, estrogens","High BP, low K+, low renin, low aldosterone","46,XY undervirilized; 46,XX absent puberty and primary amenorrhea"]],"caption":"ACTH is high in all four. 11-deoxycorticosterone is a mineralocorticoid; its excess suppresses renin and therefore aldosterone."},{"type":"p","text":"In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, both cortisol and aldosterone synthesis are impaired, so precursors flow to androgens; in the salt-wasting form, newborns can present in the first weeks with hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and shock, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the newborn screening marker. In 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency, adrenal crisis is uncommon because corticosterone, produced in excess, has weak glucocorticoid activity. Hepatic reduction and conjugation then inactivate steroids for urinary excretion; cortisol, about 90% bound to cortisol-binding globulin and albumin, has a half-life of about 60 to 90 minutes, and aldosterone, weakly bound, about 20 minutes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: locating the block","text":"A 4-year-old girl has pubic hair, an enlarged clitoris, blood pressure 128/84 mm Hg, and K+ 3.1 mEq/L (reference about 3.5 to 5.0). Step 1: androgens are high, so precursors reach 17,20-lyase and 17-alpha-hydroxylase works, which excludes 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency. Step 2: hypertension with hypokalemia means excess salt-retaining steroid, which rules out 21-hydroxylase deficiency, where mineralocorticoid is lacking. Step 3: a block after 21-hydroxylation but before cortisol makes 11-deoxycortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone pile up; the latter acts on the mineralocorticoid receptor and suppresses renin. The answer is 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, confirmed by high 11-deoxycortisol with low renin and low aldosterone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C03","obj":"EN-8.3","title":"Catecholamine synthesis, degradation, and pheochromocytoma testing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Catecholamines are made from tyrosine in adrenal medullary chromaffin cells, sympathetic postganglionic neurons, and dopaminergic neurons. Each enzyme needs a cofactor, and the first step sets the overall rate. Tyrosine hydroxylase is inhibited by its catecholamine products, which compete with its cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin, and it is activated by phosphorylation when the cell is stimulated, so output tracks demand."},{"type":"flow","title":"Catecholamine synthesis","steps":["Tyrosine hydroxylase: tyrosine to L-DOPA (rate limiting, tetrahydrobiopterin)","DOPA decarboxylase: L-DOPA to dopamine (pyridoxal phosphate)","VMAT pumps dopamine into the vesicle","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase: dopamine to norepinephrine (ascorbate, copper)","Norepinephrine leaks to the cytosol in chromaffin cells","PNMT: norepinephrine to epinephrine (S-adenosylmethionine)","Epinephrine is repackaged into granules for release"],"caption":"Dopamine neurons stop after step 2, noradrenergic neurons after step 4, and adrenal chromaffin cells continue to epinephrine."},{"type":"p","text":"PNMT is expressed in the adrenal medulla because medullary capillaries receive blood that has just passed through the cortex, carrying cortisol at concentrations far above systemic levels, and cortisol induces PNMT. The medulla therefore secretes about 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine. Extra-adrenal paragangliomas lack this cortisol exposure and usually secrete norepinephrine, so a tumor producing mainly epinephrine is likely adrenal."},{"type":"p","text":"Catecholamine action at the synapse ends mainly by reuptake. Degradation uses two enzymes. Monoamine oxidase (MAO), on the outer mitochondrial membrane, deaminates amines inside neurons. Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) transfers a methyl group from S-adenosylmethionine, mainly in liver, kidney, and chromaffin cells, converting epinephrine to metanephrine and norepinephrine to normetanephrine. Whichever enzyme acts first, the other follows, and hepatic metabolism ends in vanillylmandelic acid (VMA). Dopamine ends as homovanillic acid (HVA)."},{"type":"table","title":"Metabolites and what they report","cols":["Metabolite","Parent","Formed by","Clinical use"],"rows":[["Metanephrine","Epinephrine","COMT","Plasma free or urine fractionated: most sensitive pheochromocytoma test"],["Normetanephrine","Norepinephrine","COMT","Same; dominant in paragangliomas"],["Vanillylmandelic acid","Epinephrine and norepinephrine","COMT and MAO","Less sensitive for pheochromocytoma; raised in neuroblastoma"],["Homovanillic acid","Dopamine","COMT and MAO","Raised in neuroblastoma"]],"caption":"Metanephrines measure continuous intratumoral metabolism; parent catecholamines and VMA reflect episodic release."},{"type":"p","text":"Pheochromocytoma cells contain membrane-bound COMT. Catecholamines leak continuously from tumor granules into the cytosol and are methylated to metanephrines, which diffuse into the blood whether or not the tumor is secreting at that moment. Paroxysms of headache, sweating, palpitations, and hypertension reflect episodic catecholamine bursts, but metanephrines stay elevated between them, which is why plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines are the most sensitive tests and a normal result makes the diagnosis unlikely. Drugs that block reuptake or MAO raise catecholamine metabolites and can cause false positives."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor secretes large amounts of norepinephrine but almost no epinephrine, and it lies beside the aortic bifurcation. Why does it make norepinephrine only?","explain":"It is an extra-adrenal paraganglioma. Without cortisol-rich portal blood from the adrenal cortex, PNMT is not induced, so synthesis stops at norepinephrine and normetanephrine dominates the metabolite profile.","check":"The paragraph on PNMT induction by cortisol."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C04","obj":"EN-8.4","title":"Insulin processing and C-peptide in hypoglycemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Insulin is made as a single chain and finished as two. The single chain lets the molecule fold and form its disulfide bonds correctly; cutting out the connecting segment afterward leaves the A and B chains linked by those bonds."},{"type":"table","title":"From gene product to secreted insulin","cols":["Stage","Location","Event"],"rows":[["Preproinsulin","Rough ER","Signal peptide directs the chain into the ER lumen and is removed"],["Proinsulin","ER lumen","Chain folds (B chain, C-peptide, A chain); two interchain and one intrachain disulfide bonds form"],["Sorting","Golgi to immature granule","Proinsulin is packaged into secretory granules"],["Cleavage","Maturing granule","Prohormone convertases (PC1/3, PC2) cut at paired basic residues; carboxypeptidase E trims the ends"],["Storage","Mature granule","Insulin (21-residue A chain, 30-residue B chain) crystallizes with zinc; free C-peptide stays in the granule"],["Secretion","Plasma membrane","Glucose metabolism raises ATP, which closes ATP-sensitive K+ channels; depolarization opens Ca2+ channels; granules undergo exocytosis"]],"caption":"Each granule releases insulin and C-peptide in equal molar amounts, plus a small amount of unprocessed proinsulin."},{"type":"p","text":"Insulin and C-peptide leave the beta cell together but are cleared differently. Insulin enters the portal vein, and the liver removes about half of it on first pass; the remainder has a plasma half-life of about 5 minutes. C-peptide is barely extracted by the liver, is cleared mainly by the kidney, and has a half-life of about 30 minutes. Peripheral C-peptide therefore exceeds insulin on a molar basis and is a steadier measure of beta cell secretion. Renal failure raises C-peptide, which matters when interpreting it."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypoglycemia is evaluated during a documented episode, ideally meeting the Whipple triad: symptoms, a low plasma glucose (in nondiabetic adults usually below about 55 mg/dL, 3.0 mmol/L), and relief when glucose is raised. Normally, falling glucose turns insulin secretion almost off. Measurable insulin at that moment is inappropriate, and C-peptide shows where it came from. Pharmaceutical insulin contains no C-peptide, and injected insulin suppresses the patient's own beta cells, so C-peptide falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Insulin, C-peptide, and proinsulin during hypoglycemia","cols":["Cause","Insulin","C-peptide","Proinsulin","Deciding clue"],"rows":[["Insulinoma","High","High","High","Insulin secretagogue screen negative"],["Sulfonylurea or meglitinide ingestion","High","High","High","Insulin secretagogue screen positive"],["Injected insulin","High","Low","Low","Insulin to C-peptide molar ratio well above 1"],["Non-insulin causes (fasting with liver failure, adrenal insufficiency, sepsis)","Low","Low","Low","Insulin appropriately suppressed"]],"caption":"High C-peptide means the patient's own beta cells are secreting; low C-peptide with high insulin means the insulin came from outside."},{"type":"p","text":"Sulfonylureas and meglitinides bind the SUR1 subunit of the beta cell ATP-sensitive K+ channel and close it regardless of glucose, so they reproduce the biochemistry of an insulinoma exactly; only a drug screen separates them. Insulinoma cells often release granules before processing is complete, which is why proinsulin is a sensitive marker of tumor secretion."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the hypoglycemia panel","text":"A 34-year-old nurse is found confused with plasma glucose 38 mg/dL (2.1 mmol/L). Insulin is markedly elevated, C-peptide is below the detection limit, proinsulin is low, and the sulfonylurea screen is negative. Step 1: insulin should be nearly zero at this glucose, so hypoglycemia is insulin mediated. Step 2: endogenous secretion would raise C-peptide in equal molar amounts, but C-peptide is suppressed, so the beta cells are switched off. Step 3: the only source of insulin without C-peptide is administered insulin. An insulinoma or sulfonylurea would have raised both. Exogenous insulin is the cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C05","obj":"EN-8.5","title":"ADH and oxytocin: hypothalamic synthesis and posterior pituitary release","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"ADH (arginine vasopressin) and oxytocin are nine-amino-acid peptides that differ at only two positions. Both are made by large magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. Each nucleus contains both cell types, but the supraoptic nucleus is the main source of ADH and the paraventricular nucleus of oxytocin. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is neural tissue made of their axon terminals and pituicytes; it stores and releases these hormones but does not synthesize them."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cell body to bloodstream","steps":["Precursor made on rough ER of magnocellular neurons","ADH precursor: signal peptide, ADH, neurophysin II, copeptin","Oxytocin precursor: signal peptide, oxytocin, neurophysin I","Golgi packages precursor into neurosecretory granules","Fast axonal transport down the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract","Precursor cleaved within granules during transport","Granules stored in terminals and swellings (Herring bodies)","Action potential opens Ca2+ channels; exocytosis into capillaries"],"caption":"Hormone, its neurophysin, and (for ADH) copeptin are released together in equal molar amounts."},{"type":"p","text":"Neurophysin binds its hormone inside the granule and helps package it. Dominant mutations in the AVP gene, many in the neurophysin II region, produce a misfolded precursor that is retained in the ER and gradually kills magnocellular neurons, causing familial central diabetes insipidus that appears in childhood rather than at birth. Copeptin is stable in plasma, so it can serve as a surrogate measure of ADH release."},{"type":"p","text":"Release follows electrical activity of the cell bodies. ADH neurons fire in response to rising plasma osmolality, detected by osmoreceptors in circumventricular organs, and to a substantial fall in blood volume or pressure signaled by baroreceptors. Oxytocin neurons respond to suckling and to stretch of the cervix during labor. A separate population of smaller parvocellular paraventricular neurons sends ADH to the median eminence, where it enters the portal circulation and potentiates CRH stimulation of ACTH."},{"type":"p","text":"Circulating ADH is not protein bound and has a plasma half-life of only minutes, cleared mainly by liver and kidney. In pregnancy, a placental aminopeptidase (vasopressinase) degrades ADH and oxytocin, which can unmask or cause transient diabetes insipidus. The site of a lesion decides its effect. Removing only the posterior lobe usually causes transient diabetes insipidus, because cut axons in the stalk and median eminence soon release ADH again. A hypothalamic or high stalk lesion that destroys most magnocellular cell bodies causes permanent central diabetes insipidus. Stalk section can produce a triphasic sequence: diabetes insipidus, then unregulated ADH leak from degenerating terminals with water retention, then permanent diabetes insipidus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-08-01-C06","obj":"EN-8.6","title":"11-beta-HSD2, receptor protection, and licorice","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The mineralocorticoid receptor binds cortisol and aldosterone with about equal affinity, yet free cortisol in plasma is at least 100 times more concentrated than free aldosterone. If nothing intervened, cortisol would occupy nearly every receptor and aldosterone could not regulate sodium. Specificity is supplied by an enzyme, not by the receptor."},{"type":"p","text":"11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (11-beta-HSD2) is an NAD+-dependent enzyme located beside the receptor in aldosterone-target epithelia: principal cells of the late distal nephron and collecting duct, colon, and salivary and sweat ducts. It oxidizes cortisol to cortisone, which does not bind the receptor, so aldosterone becomes the effective ligand. The placenta uses the same enzyme to shield the fetus from maternal cortisol. The type 1 isoform, in liver and adipose tissue, runs mainly in the opposite direction, regenerating cortisol from cortisone."},{"type":"flow","title":"Licorice and apparent mineralocorticoid excess","steps":["Glycyrrhetinic acid from licorice inhibits renal 11-beta-HSD2","Cortisol is no longer converted to cortisone","Cortisol occupies and activates mineralocorticoid receptors","ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase activity rise; Na+ is retained","Lumen-negative potential drives K+ and H+ secretion","Volume expands; renin and aldosterone fall","Hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis"],"caption":"Plasma cortisol stays normal; the defect is local loss of cortisol inactivation in the kidney."},{"type":"p","text":"The resulting pattern follows from receptor physiology. Activated receptors increase ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase in principal cells, so Na+ reabsorption makes the lumen more negative and drives K+ secretion through ROMK channels, while intercalated cells secrete more H+. Edema is usually absent because, once volume expands, higher renal perfusion pressure and natriuretic peptides increase Na+ excretion (mineralocorticoid escape), leaving hypertension and hypokalemia as the main signs."},{"type":"p","text":"Glycyrrhizin in licorice root (Glycyrrhiza glabra) is hydrolyzed by gut bacteria to glycyrrhetinic acid, the active inhibitor. Deglycyrrhizinated licorice lacks this compound and does not have the effect. The same mechanism appears in two other settings. Inherited loss of 11-beta-HSD2 (apparent mineralocorticoid excess, autosomal recessive) causes severe low-renin hypertension in children. Very high cortisol, as with ectopic ACTH secretion, saturates the enzyme, which is why that form of Cushing syndrome commonly causes hypokalemia. Urinary cortisol to cortisone metabolite ratios rise in all three because conversion is reduced."},{"type":"table","title":"Low-renin hypertension with hypokalemia: telling the causes apart","cols":["Cause","Renin","Aldosterone","Cortisol","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Primary hyperaldosteronism","Low","High","Normal","Autonomous aldosterone secretion"],["Licorice or inherited 11-beta-HSD2 loss","Low","Low","Normal","Cortisol reaches the mineralocorticoid receptor"],["Ectopic ACTH","Low","Low or normal","Very high","Cortisol saturates 11-beta-HSD2"],["11-beta- or 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","Low","Low","Low","11-deoxycorticosterone excess (C02)"],["Liddle syndrome","Low","Low","Normal","Gain-of-function ENaC, independent of the receptor"]],"caption":"A high aldosterone separates primary hyperaldosteronism from every mimic; among the low-aldosterone causes, cortisol level and history separate the rest."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with hypertension and K+ 2.9 mEq/L has low renin. What aldosterone level would you expect if the cause is daily licorice tea rather than an adrenal adenoma?","explain":"Low. Cortisol is activating the mineralocorticoid receptor, so volume expansion suppresses renin and, through low angiotensin II, aldosterone. An adenoma would show high aldosterone despite low renin.","check":"The comparison table of low-renin hypertension."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and anatomy explain where each hormone is finished. Thyroid hormone is completed at the apical membrane because TPO and its hydrogen peroxide source sit there; epinephrine is made in the adrenal medulla because the cortex drains cortisol into it and cortisol induces PNMT; insulin is finished in the granule because the convertases work there; and ADH is finished in transit because it is cleaved inside granules moving down axons. Cofactors connect the pathways to nutrition: iodine for thyroid hormone, tetrahydrobiopterin, pyridoxal phosphate, ascorbate, and copper for catecholamines, and selenium for the deiodinases."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and genetics apply one rule across the chapter: a block raises what lies upstream and lowers what lies downstream, and feedback amplifies the upstream rise. This explains goiter in dyshormonogenesis, adrenal hyperplasia with predictable precursor patterns in CAH, and the metabolite patterns used to recognize pheochromocytoma and insulin-mediated hypoglycemia. The rule has limits. Laboratory patterns overlap (sulfonylurea and insulinoma), metabolic clearance changes measured values (C-peptide in renal failure), and drugs and diet can mimic disease (licorice), so each pattern is interpreted with the clinical setting."}],"summary":["NIS traps iodide basolaterally using the Na+ gradient; TPO, using hydrogen peroxide, organifies iodide onto thyroglobulin and couples DIT plus DIT to T4 and MIT plus DIT to T3.","TSH stimulates every thyroid step, including colloid endocytosis and lysosomal release; the thyroid secretes mostly T4, and peripheral D1 and D2 make most circulating and intracellular T3, while D3 inactivates.","StAR transfer of cholesterol into mitochondria is rate limiting; side-chain cleavage makes pregnenolone, and each adrenal zone's product depends on which enzymes it expresses.","A cortisol pathway block raises ACTH and pushes precursors into open branches: 21-hydroxylase deficiency gives salt wasting with virilization, 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency gives hypertension with virilization, and 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency gives hypertension with absent sex steroids.","Tyrosine hydroxylase is rate limiting for catecholamines; cortisol arriving from the cortex induces PNMT, so epinephrine is made mainly in the adrenal medulla.","COMT makes metanephrines and, with MAO, VMA; continuous intratumoral metabolism makes plasma free or urine fractionated metanephrines the most sensitive test for pheochromocytoma.","Proinsulin is cleaved in the granule to insulin and C-peptide, released in equal molar amounts; high insulin with high C-peptide means endogenous secretion (insulinoma or secretagogue), and high insulin with low C-peptide means injected insulin.","ADH and oxytocin are made in supraoptic and paraventricular neurons with their neurophysins, cleaved during axonal transport, and stored and released from the posterior pituitary, which does not synthesize them.","11-beta-HSD2 converts cortisol to cortisone to protect the mineralocorticoid receptor; licorice inhibits it, producing hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with low renin and low aldosterone."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Thyroid Metabolic Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Adrenocortical Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Insulin, Glucagon, and Diabetes Mellitus"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine sections on the hypothalamus and pituitary, adrenal gland, and thyroid gland"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Amino acid conversion to specialized products (catecholamines) and steroid hormone synthesis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System"}]},"EN-9":{"unit":"EN-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01","title":"Endocrine Genetics","subject":"Endocrine System (ENDO)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the genetic basis of endocrine disease: inherited tumor syndromes, recessive enzyme defects, the genetics of the diabetes types, driver mutations in thyroid cancer, disorders of sex development, and hypogonadism. It closes with how steroid and thyroid hormones act as ligand-activated transcription factors. Board questions usually give a pedigree, a karyotype with hormone levels, or a tumor with its mutation and ask for the gene, the mode of inheritance, or the mechanism. If you can say whether a mutation removes or adds function, which cells express the gene, and how feedback shifts the trophic hormones, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance patterns and pedigree analysis","Tumor suppressor genes, proto-oncogenes, and the two-hit hypothesis","Adrenal steroid synthesis pathways and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and male sexual differentiation","Receptor tyrosine kinase and MAP kinase signaling"],"connections":[{"to":"Adrenal physiology and pathology","how":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia shows how an enzyme block and loss of cortisol feedback reshape the whole steroid pathway."},{"to":"Thyroid and parathyroid pathology","how":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia are the tumors through which MEN syndromes present."},{"to":"Endocrine pancreas and diabetes","how":"The genetics of type 1, type 2, and monogenic diabetes reflect autoimmunity, polygenic beta cell dysfunction, and single-gene glucose sensing defects."},{"to":"Reproductive system and embryology","how":"Androgen insensitivity, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, and Kallmann syndrome map onto the separate hormonal signals that build the male reproductive tract."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"HLA class II alleles that present islet peptides set much of the inherited risk for type 1 diabetes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tumor suppressor gene","def":"A gene whose product restrains cell proliferation, so both alleles must be inactivated in a cell before a tumor forms."},{"term":"Proto-oncogene","def":"A normal growth-promoting gene that becomes an oncogene when a gain-of-function mutation makes its product overactive."},{"term":"Loss of heterozygosity","def":"Loss of the remaining normal allele in a cell that inherited one mutant allele, often by deletion or mitotic recombination."},{"term":"Penetrance","def":"The proportion of people carrying a pathogenic genotype who show the phenotype."},{"term":"Compound heterozygote","def":"A person with two different pathogenic alleles at the same locus, one on each chromosome."},{"term":"Gene conversion","def":"Nonreciprocal transfer of sequence from one DNA segment to a similar one, such as from a pseudogene to its functional gene."},{"term":"Polygenic trait","def":"A trait shaped by many genetic variants of small effect acting together with environmental factors."},{"term":"Concordance","def":"The proportion of twin pairs in which both twins have a trait when at least one has it."},{"term":"Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Low gonadal hormone output from gonadal failure, with high LH and FSH because negative feedback is lost."},{"term":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Low gonadal hormone output because the hypothalamus or pituitary fails to supply GnRH, LH, or FSH."},{"term":"Hormone response element","def":"A short DNA sequence near a target gene to which a hormone-receptor complex binds to change transcription."},{"term":"Nuclear receptor","def":"A ligand-activated transcription factor with a zinc finger DNA-binding domain and a ligand-binding domain."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C01","obj":"EN-9.1","title":"MEN1 and MEN2: tumor suppressor loss versus proto-oncogene activation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) syndromes cause tumors or hyperplasia in several endocrine glands of the same person, usually at a younger age and in more than one site than sporadic tumors. The two main forms arise from opposite kinds of mutation, and that difference explains how each one behaves inside a cell."},{"type":"p","text":"MEN1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MEN1 gene on chromosome 11q13, which encodes menin. Menin is a nuclear scaffold protein that, among other actions, helps a histone methyltransferase complex keep the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors p27 and p18 switched on, so it restrains proliferation in parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and anterior pituitary cells. One normal copy is enough to do this. A person with MEN1 inherits one mutant allele in every cell, but a gland cell becomes neoplastic only after a second, somatic hit removes the normal allele, most often by loss of heterozygosity. Because every cell already carries the first hit, second hits occur often, which is why tumors are multiple, multiglandular, and early."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two-hit tumorigenesis in MEN1","steps":["First hit: mutant MEN1 allele inherited in every cell","Remaining normal allele still makes enough menin","Second hit in one gland cell: loss of heterozygosity","No menin; p27 and p18 expression falls","Clonal proliferation forms a tumor"],"caption":"Recessive at the level of the cell, dominant at the level of the family."},{"type":"p","text":"MEN2 is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene on chromosome 10q11, which encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase for glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF) family ligands. RET is expressed in neural crest derivatives, including thyroid parafollicular C cells and adrenal chromaffin cells. In MEN2A, most mutations replace a cysteine in the extracellular domain (codon 634 is the most common), leaving an unpaired cysteine that forms disulfide-linked dimers without ligand. In MEN2B, a kinase domain mutation (usually M918T) activates the receptor and alters its substrates. A single mutant allele signals continuously, so no second hit is needed; the result is C cell hyperplasia progressing to medullary carcinoma in nearly all carriers. By contrast, loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease, because enteric neural crest cells need RET signaling to colonize the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Both syndromes are autosomal dominant: each child of a carrier has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutation, and penetrance is high. MEN1 therefore shows that dominant inheritance in a pedigree does not require a dominant mechanism in the cell; it requires only that one inherited allele makes disease very likely."},{"type":"table","title":"MEN1 compared with MEN2A and MEN2B","cols":["Feature","MEN1","MEN2A","MEN2B"],"rows":[["Gene and type","MEN1, tumor suppressor (menin)","RET, proto-oncogene","RET, proto-oncogene"],["Mutation","Loss of function; second hit needed","Gain of function, usually extracellular cysteine","Gain of function, kinase domain (M918T)"],["Main lesions","Parathyroid hyperplasia, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (gastrinoma, insulinoma), pituitary adenoma (often prolactinoma)","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia","Medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus"],["Inheritance","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal dominant"]],"caption":"MEN2B lacks hyperparathyroidism, and its medullary carcinoma tends to appear earliest and behave most aggressively."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Tumor DNA from a parathyroid adenoma in a woman with MEN1 is compared with her blood DNA. What difference at 11q13 would you expect?","explain":"Blood DNA is heterozygous, with one mutant and one normal MEN1 allele. Tumor DNA has lost the normal allele (loss of heterozygosity), so only the mutant allele remains, showing the second hit.","check":"The two-hit flow and the paragraph on menin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C02","obj":"EN-9.2","title":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: autosomal recessive enzyme deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a group of inherited defects in the enzymes of adrenal cortisol synthesis. Like most enzyme deficiencies, it is autosomal recessive, because a heterozygote's single working allele makes enough enzyme to keep the pathway running. Disease appears only when both alleles are defective, either as the same mutation (homozygous) or, more often, as two different mutations (compound heterozygous); the milder allele largely sets the severity."},{"type":"p","text":"About 90 to 95% of CAH is 21-hydroxylase deficiency, caused by mutations in CYP21A2 on chromosome 6p21.3 within the HLA class III region. CYP21A2 lies next to a highly similar but nonfunctional pseudogene, CYP21A1P. Misalignment during meiosis allows unequal crossing over, which deletes or fuses the genes, and gene conversion, which copies disabling pseudogene sequence into the active gene. These two events account for most pathogenic alleles, which explains why the disease is relatively common: the classic form occurs in about 1 in 15,000 births, and the milder nonclassic form is considerably more frequent."},{"type":"p","text":"21-hydroxylase converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol. When it fails, cortisol falls, loss of cortisol feedback raises ACTH, and chronic ACTH stimulation enlarges the adrenal cortex. Precursors that cannot be hydroxylated at carbon 21 accumulate and are diverted through 17,20-lyase into adrenal androgens. Aldosterone synthesis also fails in the salt-wasting form, which makes up about three-quarters of classic cases."},{"type":"flow","title":"From CYP21A2 deficiency to the CAH phenotype","steps":["Both CYP21A2 alleles defective","Cortisol (and often aldosterone) synthesis falls","Lost feedback raises CRH and ACTH","ACTH drives adrenal cortical hyperplasia","17-hydroxyprogesterone accumulates","Shunting to DHEA, androstenedione, and testosterone"],"caption":"A raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the biochemical marker of 21-hydroxylase deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from this shunt. A 46,XX fetus is exposed to adrenal androgens during the period of external genital development, so she is born with ambiguous genitalia (clitoral enlargement, labioscrotal fusion) but with normal ovaries and uterus, because she has no testes and no anti-Mullerian hormone. A 46,XY infant looks normal at birth; in the salt-wasting form, aldosterone deficiency causes hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, volume depletion, and hypotension in the first weeks of life. Later, androgen excess causes early pubic hair, rapid growth, and advanced bone age in both sexes."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the main CAH enzyme defects","cols":["Enzyme (gene)","Mineralocorticoid effect","Blood pressure","Androgens","46,XX genitalia"],"rows":[["21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2)","Aldosterone low in salt-wasting form","Low or normal","High","Virilized"],["11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1)","11-deoxycorticosterone accumulates","High","High","Virilized"],["17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17A1)","11-deoxycorticosterone accumulates","High","Low","Female; 46,XY undervirilized"]],"caption":"All three are autosomal recessive and all raise ACTH; the substrate that accumulates determines blood pressure and virilization."},{"type":"example","title":"Recurrence and carrier risks in a CAH family","text":"Two unaffected parents have a child with classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency, so each parent must be a carrier. For each pregnancy the risks are 1/4 affected, 1/2 carrier, and 1/4 noncarrier. An unaffected older sibling cannot be the affected genotype, so only three outcomes remain: carrier (2 of 3) or noncarrier (1 of 3). Her risk of being a carrier is therefore 2/3, not 1/2. If she later has children with an unrelated partner whose carrier risk is about 1/60, the chance their child is affected is 2/3 x 1/60 x 1/4, or about 1 in 360."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C03","obj":"EN-9.3","title":"Genetics of type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes, and MODY","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The three forms of diabetes differ not only in mechanism but in genetic architecture: type 1 is an autoimmune disease with a strong HLA component, type 2 is polygenic and heavily shaped by environment, and maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is a single-gene disorder of the beta cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Type 1 diabetes results from T cell-mediated destruction of beta cells. The HLA region on chromosome 6p21 accounts for roughly half of its inherited risk. The highest-risk haplotypes are HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4, inherited together with the class II alleles DQ2 and DQ8, and DR3/DR4 heterozygotes carry the greatest risk. These class II molecules have peptide-binding grooves that present islet antigens, such as insulin peptides, in a way that favors activation of autoreactive CD4 T cells. Other genes add smaller effects, including the insulin gene promoter region, PTPN22, and CTLA4, which influence thymic selection and T cell activation. Because an environmental trigger and stochastic immune events are also needed, monozygotic twin concordance is only about 30 to 50%."},{"type":"p","text":"Type 2 diabetes has no HLA association. It arises from insulin resistance plus beta cell failure, and genome-wide studies have found many common variants of small effect, most of which impair insulin secretion rather than insulin action; TCF7L2 has the largest single effect among common variants. The combined genetic contribution is nevertheless larger than in type 1, so monozygotic twin concordance is higher, roughly 70% or more, with obesity and inactivity unmasking the susceptibility."},{"type":"p","text":"MODY is autosomal dominant: onset usually before age 25, in nonobese people, with no islet autoantibodies and no tendency to ketosis, and with diabetes in successive generations. Glucokinase (GCK) MODY comes from heterozygous loss of function of glucokinase, the beta cell hexokinase whose low affinity for glucose makes it the glucose sensor that couples blood glucose to insulin release. With half the enzyme, the beta cell needs a higher glucose concentration to secrete insulin, so the set point rises. The result is mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia, about 5.5 to 8 mmol/L (100 to 145 mg/dL), that rarely progresses or causes microvascular complications. HNF1A MODY affects a transcription factor needed for beta cell gene expression; it causes progressive secretory failure, and because HNF1A also regulates proximal tubule glucose transport, glycosuria can appear at lower blood glucose than normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Genetic comparison of the main diabetes types","cols":["Feature","Type 1","Type 2","MODY"],"rows":[["Genetic architecture","Polygenic, dominated by HLA class II","Polygenic, many small-effect variants","Monogenic"],["Key loci","HLA-DR3/DQ2, HLA-DR4/DQ8","TCF7L2 and many others; no HLA link","GCK, HNF1A (also HNF4A and others)"],["Inheritance","Complex","Complex, strong family clustering","Autosomal dominant"],["Monozygotic twin concordance","About 30 to 50%","About 70% or more","Near 100% if the mutation is shared"],["Autoantibodies","Present","Absent","Absent"]],"caption":"Higher twin concordance in type 2 diabetes reflects a larger total genetic contribution, not a single major gene."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has homozygous inactivating GCK mutations. Would the phenotype resemble GCK MODY?","explain":"No. With no glucokinase, the beta cell cannot sense glucose and secretes little insulin, so the child has permanent neonatal diabetes. Heterozygotes keep half the enzyme and only shift the set point. Conversely, activating GCK mutations lower the set point and cause hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia.","check":"The glucokinase paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C04","obj":"EN-9.4","title":"Driver mutations in thyroid carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three of the four major thyroid carcinomas arise from follicular epithelial cells (papillary, follicular, and anaplastic), and one arises from parafollicular C cells (medullary). The driver mutations follow this cellular origin. Most follicular cell cancers are driven by a single activating lesion in the receptor tyrosine kinase, RAS, RAF, MEK, ERK (MAP kinase) pathway or in the PI3K-AKT pathway, while medullary carcinoma is driven by RET, the receptor that C cells normally express."},{"type":"flow","title":"MAP kinase pathway activation in papillary carcinoma","steps":["RET/PTC fusion: RET kinase signals without ligand","RAS-GTP activates RAF","BRAF V600E: RAF active without upstream signal","MEK phosphorylates ERK","ERK enters nucleus; growth genes transcribed","Follicular cell proliferation and dedifferentiation"],"caption":"Activation at any one level is enough, so these drivers rarely occur together in one tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Papillary carcinoma, the most common type, is usually driven by the BRAF V600E point mutation (in roughly 40 to 60% of tumors) or by a RET/PTC rearrangement. In V600E, glutamate replaces valine in the kinase activation loop, mimicking the phosphorylation that normally switches RAF on, so the kinase is constitutively active. RET/PTC forms when a rearrangement, usually an inversion within chromosome 10, fuses the RET kinase domain to the promoter and dimerization region of a gene that is always expressed in follicular cells, so a kinase normally silent in these cells is produced and active without ligand. Ionizing radiation causes the double-strand breaks that create such fusions, which is why RET/PTC is especially frequent in papillary cancers of children exposed to radiation. BRAF V600E tumors tend to lose expression of genes for iodide handling, including the sodium-iodide symporter."},{"type":"p","text":"Follicular carcinoma is associated with activating RAS point mutations (NRAS most often) and with the PAX8-PPARG fusion from a t(2;3)(q13;p25) translocation, each found in roughly a third to a half of tumors and largely mutually exclusive. PAX8 is a thyroid transcription factor, and the fusion protein is thought to interfere with normal PPAR-gamma function. Follicular carcinoma is defined by capsular or vascular invasion rather than nuclear features, and it spreads mainly through the blood, whereas papillary carcinoma spreads through lymphatics."},{"type":"p","text":"Medullary carcinoma carries RET point mutations: germline in MEN2 and familial medullary carcinoma (about a quarter of cases), and somatic in many sporadic tumors. The tumor secretes calcitonin, and amyloid in its stroma is derived from calcitonin. Anaplastic carcinoma usually arises from a preexisting papillary or follicular tumor that already carries BRAF or RAS and then loses TP53, removing a checkpoint and allowing rapid dedifferentiated growth."},{"type":"table","title":"Thyroid carcinoma types and characteristic mutations","cols":["Type","Cell of origin","Characteristic mutations","Pathway"],"rows":[["Papillary","Follicular","BRAF V600E; RET/PTC rearrangement (radiation)","MAP kinase"],["Follicular","Follicular","RAS point mutation; PAX8-PPARG t(2;3)","MAP kinase and PI3K-AKT (RAS); transcriptional (fusion)"],["Medullary","Parafollicular C cell","RET point mutation, germline or somatic","Receptor tyrosine kinase"],["Anaplastic","Follicular","TP53 loss added to BRAF or RAS","Loss of checkpoint control"]],"caption":"RET appears twice: rearranged in papillary carcinoma, point-mutated in medullary carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C05","obj":"EN-9.5","title":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Male sexual differentiation in a 46,XY fetus depends on three separate signals from the testis. Anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) from Sertoli cells makes the Mullerian ducts regress, so no uterus, fallopian tubes, or upper vagina form. Testosterone from Leydig cells, acting on the androgen receptor, maintains the Wolffian ducts as epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles. Dihydrotestosterone (DHT), made from testosterone by type 2 5-alpha-reductase in genital tissue, binds the same receptor with higher affinity and masculinizes the external genitalia and prostate. A defect in the receptor or in the enzyme removes different combinations of these signals."},{"type":"p","text":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) is caused by mutations in the androgen receptor gene on Xq12, so it is X-linked recessive: a carrier mother passes it to half of her 46,XY children, and her 46,XX children are unaffected but may be carriers. In complete AIS, testes form and secrete AMH, so there is no uterus and only a short, blind-ending vagina derived from the urogenital sinus. Neither testosterone nor DHT can act, so Wolffian structures regress and the external genitalia are female. Because the hypothalamus and pituitary also cannot sense androgen, negative feedback fails and LH rises, driving testosterone to normal or high male levels. Aromatization of this testosterone to estradiol, unopposed by androgen action, produces breast development at puberty, while pubic and axillary hair, which depend on androgen, are sparse. Testes may lie in the labia, inguinal canals, or abdomen, and carry an increased risk of germ cell neoplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency is caused by autosomal recessive mutations in SRD5A2. The phenotype appears only in 46,XY individuals, since 46,XX homozygotes have no testosterone-dependent development to disrupt. Testosterone and the androgen receptor are normal, so Wolffian ducts develop and the testes descend partly or fully, and AMH removes the Mullerian ducts. DHT is low, so the external genitalia at birth are ambiguous or predominantly female, with a small phallus, bifid scrotum, perineal hypospadias, and a blind vaginal pouch. At puberty, testosterone rises to adult male levels and, acting directly on the receptor (with help from the type 1 isoenzyme), produces phallic growth, muscle mass, and a deeper voice. Prostate growth, facial and body hair, and male-pattern hair loss, which depend more heavily on DHT, remain limited. The testosterone to DHT ratio is high."},{"type":"table","title":"AIS compared with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (both 46,XY with testes)","cols":["Feature","Complete AIS","5-alpha-reductase deficiency"],"rows":[["Gene and inheritance","Androgen receptor, X-linked recessive","SRD5A2, autosomal recessive"],["Uterus and upper vagina","Absent (AMH acts)","Absent (AMH acts)"],["Wolffian structures","Absent or rudimentary","Present"],["External genitalia at birth","Female","Ambiguous or mostly female"],["Puberty","Breasts develop; sparse pubic hair; no virilization","Virilization; no breast development"],["Testosterone","Normal to high","Normal male"],["LH","High","Normal or mildly high"],["DHT","Normal or high","Low; testosterone to DHT ratio high"]],"caption":"The receptor defect blocks every androgen action and feedback; the enzyme defect removes only DHT."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the hormones in a 46,XY girl","text":"A 16-year-old with female external genitalia and primary amenorrhea has a 46,XY karyotype, no uterus on ultrasound, and gonads in the inguinal canals. Testosterone is in the high male range and LH is high. A high LH with high testosterone means the hypothalamus is not sensing androgen, which points to a receptor defect rather than an enzyme defect. Absent uterus means AMH was secreted, so the gonads are testes. Breast development with sparse pubic hair fits estrogen action without androgen action. The diagnosis is complete AIS. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, LH would be near normal because testosterone feedback works, and puberty would bring virilization, not breasts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C06","obj":"EN-9.6","title":"Primary versus secondary hypogonadism: Klinefelter and Kallmann syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypogonadism means inadequate production of gametes, sex steroids, or both. The level of the defect is located by the gonadotropins. When the gonad fails (primary or hypergonadotropic hypogonadism), the low testosterone and low inhibin B remove negative feedback, and LH and FSH rise. When the hypothalamus or pituitary fails (secondary or hypogonadotropic hypogonadism), LH and FSH are low or inappropriately normal despite low sex steroids."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Male hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal feedback","steps":["Pulsatile GnRH from the hypothalamus","LH and FSH from anterior pituitary gonadotropes","LH: Leydig testosterone; FSH: Sertoli inhibin B and spermatogenesis","Testosterone and inhibin B suppress GnRH, LH, and FSH"],"caption":"Klinefelter breaks the loop at the testis; Kallmann breaks it at GnRH."},{"type":"p","text":"Klinefelter syndrome, 47,XXY, is the most common genetic cause of male hypogonadism, at about 1 in 600 male births. It results from meiotic nondisjunction in either parent; some patients are 46,XY/47,XXY mosaics with milder features. The extra X is largely inactivated and forms a Barr body, but genes that escape inactivation are overexpressed. Seminiferous tubules undergo progressive hyalinization and fibrosis, so the testes are small and firm, sperm are usually absent, and loss of Sertoli cell inhibin B raises FSH. Leydig cell function also declines, so testosterone is low or low-normal and LH rises. The relatively high estradiol to testosterone ratio, partly from increased aromatization, causes gynecomastia. Tall stature with long legs reflects delayed epiphyseal closure from low androgen and an extra copy of SHOX, a growth gene that escapes X inactivation. Risks of breast cancer and mediastinal germ cell tumors are increased."},{"type":"p","text":"Kallmann syndrome combines hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia or hyposmia. GnRH neurons arise in the olfactory placode and migrate along olfactory and terminal nerve axons through the cribriform plate into the forebrain and hypothalamus. When olfactory axons fail to reach the forebrain, the olfactory bulbs are hypoplastic or absent and the GnRH neurons are stranded outside the brain. Without GnRH, LH and FSH stay low, the gonads remain unstimulated, and puberty is delayed or absent with eunuchoid proportions. The pituitary itself is normal. In boys, external genitalia are male because placental hCG drives early differentiation, but micropenis and cryptorchidism are common because late fetal growth depends on fetal LH. Genes include X-linked ANOS1 (encoding the adhesion protein anosmin-1) and autosomal dominant FGFR1, among others."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing primary and secondary hypogonadism","cols":["Feature","Klinefelter (primary)","Kallmann (secondary)"],"rows":[["Defect","Testis: tubule fibrosis, Leydig dysfunction","Hypothalamus: no GnRH neurons in place"],["Genetics","47,XXY from nondisjunction; not usually inherited","ANOS1 (X-linked), FGFR1 (autosomal dominant), others"],["LH and FSH","High","Low or inappropriately normal"],["Testosterone","Low or low-normal","Low"],["Testes","Small and firm","Small, prepubertal, soft"],["Distinguishing features","Gynecomastia, tall stature, Barr body","Anosmia, absent olfactory bulbs"]],"caption":"Both cause infertility and delayed or incomplete virilization; gonadotropins and smell separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-ENDO-09-01-C07","obj":"EN-9.7","title":"Intracellular receptors: steroid and thyroid hormones as transcription factors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, androgens, estrogens, progesterone), calcitriol, and thyroid hormone are lipophilic. They travel bound to plasma proteins such as thyroxine-binding globulin, corticosteroid-binding globulin, sex hormone-binding globulin, and albumin, and only the small free fraction enters cells. Steroids cross the membrane by diffusion; thyroid hormone needs membrane transporters such as MCT8. Inside, they bind members of the nuclear receptor superfamily, which share a central zinc finger DNA-binding domain, a C-terminal ligand-binding domain, and activation regions that recruit coregulator proteins."},{"type":"p","text":"Steroid receptors such as the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and androgen receptors sit in the cytoplasm without ligand, held in an inactive complex with heat shock proteins such as hsp90 (estrogen and progesterone receptors are mainly nuclear). Hormone binding releases the chaperones, and the receptors form homodimers, enter the nucleus, and bind hormone response elements, usually two six-base half-sites arranged as an inverted repeat. The bound receptor recruits coactivators with histone acetyltransferase activity, opening chromatin so RNA polymerase II can transcribe the target gene."},{"type":"flow","title":"Steroid hormone action through a cytoplasmic receptor","steps":["Free steroid diffuses into the cell","Binds receptor; heat shock proteins released","Receptor dimerizes and enters the nucleus","Dimer binds hormone response element","Coactivators acetylate histones; transcription starts","mRNA translated; new protein changes cell function"],"caption":"Each step after binding takes time, so the response develops over hours."},{"type":"p","text":"The thyroid hormone receptor works differently. It is already in the nucleus and bound to DNA without hormone, as a heterodimer with the retinoid X receptor (RXR), at thyroid response elements made of two direct repeats spaced by four bases. Unliganded, the heterodimer binds corepressors that recruit histone deacetylases and silences target genes. T3, which binds the receptor with about ten times the affinity of T4 and comes mostly from deiodination of T4 outside the thyroid, displaces the corepressors and recruits coactivators. The vitamin D receptor, retinoic acid receptor, and PPARs also partner with RXR."},{"type":"p","text":"Because these hormones act by changing which proteins a cell makes, their effects begin after a latent period of about 30 minutes to hours and last long after hormone levels fall, aided by long plasma half-lives (about a week for T4). Peptide hormones and catecholamines act on surface receptors that generate second messengers and modify existing proteins within seconds to minutes. Some steroids also have rapid nongenomic membrane actions, but the classic effects are genomic. Receptor mutations produce hormone resistance with high hormone levels: androgen insensitivity (C05), thyroid hormone resistance from THRB mutations with high T4 and T3 and unsuppressed TSH, and hereditary vitamin D-resistant rickets."},{"type":"table","title":"Surface versus intracellular hormone receptors","cols":["Feature","Peptide hormones and catecholamines","Steroid and thyroid hormones"],"rows":[["Solubility and transport","Water soluble; mostly free in plasma","Lipophilic; mostly protein bound"],["Receptor location","Plasma membrane","Cytoplasm (most steroids) or nucleus (thyroid, estrogen)"],["Mechanism","Second messengers modify existing proteins","Receptor acts as transcription factor at a response element"],["Onset and duration","Seconds to minutes; brief","About 30 minutes to hours or days; prolonged"],["Plasma half-life","Minutes","About an hour (cortisol) to about a week (T4)"]],"caption":"Synthesis of new mRNA and protein explains the slower, longer actions of intracellular receptor hormones."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has a mutant thyroid hormone receptor that cannot bind T3 but still binds DNA and RXR. Would target genes be expressed normally, below normal, or not at all?","explain":"Below normal or silenced. The mutant receptor stays on the response element with its corepressors, actively repressing the gene, and can also block the normal receptor from the same element, so the defect behaves as dominant negative.","check":"The thyroid receptor paragraph on corepressors and RXR."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Genetics in this chapter is read through tissue biology. Whether a mutation removes or adds function predicts inheritance at the cellular level (two hits for MEN1, one for RET), while expression pattern predicts which organs suffer: RET in neural crest derivatives such as C cells, chromaffin cells, and enteric neurons; menin in endocrine cells that depend on it to restrain growth; the androgen receptor everywhere testosterone and DHT act. Anatomy and embryology explain the rest, from Wolffian and Mullerian development in disorders of sex development to the olfactory migration route of GnRH neurons in Kallmann syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Feedback physiology turns these genotypes into laboratory patterns: a block below the feedback signal raises the trophic hormone (ACTH in CAH, LH in AIS and Klinefelter syndrome), while a hypothalamic defect leaves it low. Immunology explains the HLA link in type 1 diabetes, and receptor biochemistry explains why steroid and thyroid hormone actions are slow, prolonged, and abolished by receptor mutations. Population figures such as mutation frequencies and twin concordances vary among series, so they are best remembered as approximate patterns."}],"summary":["MEN1 is a loss-of-function tumor suppressor (menin) that needs a second somatic hit; MEN2 is a gain-of-function RET receptor tyrosine kinase that needs only one allele. Both are autosomal dominant.","MEN1 affects parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and pituitary; MEN2A adds medullary carcinoma and pheochromocytoma to parathyroid hyperplasia; MEN2B adds mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus without hyperparathyroidism.","CAH is autosomal recessive because one working allele makes enough enzyme; 90 to 95% is CYP21A2 deficiency, with most mutations from pseudogene recombination in the HLA region.","21-hydroxylase deficiency lowers cortisol and aldosterone, raises ACTH and 17-hydroxyprogesterone, and shunts precursors to androgens, virilizing 46,XX fetuses.","Type 1 diabetes is linked to HLA-DR3/DQ2 and DR4/DQ8; type 2 is polygenic with no HLA link and higher monozygotic concordance; MODY is autosomal dominant, and GCK MODY raises the beta cell glucose set point.","Papillary carcinoma: BRAF V600E or RET/PTC; follicular: RAS or PAX8-PPARG; medullary: RET point mutation; anaplastic adds TP53 loss.","Complete AIS is an X-linked androgen receptor defect: 46,XY, testes, no uterus, female genitalia, breasts, high testosterone and LH.","5-alpha-reductase deficiency lowers DHT with normal testosterone and receptor: ambiguous genitalia at birth, Wolffian structures present, virilization at puberty.","Primary hypogonadism (Klinefelter 47,XXY) has high LH and FSH; secondary hypogonadism (Kallmann, failed GnRH neuron migration with anosmia) has low LH and FSH.","Steroid and thyroid hormones bind nuclear receptors that act as transcription factors at response elements; the thyroid receptor pairs with RXR, and gene transcription explains their slower onset."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System: thyroid neoplasms, multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes, diabetes mellitus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia: tumor suppressor genes and oncogenes"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Introduction to Endocrinology; Adrenocortical Hormones; Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation and nuclear receptors; sexual differentiation"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital system, development of the genital ducts and external genitalia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex; Disorders of the Male Reproductive Tract"}]}});
